window.TOL_SEARCH = {"v":1,"pages":[{"u":"/about.html","t":"About Spread Love & Acceptance and Its Creator","h":"About Spread Love & Acceptance and its creator","d":"Meet Christian, the creator of Spread Love & Acceptance and The Objective Ledger: fifteen years auditing systems, and a free, growing suite of kindness tools.","s":["Ask Tide about Christian","The Auditor","IThe Mandate","IIA Different Language","IIIThe Constant Audit","IVThe Invisible Cost","VRelational Static","VIDropping the Mask","VIIThe Mission","Which part of this sounds like you?","The Nine Inputs"],"x":"Tide is a small helper that knows only about Christian: their story, how they think and how the program grew out of their life. It works on your device, and what you type isn’t sent anywhere. Live & Growing · Updated Often This site changes fast. New pages, tools and improvements go live often, sometimes several in a day. Nothing sits half-finished behind a coming-soon page. If it's live, it's live, and the Content Roadmap shows exactly what is still being written. Check back whenever you like, and you'll find something that wasn't here last time. And if any of this helps you, please pass it on. Share it, send it to someone who needs it, talk about it, and help spread love and acceptance. About Christian — founder, Spread Love and Acceptance A Career in Audit · A Lifetime in the Other One I spent my career as a government auditor and audit manager in tax enforcement, internal controls and compliance. Under the job titles it was always the same work: walk into something complicated, where nobody could tell you straight what had happened, and find out what was actually true. Finding the truth was only the opening move. After that came the part I found genuinely interesting. First the risks: the places where this was always going to go wrong eventually, whether or not it had yet. Then the failures: where it already had, and what it had cost while nobody was looking. Then the question that actually matters, which is never who let this happen but which control broke, or which control was never there to begin with. Those are two different findings with two different fixes, and mixing them up is how organizations keep solving the wrong problem. A control that broke was working once, so something changed around it. A control that never existed means everyone had been relying on goodwill, memory and the same person quietly catching it every time, and eventually that person was busy. A control is just an agreement. How a thing gets done, who owns it, and how anyone would notice if it stopped. And the cause, most of the time, was not fraud and it was not laziness. It was complacency. Something had worked for years, so people stopped checking it. A step got skipped once because the day was busy, nothing went wrong, so it got skipped again. The person who used to catch it moved on and nobody replaced them, because nobody had realized they were the control. \"We have alway","f":0},{"u":"/architecture/architecture.html","t":"The site’s architecture, in detail","h":"Architecture Review","d":"The full technical picture of how The Objective Ledger is put together: pages, tools, privacy and how it all connects.","s":["1. Why This Architecture?","2. Carrier Wave vs. Payload","3. Maintenance Margin","4. The Dual-Dial Dashboard","5. Implementation Order (What to Do First)","6. Risks & Enhancements"],"x":"System Design How The Objective Ledger is structured and why we made these choices. The Objective Ledger is built on three core convictions: Visibility before change. You can't fix what you can't see. This system makes invisible work visible to both people at the same time. Structure over willpower. Good intentions tend to fall apart under stress. This program holds up because it's built into your routine: a regular rhythm, clear owners, and the same shared numbers each time. Nervous system first. Everything here accepts that it's hard to think clearly when you're overwhelmed. The Ladder of three states (calm and connected, revved up, shut down) is the foundation, because checking your state comes before any conversation. These three ideas are the first three of the Five Pillars : Pillar I, See the whole load ; Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person ; and Pillar III, Read your state first . The carrier wave idea below is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , and the maintenance margin keeps an eye on Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives , the slow pull of work drifting back to one person. Each pillar starts inside you, then shows up between you and the people you share life with. In radio, the \"carrier wave\" is the steady signal that carries the message. The \"payload\" is the message itself. In The Objective Ledger: Carrier Wave: Weekly check-in, shared document, same metrics every time Payload: The actual tasks, the actual feelings, the actual workload We spent a lot of effort on the carrier wave because the payload keeps changing. Your jobs change. Your health changes. The number of dishes changes. But if the carrier wave is solid — if both of you know when you're sitting down, what you're measuring, how you're measuring it — you can handle any payload. A bridge built to carry 100 cars will wear out if it carries 100 cars all the time. Every structure needs headroom. In relationships: Workload should be 60–75% of capacity, not 95% Check-ins should happen when things are okay, not in crisis Decisions should get made when energy is available, not at 11 PM after a hard day If your household is at 95% capacity all the time, this framework helps you see it. But naming the problem is only half the solution. You actually need to reduce the load. One dial won't work because a household has two dimensions: Dial 1: Workload Balance — Is the work divided relat","f":0},{"u":"/architecture/index.html","t":"How the site is built","h":"System Architecture","d":"How The Objective Ledger is designed and built, and why.","s":["What's Inside"],"x":"Design An open look at how The Objective Ledger is built, and why. We believe everyone in a relationship deserves to understand the system they're using. Not marketing language. Not hand-waving. The actual design decisions, the tradeoffs, and what could still be better. Explore the Architecture Why this architecture? (The design philosophy) Carrier Wave vs. Payload (how a steady routine carries what you talk about) Maintenance Margin (why breathing room matters) The Dual-Dial Dashboard (How to see your relationship clearly) Implementation order (What to do first) Risks & enhancements (What could go wrong, what we're planning)","f":0},{"u":"/ask.html","t":"Free Relationship Help Chat: Ask Professor Puddles","h":"Chat with Professor Puddles","d":"Describe what is going on in a relationship and get kind, practical next steps and words you could use. Free and private: what you type stays on your device.","s":[],"x":"What the Professor can help with Type a question in your own words, like “how do I use the Lemonade Stand?”, “what does my battery score mean?” or “my coworker keeps interrupting me”, and Professor Puddles, a small drop with a big brain, will answer from this program, with links to the right pages. Knows the whole program. Every tool, workpaper, chapter and game: what it’s for, how to use it step by step, and what your results mean. Type your Battery Meter answers or CALC-01 numbers and he’ll work out the score with you. Kind next steps for what’s going on. Describe a situation, with yourself or someone else, and he’ll suggest a few steps, words you could use and a short path on the site. You know your situation best. Background notes, clearly labelled. When the site doesn’t cover something, the Professor has notes on related topics, like negotiation, habits or co-parenting logistics. Answers from them always say so, and point you to a page to put it into practice. What you type stays on this device. Everything happens in your browser. Nothing you type or choose is sent anywhere, and the conversation is forgotten when you close the tab. Not a counselor. It’s general guidance and a friendly way around the site, not therapy or professional advice. Prefer to browse? The Contents and its site directory list every page, and Start here is a gentle first step.","f":0},{"u":"/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html","t":"Chapter I in depth: The Radio Frequency Paradigm","h":"The Radio Frequency Paradigm","d":"Chapter I in full: a radio-tuning picture of why two kind people fall out of step, how pace and tone create static, and the steps for getting back in tune.","s":["Where this fits: the Five Pillars","The radio whistle","Why being nearly in step is the noisy part","What sets the frequency","How stress changes your frequency","Nobody has to be wrong","Retuning, step by step","A worked example: the trash, replayed","Going back in: say it so it lands","Staying in tune: small and often","Where the nine fields meet in this chapter","How they connect","When it goes wrong, and when it goes right","Questions people often ask","Isn’t “we’re out of sync” just a way of avoiding the subject?","What if only one of us wants to retune?","What if we keep getting out of tune about the same thing?","Can I talk it through with someone first?","Limits and cautions"],"x":"Tuesday, 6:40pm “I said I’d do it.” “I know, I’m just asking when.” Four sentences later, nobody is talking about the trash anymore, and neither of you can point to the moment it turned. A picture, not a measurement. Nothing in this chapter records, analyzes or scores anyone’s real voice. It borrows an image from radio to explain why calm, kind people can sound harsh to each other when they’re under stress. “Paradigm” just means a way of looking at something. The Radio Frequency Paradigm is this book’s way of looking at everyday clashes: not as one person attacking the other, but as two signals that are slightly out of tune. The whole program rests on five pillars . Each one starts inside you and then shows up between you and other people. This chapter mostly teaches Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , with help from Pillar III, Read your state first . (Drawn from psychology and neurobiology, with a picture borrowed from radio.) In you: Know your own pace, your usual tone and what “soon” means to you. Notice when stress speeds you up or slows you down. Between you and others: When a clash starts, treat it as a tuning problem, not a moral failing. Retune together, then translate what you meant into words the other person can hear. Tune two radios to stations that are close but not the same, and you don’t get silence. You get a whistle. The two signals overlap just enough to interfere, and they make a third, uglier sound that neither radio was sending. Engineers call this a heterodyne , or a “beat”: the extra tone that appears when two signals are almost, but not quite, in step. This book borrows that picture for a familiar moment. Two people each say something reasonable. Each one’s tone is shaped by their own tiredness, hurry or stress. Together they get an argument that neither of them meant to start. The words weren’t the problem. The mismatch was. The pitch of that radio whistle depends on the gap between the two stations. When the gap shrinks to nothing, the whistle disappears. When two stations are very far apart, the two signals don’t mix into anything you can hear. People can work the same way. Strangers who barely interact rarely clash. People who share a home, a family or a job are close enough to overlap all day. That closeness is a good thing, and it also means small differences in pace or tone rub against each other again and again. A clas","f":1},{"u":"/book/chapter-1.html","t":"Why We Get Out of Tune: Communication at Home (Ch. I)","h":"Why we get out of tune: communication at home","d":"Chapter I: why kind people still end up resentful at home, and how small mismatches in pace, tone and what we expect add up, plus how to get back in tune.","s":["The tangle often isn’t about the words.","Nearly in step is the noisy part.","Three things knock you out of tune.","Stress changes your station.","Nobody has to be wrong.","Get back in tune before you carry on.","Go back in with a clear sentence.","Small daily check-ins keep you in tune.","Inside you, and between you","The fields behind this chapter","In real life","Common mix-ups"],"x":"Why two kind, reasonable people can end up in a tangle neither of them started, and how to get back in step. Five Pillars: This chapter mostly teaches Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , with help from Pillar III, Read your state first . What are the Five Pillars? Tuesday, 6:40pm “I said I’d do it.” “I know, I’m just asking when.” Four sentences later, nobody is talking about the trash anymore, and neither of you can point to the moment it turned. Two people can each say something reasonable and still clash, because the tone underneath doesn’t match. It’s like two radios slightly out of tune: together they make a crackle that neither one was making alone. Dig deeper: the radio static Radios on very different stations don’t whistle at each other. The whistle comes when they are close but not quite matched. People who know each other well can still clash. Research suggests we check our meaning less with people we’re close to, because we assume they already understand us. Dig deeper: why close can be noisy How fast you’re talking. The tone, when a simple update is heard as a complaint. And how urgent it feels: “when you get a chance” can mean tonight to one person and next week to the other. Dig deeper: pace, tone and urgency When you’re tense, you may talk faster and be more likely to hear a pause as a snub. When you’re tired and flat, your replies get slower and shorter. The Nervous System Ladder , a thirty-second self-check, helps you guess which one you’re in. It’s a best guess, not a reading. Dig deeper: how stress shifts your tune Two radios that both work fine will still crackle if they’re slightly off. Arguing about whose fault the static is won’t clear it. Retuning will. Dig deeper: taking the blame out Say it out loud: “I think we’re out of sync.” Check how tense you feel. Either of you can call a pause, and the person who paused names a time to come back. Gently slow down, with shorter turns and more pauses. Only then go back to the actual topic. Dig deeper: the four steps to retune When you return to the topic, say the fact, how you feel, and what you’d like. “The trash goes out early Wednesday morning. I get anxious when it’s late. Could you take it out tonight?” Dig deeper: going back in A short chat each day, like the 90-second daily check-in (WP-13) , can make tiny corrections before small things drift into a big argument. Habit research ","f":0},{"u":"/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html","t":"Chapter II in depth: The Epistemic Verdict Engine &…","h":"The Epistemic Verdict Engine & P(Solvency)","d":"Chapter II in full: a self-reflection tool that reads whether the household workload split looks like it can last, counting time, effort and invisible jobs.","s":["Where this fits: the Five Pillars","What the names mean","What the check is for","The three inputs","Workload balance","Ownership clarity","Autonomic saturation","The formula, in plain language","Why these weights?","A worked example","Reading your result: start with the shortfall","The wider view: the apex score","One reading is a snapshot. A month of readings is a trend.","Where the nine fields meet in this chapter","How they connect","When it goes wrong, and when it goes right","Questions people often ask","Our score is 0.69. Should we worry?","What if our hours are even but it still feels unfair?","Does our score mean we’re a good or bad match?","Can I ask someone to walk me through it?","What this number is not"],"x":"Any given week One of you is quietly doing eleven small things a day that the other has never had to think about. When the other one says it feels even, they mean it. From inside each person’s own week, it does. Before anything else: this chapter describes a rough self-reflection tool, not a scientific instrument. “P(Solvency)” is not a proven statistical model, and it never judges either person or the worth of the relationship. It scores one narrow thing: whether the way you currently share the household work looks like it can last, based on what you wrote down. Think of a small shop asking whether it can keep paying its bills, month after month, not whether it had a good Saturday. The answer is about the shop, not about whether the owners are good people. The whole program rests on five pillars . Each one starts inside you and then shows up between you and other people. This chapter mostly teaches Pillar I, See the whole load and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person , with help from Pillar III, Read your state first . (Borrowed from ledger accounting, systems thinking and nervous-system science.) In you: See your own share honestly, from what you wrote down rather than from memory. See the way you share work as a setup you can redesign, not a flaw in you. Between you and others: Put both people’s numbers on one page, and read the result as a note about the setup, never a verdict on a person. Clear owners leave less room for blame. The formal names sound grander than the tool is, so here they are in plain words: Epistemic means “to do with what we know.” The Epistemic Verdict Engine is simply a check that uses only what you both wrote down (your logs and your own self-ratings), not what either of you remembers in the heat of the moment. Despite the grand name, the “verdict” is about the setup, never about either of you. Solvency is an accounting word. Think of a small shop asking whether it can keep paying its bills, month after month. Here, a solvent setup is one where the work gets done without one person quietly running out of energy. P(Solvency) looks like a probability, but it isn’t one. The “P” is only a name carried over from the original manuscript. The number is a simple weighted sum, not a prediction. Sooner or later, everyone who shares a home asks some version of “Is this actually working?” Usually the answer comes from whatever happened mos","f":1},{"u":"/book/chapter-2.html","t":"Is the Chore Split Fair? Division of Labor (Ch. II)","h":"Is the split working? A fair look at chores at home","d":"Chapter II: a fair way to ask whether the division of labor at home is working for everyone, counting time, effort and the invisible jobs, never blame.","s":["Look at the month, not last night.","Three things go in.","Balance: how the hours split.","Ownership: does each job have a name?","Stress: how full each stress tank is.","One number comes out, from 0 to 1.","Start with the biggest shortfall.","It judges the setup, never a person.","Inside you, and between you","The fields behind this chapter","In real life","Common mix-ups"],"x":"One gentle question: can the way you share the work keep going, or is one of you quietly running on empty? Five Pillars: This chapter mostly teaches Pillar I, See the whole load and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person , with help from Pillar III, Read your state first . What are the Five Pillars? Any given week One of you is quietly doing eleven small things a day that the other has never had to think about. When the other one says it feels even, they mean it. From inside each person’s own week, it does. When people ask “Is this working?”, the answer usually comes from whatever happened most recently. This chapter asks a calmer question, using what you both wrote down. Dig deeper: what the check is for How evenly the hours are shared. Whether every regular job has a clear owner (the job list, WP-03 ). And how stressed each of you already is from everything else going on. Dig deeper: where each number comes from From your week-long logs, work out each person’s share of the hours. An even split scores 1. One person doing everything scores 0. It doesn’t matter which person is doing more. If you agreed an uneven split that suits you (say, one of you works longer hours outside the home), compare against that split instead. Three or more of you? Compare each person’s share with an even share (100 ÷ number of people), or with a split you agreed. The Lemonade Stand , a free page where each person lists their hours, does the arithmetic. Dig deeper: the balance score Each job gets two names: the owner, who does the job start to finish, and a backstop, who notices if it slipped and gently checks in (never a supervisor). It can be the same person, though a second name gives the job a gentle check. Count the jobs that have both names filled in, then divide by all the jobs on the list. Leave jobs nobody owns on the list. They are the ones that matter most. Dig deeper: the ownership score Each of you rates how full your own stress tank is with the Battery & Stress Meter ( WP-02 ). A higher score means more stress. Under 0.30 is low, 0.30 up to 0.60 is moderate, and 0.60 or more is high. If either of you is at 0.60 or more, treat that as the headline, whatever the average says. A setup that only works when you’re both well rested isn’t one that can last, so more stress lowers the result. Dig deeper: the stress score 0.7 or above: the setup is holding. From 0.4 up to 0.","f":0},{"u":"/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html","t":"Chapter III in depth","h":"Autonomic Saturation & the 7 Ocular Vectors","d":"Chapter III in full: two self-report tools for how much of a reaction is leftover stress, and which of seven angles each person is seeing a moment from.","s":["Where this fits: the Five Pillars","Autonomic Saturation","Leftover stress and this-moment stress","The three everyday states","The stress-tank score, step by step","How it feeds the Chapter II check","What a high reading asks of you","The 7 Ocular Vectors","Two true views of the same thing","Using both together","A worked example: the dentist, replayed","Where the nine fields meet in this chapter","How they connect","When it goes wrong, and when it goes right","Questions people often ask","Isn’t this just making excuses for bad behavior?","What if my score is high every day?","Can I guess which angle the other person is using?","Where can I talk this through?","Limits and cautions"],"x":"Thursday, 5:15pm They ask, perfectly pleasantly, whether you remembered to call the dentist. Your reaction is much bigger than the question deserved, and you can hear it happening while it happens. Both tools in this chapter are self-report tools. That means each person fills them in about themselves. Neither one measures anything automatically, and neither should ever be filled in by one person about the other. That would turn a reflection tool into a way of watching someone, which is exactly what this book is trying to avoid. The whole program rests on five pillars . Each one starts inside you and then shows up between you and other people. This chapter mostly teaches Pillar III, Read your state first , with help from Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive . (Borrowed from neurobiology and psychology.) In you: Know how full your stress tank is, and which angle you are seeing from, before you judge a moment. Between you and others: Choose the timing, pause and come back. Your state shapes how the other person’s words land, so say it out loud, and ask which angle they are seeing from. Two words, both plain once unpacked. Autonomic refers to the part of your nervous system that runs things without you deciding: heart rate, breathing (though you can also slow it on purpose), and how alert or braced your body feels. Saturation means how full something is, like a sponge that can’t take any more water. So Autonomic Saturation just means: how full your stress tank already is before this conversation even starts. When it’s nearly full, a small drop makes it overflow. That’s why a pleasant question about the dentist can get a reaction much bigger than the question deserved. A short version lives on the site as the Nervous System Ladder , a thirty-second self-check. The fuller version is the Battery & Stress Meter ( WP-02 ), which measures how full your stress tank is. A higher score means more stress in the tank. Any strong reaction is made of two parts: This-moment stress: the part caused by the thing in front of you right now. Leftover stress: the part you carried in from somewhere else, such as a poor night’s sleep, a hard day at work, a worry about money, or an argument that never got settled. From the outside, the two look the same. From the inside, they need very different things. “I’m upset about the dishes” is a problem about dishes. “I’m exhausted, and th","f":1},{"u":"/book/chapter-3.html","t":"Stress and Seeing Things Differently (Chapter III)","h":"Full tanks and different angles: stress and perspective","d":"Chapter III: how a full stress tank changes what we hear and say, why the same moment looks different from each side, and how to pause before you react.","s":["A full tank overflows.","Some stress is left over from earlier.","Check your stress tank in a minute.","Three everyday states.","There are seven angles on the same moment.","Both views can be true.","Say it instead of making each other guess.","A full tank means “later,” not “never.”","Inside you, and between you","The fields behind this chapter","In real life","Common mix-ups"],"x":"Why some reactions are bigger than what caused them, and why two people can see the same moment so differently. Five Pillars: This chapter mostly teaches Pillar III, Read your state first , with help from Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive . What are the Five Pillars? Thursday, 5:15pm They ask, perfectly pleasantly, whether you remembered to call the dentist. Your reaction is much bigger than the question deserved, and you can hear it happening while it happens. If you’re already tired, stretched or stressed from somewhere else, a small question can get a big reaction. That’s human. Anything decided in that moment is worth coming back to once you’ve both calmed down. Dig deeper: how full your tank is Ask yourself: how much of this feeling is about the thing in front of me, and how much did I bring in with me? “I’m upset about the dishes” and “I’m exhausted, and the dishes are what I noticed” are different problems. Some of what we bring in is much older: an understanding from growing up, like “whoever notices the mess has to clean it.” If a feeling seems a lot older than the moment, Where your lens came from helps you find where it started. Dig deeper: leftover stress The Battery & Stress Meter (WP-02) measures how full your stress tank is. A higher score means more stress in the tank. Score five things from 0 to 4: sleep, work elsewhere, any unsettled argument, how your body feels, and time pressure today. Add them up and divide by 20. Under 0.30 is low. From 0.30 up to 0.60 is moderate. 0.60 or more is high. Dig deeper: the stress-tank score Calm and connected. Revved up. Running on empty. Knowing which one you’re in tells you whether now is a good time to talk. The Nervous System Ladder , a thirty-second self-check, helps you make a best guess. These labels come from polyvagal theory, whose main claims are still debated by scientists. Here they are only everyday words, not a medical model. Dig deeper: the three states Tiredness, time pressure, history, what home was like growing up, your sense of fairness, how you communicate, and stress from outside. Many arguments are two people looking from different angles, each sure the other sees what they see. The Perspective Shifter , a free page built on these seven angles, helps you picture how the same moment might look from the other side, with questions to ask instead of guesses. Dig deeper: the seven a","f":0},{"u":"/book/chapter-4-in-depth.html","t":"Chapter IV in depth: Deontological Parity & Sensory Gating","h":"Deontological Parity & Sensory Gating","d":"Deontological parity and sensory gating: agreeing which kind of fair your household uses, and giving a comment time to land before you react to it.","s":["Where this fits: the Five Pillars","Deontological parity: the two kinds of fair","Judging by results","Judging by promises","The agreement: look to the owner","Choosing your kind of fair","Covering without resentment","A worked example: the trash, three weeks running","Sensory gating: giving a comment time to land","The three steps: notice, name, decide","Keep the two questions apart","A worked example: the flat thank-you","Where the nine fields meet in this chapter","How they connect","When it goes wrong, and when it goes right","Questions people often ask","What if we can’t agree on which kind of fair to use?","Isn’t it petty to hold someone to a small job?","What if “give me a second” turns into an hour?","Where can I get help finding the words?","Limits and cautions"],"x":"The same argument, twice a month One of you counts hours. The other counts who noticed the job needed doing. You’re both counting honestly, in two different currencies, and then arguing about the exchange rate. This chapter has two halves. The first is about agreeing what “fair” means in your home. The second is about the few seconds after a comment lands, before you reply. They belong together, because many arguments about chores mix up both: a question about a job, and a reaction to how it was said. The whole program rests on five pillars . Each one starts inside you and then shows up between you and other people. This chapter mostly teaches Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives , with help from Pillar III, Read your state first . (Borrowed from business, economics and neurobiology.) In you: Notice which kind of fair comes naturally to you, and notice the quiet habit of covering for others without saying so. When a comment stings, check your own state before you answer. Between you and others: Give each job one clear owner instead of blaming. Watch how unowned jobs slide toward one person, and keep things steady by mentioning help out loud and saying thank you. Many arguments about what fairness means come dressed up as an argument about chores. Philosophers have argued about this for centuries, and two of their big ideas are useful here: Fair by results. Philosophers call this consequentialist , from “consequences”: you judge by outcomes, by how things turned out. It asks: did the right things get done, by whoever had the time or energy? Done carefully, it counts the cost to whoever covered, too. Fair by promises. Philosophers call this deontological , from the Greek word for “duty.” It asks: did each person do the specific things they agreed to own, whatever kind of week it was? Both are reasonable. The friction starts when one person is judging the week by results (“It all got done, so we’re fine”) and the other is judging it by promises (“You said you’d handle the trash, and you didn’t, again”). Neither view is wrong. But a household needs to agree which one it is using, just as a contract needs to say which state’s laws apply. What it’s good at: it’s flexible. In a week when one person is sick or away, the other simply picks things up, and nobody has to negotiate. Where it goes wrong: it can hide a pattern.","f":1},{"u":"/book/chapter-4.html","t":"Equal vs Fair: Two Kinds of Fair at Home (Ch. IV)","h":"Two kinds of fair: equal vs fair at home","d":"Chapter IV: equal and fair are not the same thing. How couples and families find a fair chore split that fits their real lives, strengths and seasons.","s":["There are two kinds of fair.","Fair by results.","Fair by promises.","Once a job has an owner, look to the owner.","Covering is kind. Say it out loud.","Let a comment land before you react.","Notice, name, decide.","Keep the two questions apart.","Inside you, and between you","The fields behind this chapter","In real life","Common mix-ups","A note on safety"],"x":"Agree on what “fair” means before you argue about it, and give a comment a moment to land before you answer it. Five Pillars: This chapter mostly teaches Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives , with help from Pillar III, Read your state first . What are the Five Pillars? The same argument, twice a month One of you counts hours. The other counts who noticed the job needed doing. You’re both counting honestly, in two different currencies, and then arguing about the exchange rate. “Did everything get done?” and “Did each of us do what we said we’d do?” Both are reasonable. Friction starts when each of you is using a different one without realizing. Dig deeper: the two kinds of fair This kind judges by outcomes: did the right things get done, by whoever had the time or energy? It’s flexible and easy-going. Done carefully, it counts the cost to whoever covered, but done carelessly, it can hide who is always stepping in. Dig deeper: judging by results This kind asks whether each person did the jobs they agreed to own. It’s clear and steady, but it can feel rigid in a week when life gets in the way. Dig deeper: judging by promises The owner is the person who does the job, start to finish. A second name on the list is a backstop who notices if it slipped and gently checks in, never a supervisor. (It can be the same person.) If it’s your job, the question is whether you did it, not whether someone else quietly covered for you. That protects the person who keeps filling the gaps. Dig deeper: agreeing which kind you’ll use Helping out when the owner can’t is lovely. Just mention it, so the help is seen: “I took out the trash this week, since you were away.” Help that stays silent turns into unseen work. Dig deeper: covering without resentment When a remark hits harder than it should, say “Give me a second.” This chapter’s formal name borrows “sensory gating” from brain science. In the lab, it’s automatic and takes a fraction of a second. This book’s pause is different: a few seconds you choose to take on purpose. Let the spike pass, then decide whether it needs an answer now, later or not at all. Dig deeper: giving a comment time to land Notice it landed hard. Name it kindly: “That hit harder than you probably meant.” Then, once you’ve settled, decide how to reply. Say it so it lands (WP-09) helps with the words. Dig deeper:","f":0},{"u":"/book/chapter-5-in-depth.html","t":"Chapter V in depth: The Deficit Audit","h":"The Deficit Audit","d":"The Deficit Audit: a monthly look-back that catches the gaps weekly check-ins keep patching, and fixes the arrangement, not either person.","s":["Where this fits: the Five Pillars","Why a monthly look, on top of the weekly ones","Getting ready","Step one: gather the month’s notes","What a gap looks like at this scale","Step two: sort each repeat","A worked example month","Step three: close the month and keep count","How to talk about a pattern","What the look-back is for, and what it isn’t","Where the nine fields meet in this chapter","How they connect","When it goes wrong, and when it goes right","Questions people often ask","We missed a few weekly logs. Can we still do the look-back?","What if we disagree about which kind of gap something is?","How long should it take?","Can someone help us prepare?","Limits and cautions","Closing the books"],"x":"The third Tuesday in a row The trash again. You could have the same ten-minute back-and-forth about Tuesday. Or you could notice that Tuesday has now happened three times, and ask what it is about Tuesdays. Like the tools in earlier chapters, the look-back in this chapter checks a shared setup over time. It never judges either person’s character, and it never settles “who owes what” for hours already passed. The formal name is the Deficit Audit . In accounting, a deficit is a shortfall. Here, the “deficit” is a job that keeps falling short, and the “audit” is a careful, friendly look back at your month’s notes, more like a self-check than an inspection. The whole program rests on five pillars . Each one starts inside you and then shows up between you and other people. This chapter mostly teaches Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives , with help from Pillar I, See the whole load . (Borrowed from systems thinking, behavioral economics and ledger accounting.) In you: Look at your own routines as a setup you can adjust. Spot the easy defaults, like “I’ll just do it,” that let a gap quietly reopen. Between you and others: Look at the month together, give repeating gaps a clear owner, and keep count so jobs don’t drift back to whoever notices first. By this point in the book, two rhythms are already running. There’s the 90-second daily check-in (WP-13) . And there’s the weekly catch-up, a short end-of-week talk where you tally the week in the Lemonade Stand (a free page where each person lists their hours) and take a quick look at your job list ( WP-03, One owner per job ). Both are useful because they are small and frequent. But small, frequent checks have a blind spot. A job that slips for one week looks like bad luck. The same job slipping week after week is a pattern, and patterns are easy to miss week by week. They show up best when you step back and look at a whole month. The monthly look-back is that step back. Instead of asking “How did this week go?”, it asks: which gaps kept reopening, however many times we closed them? Your four weekly notes from this month, in WP-01 (the “who did what” log). A few lines a week is all it needs. Your current WP-03 job list. The WP-04 sheet (“What keeps coming back?”), on paper or on screen. Twenty minutes, at a time you’ve both agreed on, when neither of you is rushed, hungry ","f":1},{"u":"/book/chapter-5.html","t":"The Monthly Relationship Look-Back (Chapter V)","h":"The monthly look-back: a calm relationship check-in","d":"Chapter V: a calm monthly look-back for couples and households to see what changed, celebrate it, and adjust the setup before resentment builds up.","s":["Weekly check-ins miss patterns.","You need four weeks of notes and one list.","Mark an ✗ for each week a job slipped.","A problem that keeps coming back usually has no real owner.","Sort each repeat into one of three kinds.","Count the gaps each month.","It’s not a review of either person.","Keep it short and kind.","Inside you, and between you","In real life","Common mix-ups","The fields behind this chapter"],"x":"Once a month, look back for the problems that keep coming back, and fix the setup instead of having the same argument again. Five Pillars: This chapter mostly teaches Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives , with help from Pillar I, See the whole load . What are the Five Pillars? The third Tuesday in a row The trash again. You could have the same ten-minute back-and-forth about Tuesday. Or you could notice that Tuesday has now happened three times, and ask what it is about Tuesdays. One missed job looks like bad luck. The same job missed week after week is a pattern, and it’s easiest to see when you look at the whole month. The “audit” here is a careful, friendly look back, more like a self-check than an inspection. Dig deeper: why once a month Bring the month’s four weekly notes from WP-01 (the “who did what” log; a few lines a week is enough) and your job list from WP-03 (one owner per job). That’s all. Twenty minutes is usually enough. If either of your stress tanks is high that day (0.60 or more on the Battery & Stress Meter (WP-02) ), move it a day or two. Dig deeper: getting ready A job “slipped” if it was noticed but not done, only done after being raised more than once, or quietly covered by someone who isn’t its owner. Look closely at every job with two or more ✗. Three or four in a month is a real pattern, not just bad luck. Dig deeper: gathering the month’s notes It gets patched every week and never fixed. The owner is the person who does the job, start to finish. The fix is to give the job one owner whose name sticks, or to talk honestly about whether anyone has the time and energy for it. Dig deeper: spotting the gaps that keep reopening No clear owner: update the job list. An owner who can’t keep up: have a kind, honest talk. A short-term cause, like illness or travel: note it and move on. Dig deeper: sorting each gap Write down how many gaps of each kind you found. If the number of jobs with no clear owner falls over several months, the setup is working; one month can be a normal swing. If it keeps rising, something bigger has changed, and it’s worth a talk. Dig deeper: keeping count You don’t add up old hours or punish old mistakes. You can thank someone for what they carried before; you just don’t bill for the past. The only question is where the setup isn’t working yet, and what you’ll try next mo","f":0},{"u":"/book/preface-in-depth.html","t":"Preface in depth: Unbilled Debt","h":"Unbilled Debt","d":"The full Preface: every home keeps a set of books nobody agreed to. Why the invisible work of a shared life matters, and why noticing it is not keeping score.","s":["Where this fits: the Five Pillars","Unbilled debt: the work that goes unseen","The kinds of unseen work","Why the other person doesn’t see it","Writing it down is not keeping score","Your side, their side","Why the book borrows these words","How the book fits together","Who this book is for","An example week","Where the nine fields meet in this chapter","How they connect","When it goes wrong, and when it goes right","Questions people often ask","What if the other person doesn’t want to do this?","Do we have to write everything down forever?","What if our lists show a big gap?","Does this only count chores?","Where can I ask my own question?","Limits and cautions"],"x":"Sunday, 9pm Someone notices the laundry is still in the washer, the school forms are due on Tuesday, and the birthday card never got mailed. They also notice that nobody else has noticed. The house looks fine. That’s the problem. Every home keeps a set of accounts that nobody agreed to keep. Someone notices the milk is low. Someone remembers the dentist appointment three weeks away. Someone senses the mood in the room shifting before an argument starts, and quietly steers it somewhere calmer. None of this shows up on a calendar. None of it gets a thank-you, because you can only thank someone for work you have seen. And this kind of work is hidden by nature: when it’s done well, nothing seems to happen. The whole program rests on five pillars . Each one starts inside you and then shows up between you and other people. This preface mostly teaches Pillar I, See the whole load , with help from Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . (Borrowed from finance and economics.) In you: Notice everything you carry, including the thinking and feeling work you have never counted. Notice, too, the work others do that you may have missed. Between you and others: Put both lists on one shared, fair page, so nobody has to argue about whose work “counts.” Jobs nobody claims tend to drift to whoever notices first, and a specific thank-you helps keep things steady. Put together, the five pillars make one simple loop: you see the load (I), redesign the setup around it (II), check your state before you talk (III), tune the message so it lands (IV), and keep an eye on the quiet incentives that let things slide back (V). Pillar Where the book teaches it Pillar I, See the whole load This preface, Chapter II and Chapter V Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person Chapter II , Chapter IV and Chapter V Pillar III, Read your state first Chapter III , with help in Chapters I, II and IV Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive Chapter I , with help in Chapter III Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives This preface, Chapter IV and Chapter V Over months and years, that hidden work builds up like a debt that only one person can see on their books. The other person usually isn’t refusing to pay it. Most of the time, they don’t know it exists. This book calls that gap unbilled debt : the difference between the work someone does and the work anyone notices. In a business, an unbilled hour is work t","f":1},{"u":"/book/preface.html","t":"Invisible Labor at Home: The Work Nobody Sees","h":"The work nobody sees: invisible labor at home","d":"The Preface: the planning, remembering and noticing that keeps a home running, why this invisible work goes unseen, and how seeing it changes everything.","s":["Unseen work quietly adds up.","A lot of it is thinking, not doing.","The other person usually just hasn’t seen it.","Writing it down isn’t keeping score.","Each of you writes only your own side.","The borrowed words are there to take the blame away.","One small step at a time.","It can help in many kinds of shared life.","Inside you, and between you","The fields behind this chapter","In real life","Common mix-ups","Limits and cautions"],"x":"Running a shared home takes a lot of work that nobody sees. This book helps you make that work visible to both of you, so you can talk about it fairly and kindly. Five Pillars: This preface mostly teaches Pillar I, See the whole load , with help from Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . What are the Five Pillars? Sunday, 9pm Someone notices the laundry is still in the washer, the school forms are due on Tuesday, and the birthday card never got mailed. They also notice that nobody else has noticed. The house looks fine. That’s the problem. Remembering appointments, noticing the milk is low, calming a tense moment: none of it shows up on a calendar, so it rarely gets a thank-you. Over months, it piles up. The book calls this pile unbilled debt . Nobody sends a bill for it. You can thank someone for what they carried before; you just don’t bill for the past. Dig deeper: the work that goes unseen Before a job gets done, someone has to notice it, remember it, plan it and check it happened. That thinking takes real time and energy, even though nobody can see it. Dig deeper: the kinds of unseen work When this work is done well, nothing seems to happen. So the other person isn’t ignoring it. From where they stand, it looks like things take care of themselves. Dig deeper: why it stays hidden The point is for both of you to see the same picture, in the same words, at the same time. Then the talk starts from facts you share, not from two different memories. Dig deeper: why a ledger isn’t a grudge Nobody fills in anything for the other person. You each note what you did, then read the two lists together. That keeps it fair and keeps it kind. Dig deeper: your side, their side The book uses a few terms from accounting and science because they describe what’s happening without saying whose fault it is. Each one is explained in plain words, and you never need them to use anything here. Dig deeper: why the book borrows these words Each chapter adds one idea and one tool. One week of notes is enough to start. Later come a daily 90-second check-in about how much you were carrying today, a weekly catch-up and a monthly look-back. If you use the monthly look-back later, a few lines a week is all it needs. Dig deeper: how the book fits together Partners, family, roommates, co-parents and coworkers all share unseen work. The examples often use a couple at home, but the ideas fi","f":0},{"u":"/brand.html","t":"Spread Love & Acceptance. See it fairly. Say it kindly. For yourself and others. The Objective Ledger","h":"Our little logo","d":"The Spread Love & Acceptance logo: two different friends sharing one heart. The marks, the lockups, the colors, how to use them and downloads.","s":["The marks","The lockups","Sizes","Colors","How to use it","Please do","Please don’t","Downloads"],"x":"Meet Peach and Lilac. One is round and one is a squishy square, because nobody is wired quite the same. They share one heart between them, and they are very pleased about it. Snuggled in front are the two pups from the Journey: one leaner with a black mask and a big smile, one stocky with floppy ears, white feet and a gentle wag. They share a little heart of gold. That’s the whole idea of Spread Love & Acceptance: see each other fairly, and hold on kindly. The mark For the header, footer, app icons and anywhere the logo stands alone. SVG PNG Hello, friend! The playful one, for friendly spots: lost pages, offline, celebrations. SVG PNG On dark A cream sticker edge keeps everyone bright on the night pages, pups included. SVG Tiny size Just the two pup faces and a heart. Used for the browser tab. SVG 32px 16px The mark with the name beside it, or stacked on top. Use these where the name isn’t already on the page. See it fairly. Say it kindly. For yourself and others. That’s our tagline, and it sits under the name in both lockups. It says the whole idea in one breath: look at what happened fairly, say it with kindness, and do both for yourself as much as for the people around you. On the stacked version it breaks after “Say it kindly.” The small marks, the header icon and the browser-tab icon never carry words; they’re too small for them. SVG PNG SVG PNG 128 64 36 24 16 Peach #FCD5C2 / #E0957B The round friend Lilac #DCCEF4 / #9C83D0 The squishy square friend Heart #F08DA8 / #D2668A What they share Cheeks #F4A2B8 Rosy, always Sparkle #FAD27A / #DDA23A A little joy Ink #2B2620 Faces and words Cream #FFFDF8 Background Blush #FFF3F7 Background Mist #EEF2FB Background Ledger blue #2F5F8A Links and accents Headings are set in Fraunces and body text in Lora, the same as the rest of the site. Give them room: leave at least a heart’s width of space all around. Use the full mark at 24px or bigger, and the tiny-size icon below that. Keep the tagline readable: show the side-by-side lockup at 480px wide or more and the stacked one at 240px or more. Smaller than that, use the mark on its own. Use the dark version on dark or busy backgrounds. Save the winking one for happy, friendly moments. When the mark stands alone, give it the alt text “Spread Love & Acceptance”. Next to the name, leave the alt text empty so screen readers don’t say it twice. Stretch, squash, tilt or reco","f":0},{"u":"/calc01-solvency.html","t":"Is the Chore Split Fair? A Free Calculator (CALC-01)","h":"Is the setup working for everyone? Also called the solvency read · for 2 to 8 people","d":"A free calculator for the household split: time, effort and the invisible jobs, so you can see whether the setup works for everyone.","s":["Check your state","Who's sharing the load?","Balance: how the hours split workload balance","The week's hours","Ownership: does each job have a name? ownership clarity","The job list","Stress: how much each person is carrying average load (AS)","Everyone's load score","Retuning: repair after rough moments retuning (RF)","Counting repairs","The read","The math, step by step","Where it is strong, and where it is short","What would help most","Trend log","How it's scored","The two formulas","The four inputs","Balance for more than two people, worked through","Why these weights","The bands","Reading the two scores together","Edge cases, defined","Drift rule"],"x":"CALC-01 · THE FULL CALCULATOR This scores one narrow thing: whether the way you share the work right now, as you logged it, can keep going. It never scores a person or the relationship. It works for any group that shares a load: partners, families, roommates, co-carers, a small team at work. (\"Solvency\" is an accounting word. Here it just means the setup can carry its own weight.) A place to start a conversation, not a verdict. A low score doesn't blame anyone, and a high score doesn't mean anyone should stop speaking up. CALC-01 puts Pillar I, See the whole load and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work. Step Zero uses Pillar III, Read your state first , and the retuning count uses Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive . In you: you see the whole load you carry, and check your state before you read the number. Between you and others: you get one fair read of the setup, never a score on anyone. The math comes from Chapter II . Only have totals? Try the quick read . To follow it week by week, use REPORT-01 . Step zero Before you read a number about how you share the load, notice how you feel right now. The same number lands differently when you're calm than when you're tired or wound up. This is a check on yourself only. It never changes the score; it changes whether today is the day to act on it. Right now Pick the one closest to how you feel. It's optional, and you can change it any time. Set up List the people who share this set of jobs and can each fill in their own WP-02 (How much are you carrying?). Two to eight people. Names stay on this page. How many people? Or load a worked example to see how it works: You keep a Workpaper Suite draft on this device: WP-01 · Who did what Hours each person logged → Balance WP-03 · One owner per job Jobs with an owner → Ownership WP-02 · Battery check Five things each person rates about themselves → Stress WP-09 · Tone filter Rough moments you retuned → Retuning (apex only) Two scores, two questions. The solvency read uses balance, ownership and stress, and asks: can this week's load last as it's set up? The apex score adds retuning and asks a wider question: is the whole system working, including repair after rough moments? They can disagree, and the gap is useful. Input 1 of 4 · Pillar I From WP-01, Who did what Enter this week's rough hours for each job, for each person. Rough is fine: the split is wh","f":0},{"u":"/calm-visualizer.html","t":"Drift: Calm Visuals and Soft Tones to Unwind","h":"How are you feeling right now?","d":"Tell Drift how you feel, put on headphones, and let slow, dreamy visuals, kind words and gentle tones keep you company for a few quiet minutes of calm.","s":["Welcome back","About Drift","Somewhere gentle to go next"],"x":"Drift A calm visualizer 10:00 left Paused Binaural beats only work with headphones. Pop them in before you begin. Pick the closest one. There’s no wrong answer. Drift will meet you there with sound, color and a slow breath, and take its time with you. What are binaural beats? · Everything is made right here on your device. How strong is it right now? A little A lot How long? Pictures Soft background sound Check your headphones Volume Start low. The tones should sit softly in the background, never loud. Breathing words Not while driving or doing anything that needs your full attention. Stop anytime if it doesn’t feel good. Volume 30% Take a moment before you move. How do you feel now? Noticing how you feel, before and after, is Pillar III: Read your state first . A settled moment like this is a kinder time for a talk you’ve been saving. Somewhere gentle to go next: Night Garden Soundscapes Calm-Down Kit Today’s Weather Pause & Play Drift is a quiet few minutes for your senses. You tell it how you feel, and it chooses a soft pair of tones, a slow breathing pace and deep, dreamy pictures to match. Then, over the first few minutes, everything slows down a little. You don’t have to do anything. Watch the light, let your breath follow it if you like, and let your thoughts drift past. When the time is up, the sound fades away on its own. This is Pillar III: Read your state first one of the Five Pillars In you. Drift begins by asking how you feel. That small pause is the pillar: noticing whether your battery is calm, revved up or shut down before you judge the moment you’re in. Between you and others. Your state shapes how other people’s words land. The same sentence can sound like an attack when you’re on edge and like a fair point when you’re settled. So if a hard talk is coming, a few minutes here first helps you hear the other person more kindly and speak more softly yourself. And if a conversation is heating up, it’s okay to say “let me take ten minutes,” settle, and come back. An honest note about binaural beats What they are. When each ear hears a slightly different steady tone, say 200 Hz on the left and 208 Hz on the right, your brain hears a soft, slow “wah-wah” at the difference: 8 beats a second. That beat isn’t in the air. It’s made between your ears, which is why it only works with headphones. What to expect. Nothing special is promised. Drift is simpl","f":0},{"u":"/carrier-wave-decoder.html","t":"Carrier Wave Decoder: When a Talk Starts to Go Wrong","h":"Carrier Wave Decoder: when a conversation starts to go wrong","d":"A guided, step-by-step tool for the moment a conversation starts going sideways: check your state, get back in tune, and look back on the week together.","s":["One session, start to finish","Scenarios","What was out of sync?","Where are you listening from?","One-minute retune","Things that keep coming up, and a plan for each","A summary for the other person","Your journal"],"x":"The Objective Ledger TOOLS ← Read: Check-ins Tool For the moments when two reasonable people fall out of sync. Work out what happened, check your state, practice with a guided script, and keep notes on what works. It puts the radio-frequency idea from Chapter I into practice. If you still need to say the hard thing, say it in a check-in , not in the heat of the moment. Stick to one topic, with no blame and no criticism of the person. The ground rules explain how to notice things heating up early, and how to come back. Test your opening sentence in the Signal Translator before you try again. The Decoder puts Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive to work, and its state check uses Pillar III, Read your state first . In you: you notice your own state and how you are hearing things. Between you and others: you treat a mismatch as tuning, not a moral failing, and find words that land. Two signals, slightly apart 0 of 27 practices completed When a conversation went wrong, work through it in this order. Each step takes a few minutes, and you can skip any of them. Pick the state that feels closest to how you are right now. Not sure? . These are the same three words used across the site (Clear, Gusty, Fogged in), and they're only everyday names for how you feel, not measurements. Your notes Think of one recent moment that started to go wrong, with anyone: a partner, family, a co-parent, a roommate, a coworker, a friend. For each of the three channels, tap where you were, then your best guess at where they were. Pace is how fast you were talking and expecting replies. Tone is what sat underneath the words. Timing is how soon it needed to happen. Not sure how it works? Load a worked example: In tune A way to name it without blame What happened? (optional) Five quick questions. Answer for right now, not for how you usually are. Five quick questions. Answer for right now, not for how you usually are. ldquo;I can Five quick questions. Answer for right now, not for how you usually are. rsquo;t tell Five quick questions. Answer for right now, not for how you usually are. rdquo; is a fine answer. Use this before a hard conversation, or in the middle of one. Long exhale helps when you're keyed up. Look around helps when you've gone flat or far away. Ready when you are About one minute A private check before you send. This reads only your own draft, and only in this browser.","f":0},{"u":"/check-ins-in-depth.html","t":"Check-ins in depth — A Safe Room for a Hard Conversation","h":"Check-ins","d":"How to hold a hard conversation kindly: a calm time and place, listening before any reply, a reply that does not erase what was heard, and a plan for both.","s":["Ground rules","Do not overload the conversation","Stay on topic","No blame, and no criticism of the person","No negative intent, on either side","Prepare, so you are not sorting it out mid-conversation","Notice rising upset (dysregulation) before it is too late","Regulate before the next sentence","How to regroup","The setting has to be right before the topic is","A safe time","A safe environment","External factors, regulated on purpose","A stable connection, before the content","The order, and why it is this order","Acknowledgment, before any answer","Acknowledgment across wirings","How to rebut without erasing what you just heard","A solution that works for both","Using the time to turn a negative into a positive","How to build the talking notes","Where the Signal Translator sits in the program","A card you can read from","What this page can't do"],"x":"Wednesday, 10:50 p.m. You start the conversation because it is finally quiet. It goes badly, because it is 10:50 p.m. and you are both nearly empty. The topic was fine. The timing and setting were wrong. A check-in is not a fight with a time on the calendar. It is a short, agreed conversation in a setting that can hold it. That means you are both calm enough to listen, outside noise is turned down, and you take turns in an order that puts acknowledgment before any rebuttal (your side of the story). The hard topic can wait for that setting. It rarely goes well without it. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar III, Read your state first and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , part of the Five Pillars . In you: check how full your battery is before a hard talk. Between you and others: agree on the timing, pause and come back when you need to, and say it so it lands. The daily version already lives in WP-13 , the 90-second check-in: ninety seconds, one update each, no debate. This page covers the other kind: one topic that has been waiting, twenty to forty minutes, and a pause if either of you gets too upset to stay in the conversation. Before the first hard one, each of you can make a Wiring Card , so you both already know how the other takes in words. The Signal Translator can hold several sentences in your talking notes and save them as one PDF to bring with you. How to build that list is below . Leave the scorekeeping at home. Test the opening sentence How the same words land differently Ground rules The room The order Acknowledgment A proper rebuttal A solution for both Negatives to positives Talking notes Where the translator sits A card to read from A hard topic is allowed. Overload, blame, and criticism of the person are not. The moment a check-in becomes a pile of grievances, a case against someone's character, or a hunt for bad intent, it has stopped being a check-in. Neither person is here to be found guilty. Say that out loud before you start, so neither of you is left to guess. SAY THIS ONCE, AND MEAN IT \"I am not against you. I am not saying you are the problem. I do not think you meant harm. I want to sort out this one thing, and I can hear how it landed.\" If you are the listener, say the other half: \"I am not taking this as an attack until I have mirrored it. If I start defending, stop me.\" One time is enough. Repeating it after eve","f":1},{"u":"/check-ins.html","t":"Relationship Check-Ins: A Kind Weekly Ritual for Couples","h":"Check-ins: a kind weekly relationship check-in","d":"A short, kind weekly relationship check-in for couples, families and housemates: what went well, what felt heavy, and one small change to try next week.","s":["One topic, and you’re on the same side.","Pick a good moment first.","Hear it back before you answer.","A pause is fine. Come back to the same topic.","Turn a rough moment into one small habit.","Write your sentences down before you go in."],"x":"A check-in is a short, agreed talk about one hard topic, at a time that works for both of you. The topic can wait for that time, and it goes much better when it gets one. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar III, Read your state first and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , part of the Five Pillars . In you: check how full your battery is before a hard talk. Between you and others: agree on the timing, pause and come back when you need to, and say it so it lands. Wednesday, 10:50 p.m. You start the conversation because it is finally quiet. It goes sideways, because it is 10:50 p.m. and you are both running on empty. The topic was fine. The timing just wasn’t. Say it once before you start: “I am not against you. I do not think you meant harm. I want to sort out this one thing.” Talk about what happened, not about who the other person is. If a second topic comes up, jot it down for another day. Dig deeper: the ground rules Choose a time you both agreed on, not the middle of a stressful moment. Private, phones down, nobody hungry or drinking. If either of you feels too wound up or too flat, that’s okay. Pick another time and say when. Dig deeper: a safe time and place One person says one fact and how it landed. The other says back what they heard and asks, “Did I get that?” Then you swap. If something needs clearing up, wait until you have both been heard. Dig deeper: the order, and why If a voice goes up or someone goes quiet, take a break and say when you’ll come back. When you do, it’s a fresh start, not round two: “I’m back. The topic is still the sink. I am not against you.” Dig deeper: noticing it early, and how to regroup Say how it affected you, in plain, kind words. Then agree on one small thing you can keep doing, and name one thing the other person already does well. That way a hard moment helps you build something better. Dig deeper: turning a negative into a positive List everything you want to raise, then keep just the one that would make the rest feel lighter. The Signal Translator, a tool that shows how a sentence may land, can hold your notes and save them as one page. You don’t have to bring the whole list. Dig deeper: building your talking notes Try this: Ask for twenty minutes at a set time for one small topic, and practice the steps there before you use them on a big one. Start your talking notes → A grandparent talking with ","f":0},{"u":"/chore-chart-for-couples.html","t":"Fair Chore Chart for Couples (Free, Printable)","h":"A fair chore chart for couples (free, printable)","d":"A free, printable chore chart for couples, families and roommates: list every job, including the invisible ones, give each one an owner and review it weekly.","s":["How to use this chore chart in four steps","The printable chore chart","What makes a chore split fair?","Prefer a chore tracker on your phone?","A chore chart for roommates and families","Questions people often ask","How do you split chores fairly as a couple?","Should chores be split 50/50?","What if my partner won’t use a chore chart?","How often should we change the chart?","Is this chore chart really free?"],"x":"A fair chore chart for couples lists every job at home, including the invisible ones like planning meals and remembering appointments. It gives each job one owner who handles it from start to finish, and you both look at it for ten minutes once a week. Fair doesn’t have to mean fifty-fifty. It means you both agree the split works. The chart below is free: type on it or print it. List the jobs together. Start with the rows below, cross out what doesn’t fit, and add your own. Include the thinking jobs, not just the doing jobs. Pick one owner for each job. The owner notices when it needs doing, plans it and does it. No reminders needed. If you can’t agree, try one owner for a month and swap after. WP-03, One owner per chore , has more help with this. Tick the days it got done. A quick tick is enough. This is not a scorecard. It just shows what is really happening, so you don’t have to argue from memory. Hold a ten-minute review each week. What went well? What slipped? Is one person carrying too much this week? Change one thing, then stop. Keep it short and kind. Tap any cell to type. Nothing you type is saved or sent anywhere. Our fair chore chart Week of ______________ · One owner per job · Spread Love & Acceptance Job Owner How often The thinking part M T W T F S S Dishes and kitchen reset Daily Run and empty the dishwasher, wipe counters Meal planning and groceries Weekly Plan meals, check what we have, make the list Cooking dinner Most days Know what’s for dinner before 5 p.m. Laundry 2–3 times a week Notice the full basket, fold, put away Trash and recycling Weekly Remember pickup day, replace bags Bathroom cleaning Weekly Restock soap and paper, clean Bills and money admin Monthly Track due dates, check accounts Appointments and forms As needed Book, remember, fill in, send back Birthdays, gifts and cards As needed Remember dates, buy, wrap, send Pet care Daily Food, walks, vet visits, supplies Weekly review (10 minutes): What went well? What slipped? One thing we’ll change: ______________________________ On a phone, swipe the chart sideways to see every day. It prints on one landscape page. A fair chore split is one you both agree works, for this season of your lives. It is not always equal. If one of you works longer hours, has a long commute, or is caring for a parent, a fair split might look uneven on paper and still feel right to both of you. Three q","f":0},{"u":"/communication-style-quiz.html","t":"Communication Style Quiz: Find Your Wave Code (Free)","h":"Communication style quiz: find your Wave Code","d":"A free communication style quiz: find your four-letter Wave Code and which of 16 archetypes fits, from pace and detail to how you send and receive words.","s":["What the quiz looks at: the four Wave Code letters","Pace","Big picture or details","How you send","How you receive","Why your communication style matters in relationships","How to use your result with someone else","What this quiz is, and what it isn’t","Questions people often ask","Is this a personality quiz?","What are the four communication styles?","Can my Wave Code change?","Is one communication style best?","Is the communication style quiz free and private?","Do I have to pick a neurotype?","Keep going"],"x":"This free communication style quiz, called Wavelength , shows how you naturally talk and listen. It takes about ten minutes, mostly tapping. You answer short questions about everyday moments and get a four-letter Wave Code : your pace, whether you see the big picture or the details first, how you send words and how you receive them. Your code matches one of 16 archetypes, like The Firefly, The Owl or The Quilt. It is a mirror for self-discovery, not a test you can pass or fail. Ready? It’s free, there are no wrong answers, and nothing you choose is sent anywhere. Take the Wavelength quiz Each letter of your Wave Code comes from one pair. Most people lean one way in each pair, and many sit close to the middle on at least one. Q Quick Spark · S Slow Simmer Do you think out loud and answer fast, or do you like a little time to think before you speak? Quick Sparks bring energy. Slow Simmers bring depth. F Forest · T Trees Do you start with the big idea and fill in later, or do you need the steps and specifics first? Forests see where it’s all going. Trees make sure it works. D Direct · N Nuanced Do you say it plainly, or do you soften it, add context and read the room first? Direct senders are clear. Nuanced senders are careful. E Explicit · A Attuned Do you take words at face value and prefer things spelled out, or do you pick up on tone, pauses and what’s left unsaid? Explicit listeners hear the words. Attuned listeners hear the music. Put the four letters together and you get your Wave Code. There are 16 possible codes, and each one has its own archetype with a friendly name. QFNA is The Firefly: bright, warm, full of ideas and quick to sense how others feel. SFDE is The Owl: thinks long and deep, speaks plainly and takes words at face value. STNA is The Quilt: patient, detail-minded and gentle, stitching people together by noticing everything. Many arguments between kind people are not really disagreements. They are style mismatches. A Quick Spark asks a question and hears silence. A Slow Simmer feels rushed and put on the spot. A Direct sender says “this isn’t working” and means the plan. An Attuned listener hears it as being about them. When you can name your styles, you can stop taking these moments personally. “I’m a Slow Simmer, so give me until after dinner” is much easier to hear than a long silence. Good communication in relationships often starts wi","f":0},{"u":"/contents-in-depth.html","t":"Contents in depth — Everything in the Program","h":"Everything in the program","d":"Everything in The Objective Ledger, in three parts: self-discovery tools, relationship tools for any two people, and where to start in each relationship.","s":["Not sure where to start?","Check in with yourself","Understand your wiring","Settle and regulate","Understand yourself","Understand what’s shared","Talk it through","Share the work fairly","Stay in step","Partners","Family","Co-parents","Friends","Roommates","Coworkers & teams","Caregivers","Site Directory","Start here","Tools","Explore, play & listen","About & status","Legal"],"x":"The program has two halves, and this page holds both. Part 1 is for understanding yourself. Part 2 holds the shared tools. Part 3 shows which of them fit each kind of relationship. The site directory at the bottom lists everything else. Tap whatever is closest to true right now. This is a shortcut into the lists below, not a different program. The program has two halves. Self-discovery comes first: tools for understanding yourself. Relationship tools come next: shared tools for any two people, followed by a guide to which ones fit each kind of relationship. Everything is free while the program is being built. Items marked free · email open when you sign up below. Ways In explains each level. Jump to: Self-discovery Relationship tools By relationship type Sign up free Part 1 Self-discovery: understanding yourself An objective, outside look at your own load, wiring and patterns. Start here, with or without anyone else. Daily Today’s Weather A one-minute read on your own conditions: the sky, the pressure, sleep, people load and what’s blowing in. You get a forecast, a talk window, what today is good for, and a private almanac of your patterns. free WP-02 The Battery & Stress Meter Five questions that separate the load you’re already carrying from what just happened. free · email unlocked Tool Frequency Calibration Measure your natural rhythms for money, decisions, check-ins and recovery before you compare them with anyone else’s. free · email unlocked Guide Wired Differently How your neurotype (the way your brain is wired) shapes the way words reach you, across 14 kinds of wiring, from neurotypical to autistic, ADHD, anxious and highly sensitive. free Tool Wiring Card Put what you learn on one page: how you receive words, what your silence means, and what to avoid. Share it only if you choose. free WP-11 The Calm-Down Kit Decide in advance what settles your body, so it’s ready when a hard moment comes. free · email unlocked Audio Soundscape Catalog Background audio made for settling down and focusing. Optional. free Ch. III Autonomic Saturation & the 7 Ocular Vectors Why some reactions are bigger than their cause, and the angle you tend to see things from. free · email unlocked Ch. IV Deontological Parity & Sensory Gating Sensory gating: giving a comment time to land before you react to it. free · email unlocked Stories Stories from Philosophy: Knowing yourself","f":1},{"u":"/contents.html","t":"Contents: Every Free Tool, Guide and Chapter","h":"Everything in the program","d":"Everything in the program in three parts: tools for understanding yourself, tools for any two people, and where to start in your kind of relationship.","s":[],"x":"Every page in the program, grouped by what it’s for. Tap any title to open it. Not sure where to begin? Start here , or take the short path in Start in 10 minutes . Pages marked Free with sign-up open once you enter your email; it’s free while the program is being built. Pages with a Dig deeper link also come in a full version. Looking for something specific? Use Search in the menu. Open the full contents page Try this: Start with one minute on yourself: a quick read on how today is going for you. Check today’s weather →","f":0},{"u":"/conversation-reader.html","t":"Text Thread Reader: Calm Replies After a Fight","h":"The Conversation Reader: calm replies after a fight over text","d":"Paste a text thread after a fight and see where it turned, what each of you may be hearing, and calmer replies built from your own words. Free and private.","s":["01Paste the conversation"],"x":"Paste a text thread, chat or email exchange. The Reader shows where it turned, what each of you may be hearing, and a calmer way to answer. A long thread is easier to paste on a laptop or desktop, where you can select a whole conversation at once. On a phone, copying a few messages at a time works fine. Private by design. The Reader runs entirely in your browser. What you paste is never saved, collected or sent anywhere, and nobody sees it but you. Close the tab and it’s gone. Copy the messages from your phone, a chat app or an email, and paste them here. Names at the start of each line help (“Sam: …”), but they’re not required. If there are names, the Reader will ask which one is you. The conversation What can I paste? Lines with names: “Sam: …” / “Alex: …”, or “Me:” and “Them:”. Slack, Teams or Discord: select the messages and copy them. A “Sam Lee 10:02 AM” or “Sam — Today at 10:02” line with the message under it works, and so does “[10:02 AM] Sam Lee”. Lines with times: “[10:02] Sam: …”. A WhatsApp or iMessage export (“[date, time] Sam: …”, without media). A copied text thread with no names: put a blank line between messages, and the Reader will alternate speakers. You can switch who said what afterward. An email thread, with the “On … wrote:” lines left in. Screenshots of a chat (iMessage, WhatsApp, Messenger, texts, Slack): use “Choose screenshots”, or paste or drop them. They’re read on this device and never uploaded, and you check the words and sides before the Reader looks at them. A conversation from memory (in person or on the phone): write it as “Me:” and “Them:” lines. 04 Check your reply before you send it Write what you’re thinking of sending. The Reader checks it the same way, as you type. Your reply What it looks for The list of patterns appears here. What the Reader can and can’t do The Reader looks for patterns in words: absolutes like “always” and “never”, sentences about who someone is, dismissals, sarcasm, eye-rolls and passive-aggressive jabs, comparisons, shutting the door without a time to come back, capitals, apologies and other repair attempts, clear asks, questions that may not have been answered, the past being brought in, and long silences when times are included. It weighs them to show where the conversation got heated. It can’t hear tone of voice, and it doesn’t know your history, your in-jokes, or what either of you meant. Th","f":0},{"u":"/daily-ledger-crossword.html","t":"Daily Crossword Puzzle, Newspaper Style (Free)","h":"The Daily Ledger Crossword: a free daily crossword","d":"A free newspaper-style daily crossword with fair, friendly clues, from a quick 5x5 mini to a big Sunday grid. Play in your browser on any device, at your pace.","s":["Solved","Across","Down"],"x":"A real newspaper-style crossword. Every day there’s a new 5×5 Mini and one bigger grid that grows through the week, with a 13×13 Big Sunday on Sundays. Today’s puzzles are the same for everyone, and nearly two thousand more wait under the sizes below. Each word glows when it’s right, and there’s no timer. How it works: tap a square and type; tap it again to switch between across and down. The small numbers match the clues. Some squares belong to only one word, as in many newspaper crosswords. The short, one-word clues are the trickier ones. On a phone, ⊕ makes the squares bigger on the large grids (the grid then scrolls sideways).","f":0},{"u":"/do/index.html","t":"Try the workpapers","h":"","d":"A practice space to try the core worksheets on a worked example before you use your own week. Nothing is saved.","s":["What you can try"],"x":"Practice space See how the worksheets work on a worked example before you use your own week. The Playground lets you try Pillar I, See the whole load , Pillar III, Read your state first and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person in a few minutes. In you: you notice what you really carry. Between you and others: you see how clear owners change the picture. The Playground uses an example household of three roommates, with real math you can change. It works the same way for partners, families, co-carers and teams at work: the real worksheets take 2 to 8 people. Enter the Playground WP-01, Who did what: log a week and see how the hours split (Pillar I) WP-02, How much are you carrying?: five quick questions about how much you're carrying (higher = heavier) (Pillar III) WP-03, One owner per job: give every regular job a named owner (Pillar II) CALC-01, Is the setup working for everyone?: put the three together and see what would move the score Nothing is saved, and no email is needed. When you're ready, the fill-in workpapers are the real thing, and the Workpaper Suite builds a full set for your situation. Not sure where to start? Take the 2-minute snapshot first.","f":0},{"u":"/do/workpaper-playground.html","t":"Workpaper Playground","h":"","d":"Practice the core worksheets on a worked example: log a week (WP-01), check a battery (WP-02), name owners (WP-03) and see if the load can last (CALC-01).","s":["Who did what: see the whole load","How much are you carrying? Read your state first","One owner per job: fix the setup, not the person","Is the setup working for everyone?","From practice to your real week"],"x":"Practice space Four tools, one worked example, real math. Change anything and watch the numbers move. Nothing is saved. WP-01 is Pillar I, See the whole load . WP-02 is Pillar III, Read your state first . WP-03 and CALC-01 are Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person . In you: you notice what you really carry, and how full your battery is. Between you and others: clear owners replace blame. The example is three roommates. Rename them if you like, or pretend they're your partner, your family or your team: the worksheets work the same way for 2 to 8 people. WP-01 · Pillar I For one ordinary week, jot down what took time, who did it and roughly how many minutes. Include the invisible work: planning, remembering, noticing. \"Shared\" splits the minutes evenly. Job Who Minutes Open the real WP-01 WP-02 · Pillar III Score five things about yourself for the last day or two, from 0 (not at all) to 4 (very true). Add them up and divide by 20. It's a self-check, not a health measure, and only ever filled in about yourself. Open the real WP-02 WP-03 · Pillar II For each regular job, name who is Responsible (does it) and who is Accountable (notices if it didn't happen). Leave unowned jobs on the list; they're the ones that matter most. Job Responsible Accountable Open the real WP-03 CALC-01 · Pillars I and II together CALC-01 puts the three together: balance from WP-01, ownership from WP-03, and the average of everyone's battery from WP-02. Each person fills in their own battery, so here the example roommates' scores are typed in for them. Use the quick CALC-01 Open the full CALC-01 Each person does the two-minute WP-02, How much are you carrying? before you start, so you pick a calm moment. Log one ordinary week in WP-01 (or the Lemonade Stand ). Sit down once and fill in WP-03 together. Put the numbers into CALC-01 and start with the biggest shortfall. Keep the weeks side by side in REPORT-01 , or build your whole Workpaper Suite . What happens to what you type here? Nothing. This page doesn't save or send anything. The real worksheets only keep a draft on your device if you tick the box that says so, and they let you erase it.","f":0},{"u":"/echoes-of-gold.html","t":"Echoes of Gold — The Companion Album","h":"Echoes of Gold","d":"Echoes of Gold (The Christian Project): the 11-track album that came before The Objective Ledger, produced by Christian, performing as Loves Journey EP.","s":["The Boundary & The Audit","Alignment & Arrival","Resilience & Devotion","The Private Sanctum"],"x":"Companion Album (The Christian Project) — The Prelude to Analytical R&B Christian, performing as Loves Journey EP · Released February 6, 2026 · 11 songs, 41 minutes Every system starts somewhere unrecorded. Before The Objective Ledger had a website, a workpaper, or a single formula, it had a soundtrack. This album was produced entirely by Christian, the same person who later built spreadloveandacceptance.com, and it came first. The math came after. The feeling came before the framework that tries to hold it. That order is the whole point of this project. The practical and the felt aren't two separate things running side by side, kept apart so neither embarrasses the other. They're meant to meet. The workpapers make the practical side easy to read: hours logged, ownership assigned, a number you can point to. This album carries what the numbers were never going to reach. The project only works when both sides reach toward the same point at once. This project calls that point, half seriously, the third eye: the moment the practical and the felt stop competing for attention and simply line up. Listen on Spotify This loads a player from Spotify. Nothing from Spotify loads, and nothing plays, until you choose to. Spotify may set its own cookies. Or open it on Spotify ↗ Listen on Apple Music This loads a player from Apple Music. Nothing from Apple loads, and nothing plays, until you choose to. Apple may set its own cookies. Or open it on Apple Music ↗ Phase I The Cooling Cycle Higher Frequency The Golden Ratio The relational principle: stepping out of reactive cycles and checking the emotional ledger: reclaiming peace, closing loops that don't resolve, and refusing to run at a deficit. Phase II GPS (God's Perfect Setup) The Wish The relational principle: trusting a detour as part of the route, and meeting someone else in mutual readiness rather than rushing to arrive. Phase III Seasonproof The Rebirth Keep Me Grounded The relational principle: a strength that bends without breaking: working through friction instead of walking away from it. Phase IV Ghostwriter Airplane Mode Carplay & Chill x Slow Cooker The relational principle: keeping the outside noise outside: stepping back from the feed to let something real take its time.","f":0},{"u":"/five-pillars-in-depth.html","t":"The Five Pillars in depth","h":"The Five Pillars","d":"The five fields The Objective Ledger borrows from, why each one, how the pillars work together as a loop, how each starts inside you and shows up between you.","s":["See the whole load","In you","Between you and others","Where it lives","Common mix-ups","Fix the setup, not the person","In you","Between you and others","Where it lives","Common mix-ups","Read your state first","In you","Between you and others","Where it lives","Common mix-ups","Tune how you send and receive","In you","Between you and others","Where it lives","Common mix-ups","Notice the quiet incentives","In you","Between you and others","Where it lives"],"x":"The Objective Ledger is built on five pillars. Each one borrows from a field that is good at describing how a system really works without deciding who is to blame. Each one starts as a way to understand yourself, and then becomes a way to understand what happens between you and other people. Plain names come first on this site. The field names are here so you can see where the ideas come from, not because you need them. If a word ever gets in the way, skip it. The idea underneath is always simple. I See the whole load II Fix the setup III Read your state first IV Tune how you send and receive V Notice the quiet incentives How they fit together How they lean on each other In each relationship Where to start The full map PILLAR I Field: ledger accounting Why this field. A ledger does one thing well: it writes down what actually happened, in shared units, on a page both sides can read. It doesn’t ask who is a good person. It asks what the record shows. That makes it a calm way to talk about work that is usually invisible: remembering, planning, noticing, smoothing things over. You notice everything you carry, including the load that never looks like a task: the worry about a parent, the list in your head, the effort of staying pleasant on a hard day. Seeing it clearly is often a relief on its own. It explains why you feel tired when “nothing happened.” You put it on one shared, fair page, so nobody has to argue about whose work “counts.” Two records side by side replace two memories in conflict. Partners compare a week of logs. Roommates list the chores. A caregiving sibling group writes down who handles appointments, pickups and calls. Two coworkers both feel they carry the team. Once each lists a week of follow-ups, reminders and meeting notes, it turns out one does most of the chasing and the other most of the writing. Neither was imagining it; they were each seeing half. Preface: the work nobody sees names the unseen work and why it deserves to be counted. Chapter II: is the split working? asks whether the way you share the load can last. WP-01: who did what is the week-long log that starts everything. The Lemonade Stand shows the split as a plain fact, with no blame. CALC-01: can the load last? and REPORT-01 add the numbers up and keep them week by week. “So it’s scorekeeping.” No. A score says who is winning. A ledger says what is there. The goal is a pic","f":1},{"u":"/five-pillars.html","t":"The Five Pillars of Fair, Kind Relationships","h":"The Five Pillars of fair, kind relationships","d":"Five simple ideas for fair, kind relationships: see the whole mental load, fix the setup instead of the person, read your stress first, and tune how you talk.","s":["See the whole load","In you","Between you and others","Fix the setup, not the person","In you","Between you and others","Read your state first","In you","Between you and others","Tune how you send and receive","In you","Between you and others","Notice the quiet incentives","In you","Between you and others","How they fit together","Start with one pillar","The full map","The book","Workpapers","Tools","Guides","Calm games and sound"],"x":"Everything on this site rests on five simple ideas. Each one starts inside you, as a way to understand yourself. Then it shows up between you and the people you share life with: partners, family, friends, roommates, coworkers and caregivers. Tuesday, 7 p.m. The sink is full again. You could say “you never help.” Or you could see everything you both did today (I), notice the dishes have no owner (II), check that you are too tired to talk well right now (III), say it later in a way that lands (IV), and notice that whoever sees the mess first has quietly become the one who always cleans it (V). That is all five pillars in one evening. I See the whole load II Fix the setup III Read your state first IV Tune how you send and receive V Notice the quiet incentives How they fit Where to start The full map PILLAR I Borrowed from ledger accounting Notice everything you carry, including the unseen parts: remembering, planning, worrying, keeping the peace. Put it all on one shared, fair page, so nobody has to argue about whose work “counts.” You remember the dentist, the birthday card and the gas bill. None of it looks like “doing the dishes,” so none of it gets seen. One week written down changes that, for partners, roommates and caregivers alike. Put to work in: Preface: the work nobody sees · Chapter II: is the split working? · WP-01: who did what · The Lemonade Stand · CALC-01: can the load last? · REPORT-01: your progress Dig deeper: why a ledger, and what it is not PILLAR II Borrowed from systems thinking See your habits and routines as a setup you can redesign, not a flaw in your character. Agree clear owners, handoffs and routines (one owner per job) instead of blame. The trash gets missed every week. Instead of “you never remember,” the trash get one owner and a Tuesday reminder. The same move works for a team’s follow-ups or a family’s school forms. Put to work in: WP-03: one owner per job · Chapter V: the monthly look-back · WP-04: what keeps coming back? · Chapter IV: two kinds of fair · How it fits your relationships · Six gentle weeks Dig deeper: why the setup, and where you still matter PILLAR III Borrowed from nervous-system science Know how full your battery is (calm and connected, revved up, or running on empty) before you judge a moment. Pick the timing. Pause and come back. Your state shapes how the other person’s words land, and theirs shapes how you","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-buddies-live.html","t":"Frequency Buddies Live: A Kids’ Cartoon Channel","h":"Frequency Buddies Live: drop in anytime","d":"An always-on Frequency Buddies channel: drop in anytime, watch the kids’ cartoon episode playing right now, and cast it to your TV. Free, with captions.","s":["Watch it on your TV","Coming up"],"x":"Frequency Buddies · live station The Frequency Buddies station is always on. Whenever you tune in, you join the episode that’s playing right now, just like live TV, and everyone watching sees the same moment. Live Now on: … Loading the schedule… Chromecast or Google TV: use Chrome on a computer or an Android phone, on the same Wi-Fi as the TV, and press Cast. The TV joins the station at the moment that’s playing now and keeps going on its own, so you can close this page. Apple TV or AirPlay: on an iPhone, iPad or Mac, tune in, then press Play on a TV (or the AirPlay button in full screen) and pick your TV. A smart TV with a web browser: open this page on the TV itself and press Tune in, then Full screen. The station plays all five episodes in order, around the clock. Times are shown in your time zone. Pick an episode yourself Episodes on shuffle, and downloads","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-buddies-music-video-maker.html","t":"Make Your Own Frequency Buddies Music Video","h":"Make your own music video","d":"Make your own Frequency Buddies theme song music video: pick the stage, lights, effects, dance moves, costumes and who’s on stage, then play it and share the.","s":[],"x":"Frequency Buddies · the music video maker You’re the director! Pick the stage, the lights, the dance moves, the costumes and who’s in the show, then press Play and watch Tidbit and Sugarfoot sing the theme song your way. The music video maker needs JavaScript to run. 🎤 Watch the original music video Watch the episodes No sign-up needed. Your saved videos stay in this browser only, and a shared link carries the whole video inside it, so nothing is sent anywhere. Press Calm version for softer, slower lights. The lights never flash.","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-buddies-music-video.html","t":"Frequency Buddies Theme Song Music Video","h":"The Frequency Buddies theme song: the music video","d":"The Frequency Buddies theme song as a music video: Tidbit, Sugarfoot and every friend from the episodes sing, dance and cheer on a stage that moves.","s":["What happens","Who’s in it"],"x":"Frequency Buddies · the music video The curtain goes up, the stage rises out of the floor, and Tidbit and Sugarfoot sing their song with every friend from the episodes: a band, a chorus line, a conga line and a cheering crowd. The music video needs JavaScript to run. The words of the song are written out below. 🎬 Make your own music video ⬇ Download the video (MP4, 17 MB) Watch the episodes Every episode on shuffle Drop in on the live station 📺 0:00 Curtain up. The marquee lights up, the stage rises out of the floor, the curtains open, and the pals ride up on two little lifts in top hats and bow ties. 0:04 Tune in! Side steps together, a spin and a jump, while the Moon sings along in a starry sky. 0:09 Tidbit’s verse. A puff of sparkles, and Tidbit is a rock star in shades. She zooms across the stage with the ducklings following the leader, and when the sky goes gray, a rainbow comes out. 0:17 Sugarfoot’s verse. Flower crowns and a sunset. Sugarfoot takes her time across the stage with Dot the snail beside her, and when it gets too loud, the lights go soft while she takes a breath. 0:24 Different is okay. Capes and crowns, rainbow lights, and a mirror dance. The band rises up at the back: Beep on drums, Hopper on keys, Ollie on horn and Nutmeg on guitar, with Professor Puddles conducting. 0:31 The big chorus. Sparkly vests, a disco ball, confetti cannons, a heart made of lights, and a conga line all the way around the stage. 0:52 Into space. Behind the curtain, space suits. The stage splits in two: Tidbit goes fast, Sugarfoot floats slow, one says yes and one says “No, thank you,” and then they glide back together on a turntable to say sorry. 1:04 Runnin’ free. Pirate hats, a sunset sea, a belly slide across the whole stage, and everyone running round together. 1:10 Grand finale. Marching band uniforms for everyone, a starburst, fireworks, a parade around the stage, and a lift that raises the pals up high for the last note. 1:27 Take a bow. Everyone bows, the crowd cheers, and the stage sinks away as the curtain closes. Tidbit and Sugarfoot The stars, singing their own verses and every chorus, with eight costume changes. The Buddies Band Beep the robot on drums, Hopper the frog on keys, Ollie the owl on horn and Nutmeg the squirrel on guitar, with Professor Puddles conducting. The chorus line and friends The ducklings, Dot the snail, the butterfly and the Mo","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-buddies-season-2.html","t":"Frequency Buddies Season 2: Watch the Teaser","h":"Season 2 is coming!","d":"Frequency Buddies Season 2 is coming: watch the one-minute teaser with Tidbit and Sugarfoot, spot the five hidden secrets and pick the new place you can’t wait.","s":["Spot the secrets","Which new place are you most excited for?","What’s coming","Catch up on Season 1","Want to know when it’s out?"],"x":"Frequency Buddies · Season 2 Tidbit and Sugarfoot are packing for bigger adventures. Watch the one-minute teaser, and keep your eyes open: five little secrets are hiding in it. The teaser needs JavaScript to play. ⬇ Download the teaser to share (MP4, 8 MB) A star sticker, a paw print and three more surprises are tucked into the teaser. Tap one on the picture when you see it, or pause and hunt. What you find is remembered on this device only. Pick a place once the page has loaded. The pals are heading farther than ever: up above the clouds, out to a lighthouse in the fog, to the top of a snowy mountain at sunrise, through a secret door in the library, into a glowing sea and under a sky full of shooting stars. Somebody new is waiting out there too. Who could it be? Some things never change. Season 2 is still gentle, funny and made for watching together, with captions, and every story ends a little stronger than it started. Tidbit is still bold, Sugarfoot is still thoughtful, and they still figure things out side by side. There’s no date yet. It’s on its way, and we’ll share more as it gets closer. 📺 Watch the episodes All five Season 1 adventures, from The Storm Over the Treehouse to The Longest Night. 🔀 Every episode on shuffle Press play once and the pals keep going, in a random order. Or download them to watch offline. 📡 The live station Drop in anytime and watch whatever’s playing right now, like live TV. Join free with just your email and you’ll be on the site’s update list, where news about new things like Season 2 goes out. It also opens every tool and chapter on the site. Join free Read the teaser Night in the backyard. Tidbit and Sugarfoot are watching the fireflies. A low rumble rolls in, and a warm glow rises behind the far hill. Their ears perk up. Tidbit: Did you hear that? Sugarfoot: It’s coming from… out there. The pals are back. A hot-air balloon above the clouds. A rowboat by a lighthouse in the fog. A snowy mountain top at sunrise. New places. A secret door swings open in the library. Lanterns over a night market. A glowing sea. Shooting stars from a rooftop telescope. New friends. In the tall grass, someone in shadow holds a little lantern and blinks two big, friendly eyes. Tidbit: Who’s that? Sugarfoot: A new friend…? Bigger adventures. Tidbit sniffs a dandelion: “Ah… ahh… CHOO!” Sugarfoot’s explorer hat flies away. “…Bless you!” Then Tidbi","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-buddies-shuffle.html","t":"Frequency Buddies: Every Episode on Shuffle","h":"Frequency Buddies: every episode on shuffle","d":"Every Frequency Buddies episode back to back in a random order, with the theme song: a free kids’ cartoon about feelings, plus each episode to download.","s":["Download the episodes"],"x":"Frequency Buddies · on shuffle Press play once and the pals keep going: every episode in a random order, each with its theme song at the start and credits at the end. When they’ve all played, a new order begins. Picking an episode… The player needs JavaScript to run. Pick an episode yourself Drop in on the live station 📺 The theme song music video 🎤 Season 2 teaser ✨ Save any episode to watch offline, on a long drive or anywhere without Wi-Fi. Each one has its theme song, credits and captions in the picture. Episode 1: The Storm Over the Treehouse ⬇ Download (MP4, 19 MB) Episode 2: Out of Tune ⬇ Download (MP4, 20 MB) Episode 3: The Heavy Basket ⬇ Download (MP4, 18 MB) Episode 4: Who Broke the Kite? ⬇ Download (MP4, 19 MB) Episode 5: The Longest Night ⬇ Download (MP4, 17 MB) ⬇ The whole season in one video (MP4, about 74 minutes, 93 MB)","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-buddies.html","t":"Frequency Buddies: A Kids’ Cartoon About Feelings","h":"Frequency Buddies: a kids’ cartoon about feelings","d":"Free animated episodes for kids and families: two pups, Tidbit and Sugarfoot, face tough times, talk it through and come out stronger. With captions.","s":["All episodes","About the show"],"x":"An animated adventure series Two pals. One big heart each. Every frequency an adventure. Tidbit and Sugarfoot set off on big little quests, hit real tough times, and find their way through, together. The player needs JavaScript to run. The whole story is also written out below. Read the whole story The story text appears here when the episode loads. Drop in on the live station , watch every episode on shuffle , or download any episode to watch offline . 🎤 Watch the theme song music video 🎬 Make your own music video ✨ Season 2 is coming: watch the teaser Season 1: The First Adventures. 1. The Storm Over the Treehouse, a treetop expedition against a storm. 2. Out of Tune, a musical treasure hunt across the city. 3. The Heavy Basket, a summit picnic and a load to share. 4. Who Broke the Kite?, a seaside kite race. 5. The Longest Night, a night quest to the shooting stars. Gentle adventures Storms, rope bridges, lost song pages and night walks, with a little suspense and a lot of heart. Nothing too scary, and every episode ends stronger than it started. A short note before each episode says what happens, like a storm with thunder in episode 1. Lightning shows as a soft glow unless you turn bright flashes on. Made for watching together About 16 minutes each, in chapters of about 2 to 4 minutes. You can watch one chapter at a time and stop there. Captions are always on, and you can turn the voices and music off anytime. Watching with your grandkids? See the page for grandparents . Quiet little lessons Each story quietly shows one of the program’s five ideas, like checking your battery before you fix things, without a lecture in sight. Your place in each episode is remembered in this browser only, so you can pick up where you left off. Nothing you type or choose is sent anywhere.","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-framework-in-depth.html","t":"The Frequency Framework in depth","h":"The Frequency Framework","d":"How any relationship runs on five different rhythms, and the beat frequency that creates friction when they drift apart.","s":["You're here in The Objective Ledger","The Radio Frequency Paradigm","See It in Motion","The Five Frequencies That Matter","🔵 Financial Frequency","🔴 Autonomic Frequency","🟢 Decisional Frequency","🟡 Communicational Frequency","🟣 Philosophical Frequency","Beat Frequency: Where the Squeal Comes From","What's Next?"],"x":"You've learned that every relationship runs on several rhythms at once, each at its own speed. This page goes deeper: what exactly runs on each rhythm? Financial rhythm: Bills, paychecks, budgets Autonomic rhythm: How your nervous systems reset Decisional rhythm: How fast you both can commit Communicational rhythm: How often you need check-ins Philosophical rhythm: How often you check that your values still line up Next step: After understanding the five domains, you can measure your own rhythms with the Frequency Calibration tool. Saturday morning You want to decide about the holiday now, while you are both awake and it is in front of you. They want to think about it for a few days. Neither of you is stalling or rushing. You just run at different speeds. Why every relationship runs on five different rhythms, and how to bring the ones that matter most back in step. Use With Before this: Chapter I: The Radio Frequency Paradigm Understand the frequency principle At the same time: Mood Arbitrage (Free) See frequencies in action during conflict After this: Frequency Calibration 🔒 Measure your own rhythms Frequencies Covered Financial Autonomic Decisional Communicational Philosophical Tune two radios to frequencies that are close but not identical, and you get a squeal: the audible beat created by two signals almost, but not quite, in sync. This program borrows that image for a familiar moment: two people each say something reasonable, in a tone shaped by their own tiredness or urgency, and end up in a fight neither of them meant to start. Every relationship runs on several overlapping rhythms. A scientist would call each one an oscillator: something that repeats on a regular cycle. Money runs on one cycle (bills due every two weeks, paychecks every other Friday). Your nervous systems run on another: under stress, one person may be ready to reset in 90 seconds, while the other needs hours. Decisions, communication and values each have their own natural rhythm too. When those rhythms don't line up, they create a beat frequency : a pattern of interference that shows up as friction, conflict and feeling out of sync. The good news is that the mismatch isn't a sign you're incompatible. It's information. Once you both know your rhythms, you can make simple agreements that bring them back in step. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar IV, Tune how you send and re","f":1},{"u":"/frequency-framework.html","t":"The Frequency Framework: Getting Back in Step","h":"The Frequency Framework","d":"Two people can each run on their own rhythm for money, rest, decisions, talking and values. Why kind people fall out of step, and how to find the beat again.","s":["Two kind, reasonable people can still get crossed wires.","There are five rhythms to know.","The bumpiness lives in the gap, not in either person.","You don’t have to change your speed. Agree on when you’ll meet."],"x":"Two people can each run on their own rhythm for money, rest, decisions, talking and values. When those rhythms don’t line up, things can feel bumpy. Knowing them turns the bumps into something you can plan around. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , part of the Five Pillars . In you: know your own rhythms for money, rest, decisions, talking and values. Between you and others: agree on when and how you meet in the middle. A mismatch is a tuning problem, not a moral failing. Saturday morning You want to decide about the holiday now, while you are both awake and it is in front of you. They want to think about it for a few days. Neither of you is stalling or rushing. You just run at different speeds. Tune two radios close to, but not exactly on, the same station and you hear static. Two people each saying something reasonable, at their own pace, can make the same noise. Dig deeper: the radio picture Money: when bills and pay land. Rest: how long each of you needs to settle after stress. Decisions: minutes or weeks. Talking: how often you need to check in. Values: how often you want to ask if you are still headed the same way. Dig deeper: the five frequencies, with examples The bigger the gap, the louder the static. One of you wants to talk right after a hard day. The other needs an hour of quiet. One feels left alone, the other feels rushed. Nobody is wrong. The rhythms just don’t match. Dig deeper: watch the gap grow What helps is a simple agreement about when, how and how often you catch up. You’re not incompatible. Your rhythms are just out of step. Dig deeper: agreeing when you’ll catch up Try this: Pick the one rhythm where you feel most out of step, and each say how much time you need. Then agree on a time to meet in the middle. See the rhythms at work in a disagreement with Mood Arbitrage →","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-journey-play.html","t":"Play The Frequency Journey","h":"The Frequency Journey","d":"Guide Sugarfoot and Tidbit through seven gentle, tone-themed worlds to find the Perfect Frequency.","s":["Journey map"],"x":"What is this? A calm journey through seven tone-themed worlds with Sugarfoot and Tidbit, all the way up to the Perfect Frequency. Every level is a different small challenge: riddles, puzzles, breathing, sorting, a little singing. No timer, and no way to lose. About the journey The Frequency Journey How to play Every level is a different challenge. Eighteen of them across the first six worlds, each one fitted to its world: lantern riddles, a walk through the dark, a slow breath, thawing words, ice and water, putting steps in order, a feelings memory match, kinder words, a meadow to bloom, spotting small reaches, sharing the load fairly, bridge plates, echoing a tune, sorting fact, feeling and ask, finishing a line, telling what you saw from the story you added, choosing what would help most, and guiding a friend in the dark. They grow a little harder as you climb. The leaves on each level (one to five) show how challenging it is: short and easy in the first world, more thoughtful at the summit. A new set each time you play. The riddles, words, scenes, sentences and questions are drawn fresh from big pools every time, skipping ones you’ve already seen on this device; the meadow, the tune and the dark path are made new each play, and the walking levels pick one of several boards. Each set is spread across the Five Pillars, with things to notice in yourself and between you and other people. Replay a level you’ve finished and it leans a little more thoughtful. There’s no way to lose. A wrong answer just gets a gentle why and another try. There are no lives or hearts to run out. Hint and Start again (or Restart on walking levels) are always there. Walking levels: bring Sugarfoot (the one with the white feet) and Tidbit (the one with the mask) to the glowing tone together. Swipe, use the arrow pad or arrow keys (or W A S D). Switch (E) chooses who moves and Together (T) moves both. Undo is Z, Restart is R and Hint is H. Everything works by tap or keyboard. Tab to any button; type letters for thawing words, press 1 to 5 to sing the crystals, and plan calls in the dark with the arrow keys and Enter. After each level comes a small lesson card, with a link to read more on this site. At the very top, choose the three lessons that meant the most to you, and they become stars in the Infinite Sky, the seventh world: a place to rest and play. The tones are used here as calm","f":0},{"u":"/frequency-journey.html","t":"The Frequency Journey: A Calm Puzzle Adventure","h":"The Frequency Journey: a calm puzzle adventure","d":"Help Tidbit and Sugarfoot find the Perfect Frequency through seven worlds of calm puzzles, riddles and small lessons about patience, kindness and harmony.","s":["Meet the pals","Tidbit","Sugarfoot","Seven worlds, one song","The Whispering Shadows","The Melting Glaciers","The Golden Meadow","The Bridge of Echoes","The Singing Valleys","The Starry Summit","The Infinite Sky","Frequency Buddies","How the journey works","Ready when you are"],"x":"A calm puzzle journey Somewhere above the clouds is the Perfect Frequency. Tidbit and Sugarfoot are going to find it, together. ▶ Begin the journey See the seven worlds Two best friends, each wonderful in her own way. They get there as themselves, and they get there side by side. The one with the mask A black mask with little tan eyebrow dots, and a grin you can see from across the garden. She’s quick, curious and always a step ahead, keeps an eye on her pal, and is first in line for a high five. Underneath all that zoom is a big heart of gold. The one with the white feet Soft floppy ears, sweet white feet (that’s where her name comes from) and a slow, happy wag. She’s steady, brave in the dark and the best hugger there is, with a big heart of gold, and she’s happiest right beside Tidbit. Each world is tuned to its own tone, with its own kind of challenge and its own little lesson. The climb gets gently harder as you go. A stormy forest where the path only shows when the pals stay close. It’s easier to be brave next to someone you trust. Old ice gives way to new streams, and frozen paths become ways through. Letting go of the old way can open a new one. Flowers bloom wherever they run, and little lights wait to be gathered. Kindness to yourself makes room for joy. A canyon where Tidbit and Sugarfoot take separate paths and help each other across. What one of us does changes the way for the other. Crystals hum and wind chimes sing, and clarity comes from listening. Say it clearly and kindly, and it can be heard. A night climb over constellations, where not everything is what it seems. Look again: what you can see is not always what you assumed. The top of the journey: an open sky to rest in, where the pals place stars and simply be, together. The Perfect Frequency was the two of you, in tune. An animated adventure series starring Tidbit and Sugarfoot: mini movies of about 16 to 17 minutes, each in a new world, each with a real tough time the pals work through together. Captions are always on. Season 1: The First Adventures. 1. The Storm Over the Treehouse. 2. Out of Tune. 3. The Heavy Basket. 4. Who Broke the Kite? 5. The Longest Night. Watch Frequency Buddies . Open the Frequency Buddies player → A new challenge every level Riddles, little life lessons, sorting, memory, melodies, logic and path puzzles, each one different from the last. No timers, no losing ","f":0},{"u":"/full-path.html","t":"The Full Path Package and Report","h":"The full path package and your report","d":"One fillable PDF with every workpaper for your road, and a detailed report written from your answers: findings, things worth a second look, recommendations.","s":["How it works","What the report gives you","Start with your road"],"x":"One fillable PDF with every workpaper for your situation, and a detailed report written from your own answers. Use it on your own, or with 2 to 8 people: a partner, family, co-parent, friends, roommates, a team, or the people sharing care. Choose your road. Individual, Partners, Family, Co-parents, Friends, Roommates, Coworkers or Caregivers. Each road has the workpapers that fit it, in a sensible order, worded for that kind of relationship. Download one PDF. A cover and how-to, who’s on the road, every workpaper, the numbers for “Is the setup working for everyone?”, optional self-notes (your Wiring Card and a weather and battery log) and a “Ready for your report” page. It works in any PDF app and prints well. Fill it in over a few weeks. Some pages take five minutes; a week-long log takes a week. Each person fills in their own parts, about themselves. Bring it back, or type it in. Upload the filled PDF and the site shows you what it read and anything missing, so you can fix it. Or fill in the same steps right on the site. You can save a small backup file and pick up later. Read your report together, or on your own. On screen or as a PDF, at a calm time, with your batteries checked first. A plain-language summary of the whole picture, with headline scores and what they mean, strengths to protect, and the top few things to work on. It also says how complete your answers were, so you know how much weight to give it. Findings for every workpaper , from your real numbers: who carries which kinds of work (including the invisible kind), jobs with no clear owner, each person’s battery and biggest stressors, patterns that keep coming back, and how steady your check-ins have been. Each section also says what it can’t tell you. Connections across workpapers , like hours that look even while the mental load doesn’t, or tense days that line up with high-load days. Things worth a second look : numbers that don’t add up, answers that seem to disagree, a missing week. Gently worded, because it may be a typo or it may be real. Recommendations in order (now, this week, this month), each with the reason, a first step, words you could use, the right tool, and how you’ll know it’s working. Plus a 4 to 6 week plan built from them. A kind view for each person , never a ranking. On the Individual road, a deeper look at your own wiring, patterns and conditions, plus a short way to ","f":0},{"u":"/glossary.html","t":"Glossary: The Words, in Plain English","h":"Glossary: the words, in plain English","d":"Every word this site uses, from battery to talk window, in one plain sentence with an example, plus each tool’s everyday name beside its technical one.","s":["Words used on this site","📻 Static","🎚️ Frequency","🔧 Retune","〰️ Drift","🌊 Carrier wave","🔌 Wiring","🧩 Wired differently","🪪 Wiring Card","⛅ Today’s Weather","🪟 Talk window","🌡️ Pressure","📔 Your 7-day log","🔋 Battery","⚖️ The load","🧾 Unbilled debt","🙋 Owner (one owner per job)","👥 Person A and Person B","🗓️ Check-in","🫧 Calm-Down Kit","💦 Flooded","✋ A bid","🤝 Turning toward","🩹 Repair"],"x":"The words this site uses, each in one plain sentence with an example. Below them, every tool’s everyday name beside the technical name you may see on the Full pages. One plain sentence for each, with an example. Tap Wade in for a step-by-step explanation, or Listen to hear it. Find a word Spelling doesn’t need to be perfect. Static is the crackle between two people when the words are fine but the tone, pace or timing doesn’t match. For example: You ask “When will you do it?” calmly. They hear pressure. Nobody meant harm, but it crackles. Chapter I: why we get out of tune Your frequency is your natural pace: how fast you decide, talk, reply and recover. For example: One person answers a text in a minute; the other answers at the end of the day. Neither is wrong. The Frequency Framework To retune is to notice you’ve fallen out of step with someone and adjust together: slower, softer, or at a better time. For example: “Can we start that again? I came in too fast.” Chapter I Drift is how two people slowly fall out of step when nobody checks in, even when nothing is wrong. For example: Three busy weeks, no real talk, and small things start to feel big. The 90-second daily check-in The carrier wave is the feeling that travels underneath the words: the tone that arrives before the meaning does. For example: “Fine.” can carry calm, hurt or tiredness. The carrier wave tells you which. The Carrier Wave Decoder Your wiring is the way your brain and senses take in words, sound, change and people. It stays the same from day to day. For example: Some people need a plan in writing. Others think best out loud. Know your own wiring Being wired differently means your brain works in a less common way, such as being autistic, ADHD, dyslexic or highly sensitive, which changes how words land. For example: A hint like “the bins are full” may be heard as a plain fact, not a request. Wired Differently A Wiring Card is a one-page card you make that tells others how you receive words best, what silence means for you, and what to avoid. For example: “Please send big news in writing first. If I go quiet, I’m thinking, not upset.” Make your Wiring Card Today’s Weather is a one-minute check on your own conditions today, like sleep, stress and energy: Clear means calm and connected, Gusty means revved up, and Fogged in means running on empty. For example: Short night, busy day: the forecas","f":0},{"u":"/grandparents.html","t":"For Grandparents: Help Without Taking Over","h":"For grandparents: how to help with the grandkids without taking over","d":"Free, gentle help for grandparents: share childcare fairly, agree on house rules with your adult children, say no kindly, and stay close to your grandkids.","s":["Who this page is for","Check your own tank before a busy visit","Share the load when you help with childcare","Talk about house rules without a fight","Words for common moments","Offering help without taking over","Saying no to extra babysitting, kindly","Raising a worry about a grandchild with their parent","When the grandkids push limits at your house","When you hear “you spoil them”","Be a calm place for the grandkids","Stay close from far away","Care for your own energy","Ask Professor Puddles","Questions grandparents often ask","Whose rules apply when I look after the grandkids?","How do I say no to extra babysitting without hurting anyone?","What if my grown child says I spoil the grandkids?","How can I stay close to grandkids who live far away?","Is this site free for grandparents?"],"x":"Helping with the grandkids is one of the biggest gifts in a family. It goes best when everyone knows who decides what, and how much help is really being asked for. This page gathers the free tools on this site that help with that. It is for grandparents, and for great-aunts, great-uncles and other older relatives who often care for children in the family. Where this fits: This page draws on Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person , and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , part of the Five Pillars . Most family friction is not about love. It is about rules nobody said out loud, and help nobody measured. Maybe you pick the kids up from school twice a week. Maybe they stay with you every summer. Maybe you live far away and see them mostly on a screen. Any of these counts. The ideas here are simple. Know how full your own tank is. Agree out loud on who does what. Talk with your grown children as one adult to another. And keep your time with the grandkids calm and kind. Nobody here is to blame, in either generation. You are all doing your best with different rulebooks. A long day with little ones takes a lot of energy. Before they arrive, take one minute to ask yourself how you are really doing today. If you are already tired, that is useful to know. You can plan a quieter day, or ask for a shorter one. Today’s Weather A one-minute check on how you are doing today, with a gentle forecast. The Calm-Down Kit Make a small plan before you need it: your early signs, a pause signal, and what helps you settle. Help often starts small. One afternoon a week becomes three. Then it is school runs, lunches, sick days and summer. Nobody planned it, and nobody talked about it. That is how help quietly turns into an unspoken full-time job. The fix is not to help less. It is to write the jobs down, so everyone can see them. The Lemonade Stand Note who did what this week, and see the split as a plain picture, with no blame. WP-03, One owner per job Give each regular job one owner: the school run, packed lunches, school forms. WP-01, Who did what, and kind ways to say no A one-week log, plus three calm ways to say “not right now.” A clear yes is easier to keep than a vague “always.” Say which days you can do, and until what time. Screen time, sweets, bedtime, manners. These are the usual sore spots. A simple rule helps: their house, their rules; your house, a few gentle one","f":0},{"u":"/growing-up-in-depth.html","t":"Where Your Lens Came From in depth","h":"Where your lens came from: how growing up shapes the way you see yourself, others and what you expect of them","d":"A plain-language guide to the stages of growing up and the lens each one leaves: the big question at each stage, why timing matters, and family and culture.","s":["An honest note before you read on","The lens idea: understandings we carry forward","About yourself","About other people","About what others owe you","The stages: a big question at each age","Why timing matters: the same event lands differently at different ages","What makes an experience land harder","What we carry forward, and how it shows up","How the world shapes the lens","When the lens is wrong about you","When the lens is wrong about other people","The expectations we place on others","The rulebook from home","A lens check for the moment","Saying it to someone else","Doing this together","How this uses the Five Pillars","Going deeper: the research, in plain words","Questions people often ask","Isn’t this just blaming my parents?","I don’t remember much from when I was little. Can I still use this?","If it happened early, is it permanent?","What if my partner won’t look at their rulebook?"],"x":"Every stage of growing up asks a big question, and the answer we land on can become an understanding many people carry forward and keep using as adults. Most of those understandings serve us well. Some were right for the child we were and quietly out of date for the adult we are now. Going gently. This guide looks back at growing up. You can read one section and stop, and come back another day. The “Jump to a section” links below take you straight to the part you want. Not every home felt calm or kind. If “they were doing their best” doesn’t fit your story, that’s okay. Take only what is useful to you and leave the rest. Sunday, 10:15am Your partner is still in bed and the kitchen is a mess. Before you’ve had a single thought, you feel it: a hot, tight “Of course. It’s always me.” Nobody said that. Your partner may not even know the dishes are there. But in the house you grew up in, the person who noticed the mess was the person who cleaned it, and anyone who rested while there was work to do was lazy. That rule comes from your childhood, and it just made a decision for you. We like to think we see the world as it is. Mostly, we see it through a lens we started grinding very early, long before we could choose. Family, school, friends, culture, faith communities, the money situation at home, the shows we watched and, more and more, the screens in our hands all taught us what to expect: of ourselves, of other people, and of how a “good” partner, parent, friend or coworker behaves. By adulthood, that lens feels less like a lens and more like simple reality. This guide is about taking the lens off for a moment and looking at it. Where did each part come from? How old were you? Is it still true here, with these people, now? You can’t choose what shaped you. You can choose, a little at a time, which understandings you keep using. Where this fits: This page mainly uses Pillar III, Read your state first (is this feeling the size of the moment, or older?) and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (what we were rewarded for as children keeps pulling on us), with help from Pillar I, See the whole load , Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person , and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive . In you: notice when an old understanding is doing the thinking. Between you and others: say out loud the rules you each brought from home, and choose your own together. This page i","f":1},{"u":"/growing-up.html","t":"Where Your Lens Came From: How Growing Up Shapes You","h":"Where your lens came from: how growing up shapes the way you see yourself and others","d":"How growing up shapes the adult you are: the big question at each stage, why timing makes some experiences go deeper, and how to choose which old rules to keep.","s":["You see the world through a lens you didn’t choose.","Each stage of growing up asks a different question.","When something happened matters.","Some of what you believe about yourself was learned when you were a child and didn’t have all the facts.","The lens colors how you read the people you love.","An expectation nobody said out loud can’t be agreed to, so the other person can only miss it.","Write down the rulebook from home.","A one-minute lens check.","Two rulebooks, one home: write a new one together.","Roots & Wings: where your traits may have started","Old understanding, fairer reading","Inside you, and between you"],"x":"Going gently. This page looks back at growing up. You can read one card and stop, and come back another day. Nothing here needs finishing. Not every home felt calm or kind. If “they were doing their best” doesn’t fit your story, that’s okay. Take only what is useful to you and leave the rest. Every stage of growing up asks a big question: Is the world safe? Am I capable? Where do I belong? The answers we land on can become understandings many people keep using as adults, usually without noticing. Together they make up your lens. Most of these understandings serve us well. Some were right for the child we were and are out of date for the adult we are now. They can change. Where this fits: This page mainly uses Pillar III, Read your state first , and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives , with help from Pillar I , Pillar II and Pillar IV . They are all part of the Five Pillars . In you: notice when an old understanding is doing the thinking. Between you and others: say out loud the rules you each brought from home, and choose your own together. Try it: find where a trait started. Roots & Wings asks a few gentle tap questions about each stage of your life, from the early years up to now. Then it shows where a trait may have started, how to notice it, and one small way to tackle it this week. Every question can be skipped. Start Roots & Wings ↓ Sunday, 10:15am Your partner is still in bed and the kitchen is a mess. Before you’ve had a single thought, you feel it: “Of course. It’s always me.” Nobody said that. But in the house you grew up in, whoever noticed the mess cleaned it, and anyone resting while there was work to do was lazy. That rule comes from your childhood, and it just made a decision for you. Family, school, friends, culture, money at home, what you watched and what’s on your phone all taught you what to expect: of yourself, of other people, and of how a “good” partner, parent or friend behaves. By adulthood your lens doesn’t feel like a lens. It feels like simply seeing. Dig deeper: the lens idea Researchers call these developmental stages. The ages are rough, and people move at their own pace: First years (about 0 to 1) “Is the world safe? Will someone come?” Toddler years (about 2 to 3) “Can I do it myself?” Early childhood (about 4 to 5) “Is it okay to start things and take up space?” School years (about 6 to 11) “Am I capable?” Teen years (abo","f":0},{"u":"/how-it-works-in-depth.html","t":"How It Works in depth — An Outside Lens on Yourself","h":"An outside lens on yourself","d":"How The Objective Ledger works in full: an outside look at your life situation, your wiring and what you discover, and why the technical layer is optional.","s":["Your life situation","Your neurological wiring","What you discover","Plain first. Technical only if you want it.","Where the static comes from","What it should feel like"],"x":"What the program actually does, in plain language: the three things it looks at, why the technical parts are optional, and where the static between two people comes from. Most of us only see our lives from the inside, through the mood we're in, the history we carry, and the way our brain happens to be wired. The Objective Ledger gives you a view from outside: a calm, structured way to look at yourself as an honest auditor would, with no agenda except what's true and no verdict on your worth. It borrows an auditor's habit: write down what actually happened, in plain terms, before deciding what it means. Then it turns that habit gently inward, onto the three things that shape nearly every reaction you have. What are you carrying right now? A new baby, a job change, a move, an illness, caring for a parent, or grief. Life's biggest transitions change how much you can hold, often before you notice. Seeing your load as it truly is lets you stop blaming yourself, or anyone else, for what is really just the season you're in. WP-02: Battery & Stress Meter WP-04: Deficit Audit How is your nervous system built? Every brain handles stress, noise, change and conversation differently. Some people reset in minutes and others need hours. Some think out loud and others need quiet. Whether you're neurotypical (wired like most people) or neurodivergent (wired differently), your wiring isn't a flaw to hide. It's information to understand, and then to explain to the people you live and work with. Today’s Weather Wired Differently Wiring Card Signal Translator Chapter III Frequency Calibration What patterns show up when you look? Written down over a few weeks, patterns appear: the time of day you get short-tempered, the task that always slips, the conversation that always goes sideways. Seen from the outside, they stop feeling like character flaws and start looking like problems you can solve. WP-01: Field Audit REPORT-01 Stories from Philosophy The program has some technical parts, drawn from nervous-system science, accounting and even radio physics. You never need them. Each section puts understanding first: the idea in everyday language, with an example you'll recognize. The technical layer comes after, for anyone who enjoys the \"why\" behind the \"what.\" Skip it entirely and the program is just as useful. The tools work the same, and everything you learn about yourself is just ","f":1},{"u":"/how-it-works.html","t":"How It Works: An Outside Look at Yourself","h":"An outside lens on yourself","d":"How the program works: a calm, outside view of your life situation, your wiring and what you notice, like an honest auditor with no verdict on your worth.","s":["Your life situation changes how much you can hold.","Your wiring is information, not a flaw.","Patterns show up when you write things down.","The static lives between two people.","The technical parts are optional.","A few entries tell you more than one."],"x":"The program gives you a calm view of yourself from the outside, like a kind, neutral friend with no judgment on your worth. It looks gently at your situation, your wiring and what you discover. Where this fits: The whole program rests on the Five Pillars : see the whole load , fix the setup, not the person , read your state first , tune how you send and receive , and notice the quiet incentives . Each one starts inside you and then shows up between you and other people. A new baby, a move, an illness or grief can shrink what you can carry before you notice. Seeing your load clearly helps you go easier on yourself, and on everyone else, in the season you're in. Dig deeper: your life situation Every brain handles stress, noise and conversation differently. Some people reset in minutes, others need hours. Knowing yours helps you explain it to the people around you. Dig deeper: your wiring Over a few weeks you may spot the time of day you get snappy, or the task that always slips. Seen from a little distance, those patterns look like things you can work on. Dig deeper: what you discover You say “in a minute,” meaning “when I finish this.” They hear “sixty seconds.” Nobody did anything wrong. Knowing your own signal well enough to say it out loud helps close the gap. Dig deeper: where the static comes from Each idea comes first in everyday words. The science behind it is there if you want it. Skip it and the tools work just the same. Dig deeper: plain first, technical if you want it This is a practical guide, not a clinical tool. Because you fill it in yourself, your mood comes along too. One entry is a mood. Twelve entries are a pattern. Dig deeper: an honest note Try this: Take one minute to check your own conditions today. Check today’s weather →","f":0},{"u":"/how-to-stop-fighting-with-your-partner.html","t":"How to Stop Fighting With Your Partner: 7 Calm Steps","h":"How to stop fighting with your partner: 7 calm steps","d":"Practical, calm ways to stop fighting with your partner: notice the early signs, take a real pause, check your words, and fix the setup behind repeat fights.","s":["Why couples keep having the same fight","7 calm steps to stop fighting","How to say no without a fight","If you fight over text","Small daily habits that prevent fights","Questions people often ask","Is it normal for couples to fight?","How long should a pause last?","What if my partner won’t take a break?","What if we always fight about chores?","Is this relationship counseling?","Keep going"],"x":"To stop fighting with your partner, catch the argument early, take a real pause with a time to come back, and let your body settle before you talk again. Then say one fact, one feeling and one ask, listen until your partner feels heard, and fix the setup behind the fights that keep coming back. Most repeat fights between kind people are not about love. They are about tired bodies, mixed signals and jobs that nobody clearly owns. Wednesday, 9:40 p.m. It started with “did you call the landlord?” Ten minutes later you are both talking about something that happened last spring. Neither of you can remember how you got here. Both of you feel misunderstood. Sound familiar? You are not alone, and there is a calmer way through. Most couples don’t have hundreds of different fights. They have the same three or four, over and over. Usually one of these is underneath: Full tanks. When you are tired, hungry or stressed, small things land harder. The reaction is real, but part of it belongs to the day, not to your partner. Chapter III calls this a full stress tank. Out of tune. One of you talks fast and wants to fix it now. The other needs time to think. One hears a question as a complaint. Chapter I explains how small mismatches in pace and tone add up. No owner. The same chore keeps slipping because it belongs to “whoever notices.” The fight is about the dishes, but the real problem is the setup. The good news: all three can be changed without either of you becoming a different person. Notice the early signs. A tight jaw, a faster voice, the urge to say “you always.” Learn your own signs and your partner’s. The earlier you catch it, the easier it is to turn. Call a real pause, with a time to come back. Agree on a pause word ahead of time, like “timeout” or “let’s park it.” Always add when you will come back: “Can we pick this up at eight?” A pause with a return time is not walking away. It is keeping the talk safe. Calm down first. Breathe slowly, take a short walk, have a glass of water, or sit somewhere quiet for twenty minutes. The Calm-Down Kit (free with an email sign-up) helps you plan what works for you before you need it. The Night Garden breathing game is a gentle place to slow your breath. Check how your words will land. Before you go back in, try your opening sentence in the Signal Translator . It shows how a message may sound to the other person, and a kinder","f":0},{"u":"/index.html","t":"Spread Love & Acceptance: Free Relationship Self-Help Tools","h":"Free relationship self-help tools Understand yourself, then share the load kindly.","d":"Free self-help for love and acceptance at home: share the mental load, a fair chore chart, calm ways to stop fighting, a communication quiz and a kids’ cartoon.","s":["Where to start","Start where you are","Popular guides","See yourself from the outside, then share the load kindly","New here? See if this is right for you","Where to go next","Open everything free"],"x":"Spread Love & Acceptance Free tools and a short book that help you understand yourself and share the load kindly at home. Made for couples families roommates parents How much time do you have? Start in 10 minutes One short, guided path. No sign-up. Find your Wavelength How you think, talk and listen Read the book Start with the Preface: the work nobody sees For kids Frequency Buddies Calm down now Night Garden Not sure? Start here Sent a link? What it means New Pause & Play is here: calm games for a busy mind, including the Night Garden, the Frequency Journey, Word Bloom, two crosswords and Quiet Words. There are no timers and no way to lose. Every few levels, something new appears in the garden behind every page. See all the calm games → New Wired Differently and the Signal Translator are here. Wired Differently explains how people with different kinds of wiring (neurotypes) can hear the same words differently. The Signal Translator is a free tool that shows how a phrase might land for someone wired differently from you. Nothing you type is sent to us. Open the translator → More Less What it does Choose how each person is wired: one neurotype or a mix (autistic, ADHD, dyslexic, anxious, highly sensitive, neurotypical and more). Then type a phrase. The translator marks where static (misunderstanding) may come from. It shows how the phrase may land, what the speaker probably meant, and a clearer way to say it. Wired Differently is the guide behind it, with plenty of examples and the research it draws on. Your data stays with you No account and no upload. The translator runs entirely in your browser and saves nothing. You don't have to read anything in order. Tap whatever is closest to true right now, and we'll point you to the two or three pages that help most with that. Invisible labor and the mental load : what it is and how to share it A fair chore chart for couples : free and printable How to stop fighting with your partner : seven calm steps Neurodivergent relationships : tips for ADHD and autistic couples and families Communication style quiz : find your Wave Code Frequency Buddies : a free kids’ cartoon about feelings The idea behind it all The Objective Ledger , the method behind everything here, is a way of seeing yourself from the outside. It gives you a fair, neutral view of how three things shape the way you think, feel and react: your life situat","f":0},{"u":"/infographic.html","t":"The Whole Idea on One Printable Page","h":"Understand yourself first. Then share the load kindly.","d":"The whole idea on one printable page: the Five Pillars, how an unowned chore turns into resentment, the Lemonade Stand chore tracker, and three kind phrases.","s":["01 The Five Pillars","See the whole load","Fix the setup, not the person","Read your state first","Tune how you send and receive","Notice the quiet incentives","02 How an unowned job turns into resentment","A job nobody owns shows up","Someone quietly takes it on","Nobody counts it","The feeling outlasts the facts","It spills out somewhere else","03 The Lemonade Stand","04 Three kind ways to say no","“I’m full today”","“Not me, but let’s find who”","“Yes, just later”"],"x":"The Objective Ledger The whole idea on one page The whole idea on one page: five simple ideas, how an unowned job turns into resentment, the Lemonade Stand, and three kind ways to say no. Five simple ideas sit under everything here. Each one starts inside you, then shows up between you and other people. You see the load (I), redesign the setup around it (II), check your state before you talk (III), tune the message so it lands (IV), and notice the quiet incentives that let things slide back (V). I In you: notice everything you carry, even the invisible load. Between you: put it on one shared, fair page. II In you: see habits as a setup you can redesign, not a character flaw. Between you: clear owners and agreements instead of blame. III In you: know if you're calm and connected, revved up, or running on empty before you judge a moment. Between you: pick the timing, pause and come back. IV In you: know your own wiring, pace and tone. Between you: a mismatch is a tuning problem, not a moral one. V In you: spot the defaults that steer your choices. Between you: see how an unclaimed task quietly becomes one person's permanent job. Each idea borrows from a field that describes how things work without blaming anyone: ledger accounting, systems thinking, the science of stress and calm, signal theory and behavioral economics. See how each pillar ties to the course → When nobody names who owns a job, it quietly grows into resentment, in five steps. On the Full pages this is called the unbilled-work cycle. 01 Something needs doing, and no one has explicitly agreed to own it. 02 Whoever notices first, cares most, or tolerates least just quietly does it. 03 No one logs the hour, so no one sees the running total grow. 04 The feeling outlives any memory of which specific tasks actually caused it. 05 A small, unrelated moment lets out months of uncounted work all at once. ↻ Without a named owner, the cycle starts again at Step 01. The shared register exists to stop it there. Two people run a household the way two people might run a lemonade stand: shared costs, shared hours, shared profit. The difference is that most households never write any of it down. The Lemonade Stand rule of thumb says the fix isn't more effort from whoever is already doing the most. It's a shared register both people can see. List every task and log every hour, in the same columns, at the same time","f":0},{"u":"/invisible-labor-mental-load.html","t":"Invisible Labor and the Mental Load: How to Share It","h":"Invisible labor and the mental load: what it is and how to share it","d":"What invisible labor and the mental load are, real examples from everyday home life, and five calm steps couples and families can use to share it fairly.","s":["What is the mental load?","What counts as invisible labor? Everyday examples","Why the mental load goes unseen, and why it leads to fights","How to share the mental load: five calm steps","Words that help when you bring it up","It isn’t only for couples","Questions people often ask","Is the mental load the same as household chores?","Is it always one person who carries the mental load?","How do I bring up the mental load without a fight?","Isn’t writing it all down just keeping score?","Do we need an app to share the mental load?","Keep going"],"x":"The mental load is the thinking work of running a home: noticing what needs doing, remembering it, planning it and checking that it got done. Invisible labor is any work that keeps life running but is easy to miss, because it happens in someone’s head, or before anyone else is up. You share it by making it visible first, then giving each job one owner who handles all of it, the thinking part included. Sunday, 8 p.m. Nobody asked anyone to check the school forms, notice the milk is low, book the dentist or remember that Grandma’s birthday is Thursday. One person did it anyway. Nobody saw it, so it didn’t count. That quiet gap is where a lot of resentment at home begins. Think of a chore in two parts. There is the doing part, like putting a load of laundry in. And there is the thinking part: noticing the basket is full, knowing the soccer uniform has to be clean by Saturday, remembering the detergent is nearly gone and adding it to the list. The doing part is easy to see. The thinking part is the mental load. It is real work. It takes time and attention, and it rarely stops, because a home never runs out of things to keep track of. When people say “I do most of the work at home,” they often mean the thinking part. When the other person says “but I help a lot,” they often mean the doing part. Both can be true at once. That is why this topic causes so many fights between kind people. Here are common jobs that tend to go unseen. Read the list together and notice who usually does each one. Remembering: birthdays, appointments, permission slips, bill due dates, when the car needs service, who is allergic to what. Planning: what’s for dinner this week, the grocery list, holiday travel, childcare when school is closed, who drives where. Noticing: the shampoo is running low, the kids have outgrown their shoes, the plant needs water, the smoke alarm is chirping. Checking: did the form get signed, did the payment go through, did someone call the plumber back. Keeping the peace: smoothing over a tense moment, reminding someone gently, keeping in touch with family, sending the thank-you note. Caring: tracking a parent’s medicines, checking in on a friend who is having a hard time, knowing which child needs extra attention this week. None of these look like “doing the dishes.” That is exactly why they are easy to miss, and why the person carrying them can feel tired in a w","f":0},{"u":"/is-this-for-you-in-depth.html","t":"Is This Right for You? In depth","h":"Is this right for you?","d":"What The Objective Ledger is and isn't, who it helps, how to get in free while it's being built, and a guide to every page on the site.","s":["What it is, and what it isn't","What it is","What it isn't","Who it's for","It's likely a good fit if","It's probably not the right tool right now if","New: The Carrier Wave Decoder","A guided tool for the moment a conversation starts to go wrong","Ways in","Open accessFree · no sign-up","Free with emailFree · email only","Paid membershipComing later","Where everything is","Start here","The book","Workpapers","Program & record","Tools","Try it out","Listen","About & status","Legal"],"x":"The Objective Ledger is a framework for self-discovery and for building and keeping healthy relationships of every kind. It takes a logical, objective look at some of life's biggest changes, and it starts with you. First, it helps you understand your own stress, rhythms and reactions, objectively and without shame. Then it gives any two people who share time, space, work or care a fair way to see what each person carries: the noticing, remembering, planning and smoothing things over that usually goes unseen. Those two people might be partners, family, friends, roommates, co-parents, coworkers or caregivers. Once that work is written down in the same units, where both people can see it, it stops being a matter of competing impressions. It becomes a shared fact you can talk about. This page will help you decide whether it fits your situation, show you how to get in free while it's being built, and walk you through where everything lives. To see how each chapter, workpaper and tool connects to each kind of relationship, read How it fits your relationships . Patience, clarity, and kindness over speed and stress. On this page: What it is and isn't Who it's for New: Carrier Wave Decoder Ways in Where everything is How it fits your relationships A path to self-discovery: understanding your own load, rhythms and reactions. A shared ledger for the unseen work in any relationship, so it gets written down. Worksheets each person fills in about themselves, then reads together. A common vocabulary for the moment a conversation starts to go out of sync. A practical rule of thumb (a heuristic) built from accounting and nervous-system ideas. It isn't clinically tested. A scorecard for proving who's right. A ledger only one person reads is just a grudge with better formatting. Therapy, counseling, or a diagnosis of anyone. A way for one person to monitor, rate or report on another, at home or at work. A way to assign blame. Every score describes the arrangement, never a person. You want to understand your own reactions better: why some moments hit harder than they should. The same arguments about chores, fairness or \"who does more\" keep coming back, with a partner, a parent, a roommate or a teammate. Someone feels like they're carrying work the other person can't see. Both people want things to be fairer, and each is willing to write down their own side honestly. Structure h","f":1},{"u":"/is-this-for-you.html","t":"Is This Right for You? What It Is and Isn’t","h":"Is this right for you?","d":"A free self-help program for your own stress and reactions, and a fair way for two people to see what each carries at home. Education, not counseling.","s":["It's a shared ledger, not a scorecard.","It fits when the same disagreements keep coming back.","Right now, all of it is free.","Every page is listed in one place."],"x":"The Objective Ledger helps you understand your own stress and reactions, then gives two people a fair way to see what each of you carries. This page helps you decide if it fits. Each person fills in worksheets about themselves, then you read them together. It isn't therapy, a diagnosis, or a way to prove who's right. Every score describes how things are shared, never a person. Dig deeper: what it is and isn't It helps when one person carries work the other can't see, and when you're both willing to write down your own side honestly. You can also start on your own. Dig deeper: who it's for The program is still being written. Some pages open with no sign-up, and the rest open once you enter your email. You won’t be charged anything right now. Dig deeper: ways in The full directory groups every live page by what it's for: the book, the worksheets, the tools and more. Dig deeper: where everything is Try this: Read how it fits the relationship on your mind, then pick one tool to start with. How it fits your relationships →","f":0},{"u":"/keepsakes.html","t":"My garden — your keepsakes from the calm games","h":"My garden","d":"Everything that has arrived in the garden behind the pages as you played the calm games, and the constellations you dedicated in the Night Garden.","s":["In the garden","In your night sky"],"x":"Everything that has arrived in the garden behind the pages while you played, and the constellations you named in the Night Garden. There is nothing to keep up and nothing to lose: this is just a place to look back on what you've grown. Kept on this device only. Your garden lives in this browser, and nothing here is sent anywhere. Turn a keepsake off and it rests out of sight in the background; turn it on again whenever you like. Back to Pause & Play →","f":0},{"u":"/know-yourself-in-depth.html","t":"Know Your Own Wiring in depth","h":"Know your own wiring: what’s you, what’s learned, and what can change","d":"A plain-language guide to why you react the way you do: lasting wiring, patterns shaped by life and hard experiences, and today’s conditions.","s":["An honest note before you read on","Three layers of “why I react this way”","1. Wiring","2. Shaped patterns","3. Today’s conditions","Layer one: wiring, the lasting part","Layer two: shaped patterns, the learned part","Layer three: today’s conditions, the passing part","Work on it, work with it, or work around it","Sorting common reactions","Where is this feeling coming from?","Body cues, in plain words","Explaining yourself to others","For wiring","For a pattern","For conditions","How much to share, with whom, and when","Helping someone else understand theirs","Matching frameworks for a calm frequency","How this uses the Five Pillars","Going deeper: what research says about temperament","Neurodiversity and the double empathy problem","How experiences shape our expectations","Learning, in the everyday sense"],"x":"Why you react the way you do, where a feeling is coming from, and how to explain it kindly to yourself and to the people you share a life with. Friday, 6:40pm “Surprise! I booked us dinner with my cousins tonight.” They meant it as a gift. You felt your chest tighten and heard yourself say “Great” in a flat voice. Later you wonder: is that just me, is it something from years ago, or am I simply wiped out after this week? Probably a bit of all three. Most of us were never taught to take our own reactions apart. We just react, and then either defend it (“That’s just how I am”) or blame ourselves (“What’s wrong with me?”). Neither helps much. This guide offers a third way: look at a reaction as a mix of three layers, work out which layer is doing most of the work, and then choose the right response for that layer. Once you can do that for yourself, you can explain it to someone else, and help them do the same. Where this fits: This page mainly uses Pillar III, Read your state first , and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , with help from Pillar I, See the whole load , and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person . They are all part of the Five Pillars . In you: know where a feeling is coming from before you act on it. Between you and others: tell people what’s lasting, what you’re working on and what today needs, so your ways of working can meet in a calm middle. This page is general education, not diagnosis, therapy or treatment. It can’t tell you whether you’re autistic, have ADHD or anything else, and it can’t tell you what someone else has. The three layers are a thinking tool, not a scientific category system. Real people are messier than any model, and the layers overlap. If you want to understand yourself more deeply, a qualified professional, such as a psychologist, counselor or doctor, can help, if and when that’s something you want. Jump to a section: Three layers Wiring Shaped patterns Conditions On, with, around Sorting table Where’s it from? Explaining yourself Helping others Meeting in the middle Five Pillars Temperament research Neurodiversity How experience shapes us Where your lens came from Hard experiences How change happens Common mix-ups Questions Limits THE CORE IDEA Think of any reaction, from a flash of irritation to going quiet for an hour, as a sound coming through a radio. Some of the sound is the radio itself: how it was built","f":1},{"u":"/know-yourself.html","t":"Know Your Own Wiring: A Self-Discovery Guide","h":"Know your own wiring: what’s you, what’s learned, and what can change","d":"Why you react the way you do: what is your wiring, what life taught you and what is just today, plus kind words to explain what helps to the people around you.","s":["Layer one: your wiring. This part stays.","Layer two: patterns life taught you. Many of these can soften.","Layer three: today’s conditions. These pass.","Work on it, work with it, or work around it.","Where is this feeling coming from?","Explain yourself without over-sharing.","Help others understand theirs with questions, not labels.","Meet in the middle, in the right order.","A quick sorting table","Inside you, and between you"],"x":"“Why do I react this way?” Most reactions come from three places at once: how you’re built, what life has taught you, and what kind of day you’re having. Knowing which is which helps you understand yourself, and explain yourself to the people around you. Where this fits: This page mainly uses Pillar III, Read your state first , and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , with help from Pillar I and Pillar II . They are all part of the Five Pillars . In you: know where a feeling is coming from before you act on it. Between you and others: tell people what’s lasting, what you’re working on and what today needs, so you can meet in the middle. Friday, 6:40pm “Surprise! I booked us dinner with my cousins tonight.” They meant it as a gift. You felt your chest tighten and heard yourself say “Great” in a flat voice. Later you wonder: is that just me, is it something from years ago, or am I simply wiped out after this week? Probably a bit of all three. Your temperament, your sensory makeup, your pace, and how you take in words and feelings. For some people that includes being autistic, having ADHD or being highly sensitive. Wiring isn’t a flaw to fix. You can understand it, plan around it and get support for it, through routines, tools, a better setup and, if you choose, help from a qualified professional. But it will still be there, and that’s fine. Dig deeper: wiring and temperament Family, culture, school, old relationships, past jobs and hard experiences can leave “settings” behind: an alarm that goes off early, a habit of pleasing people, going quiet, or bracing for the worst. They made sense once. With awareness, safety and practice, and sometimes a counselor, many of them ease over time. Some leave a lasting sensitivity that you learn to carry and explain. When it happened matters. Each stage of growing up asks its own big question (Is the world safe? Am I capable? Where do I belong?), and something that touched the question you were answering at the time tends to go deeper. That’s also where many of our expectations of ourselves and other people come from. Where your lens came from walks through it. Dig deeper: how experiences shape us Sleep, stress, hunger, the load you’re carrying, the noise in the room, the time of day. This is the layer that changes fastest, and the easiest one to fix. Take a reading with Today’s Weather or the How much are you carryin","f":0},{"u":"/learn/index-in-depth.html","t":"Stories from Philosophy in depth","h":"Stories from Philosophy","d":"Twelve real stories from philosophy on knowing yourself and living well with others, retold in plain language and linked to the chapters and worksheets.","s":["Part one: Knowing yourself","The Second Arrow","The Ship of Theseus","What Is Up to Us","Part two: Seeing the other person","The Cave","The Blind Men and the Elephant","The Joy of Fish","The Empty Boat","Part three: Living together","The Porcupines","Three Kinds of Friendship","The Circles of Hierocles","Part four: The ethics of keeping a ledger","The Ring of Gyges","I and Thou","Read the originals"],"x":"Reading People have been working on the questions behind this program for thousands of years: how to know yourself, how to see another person clearly, how to share a life fairly, and how to keep the accounting from replacing the person. Here are twelve stories and ideas from Greek, Buddhist, Chinese, Stoic, German and Jewish thought. Each one is retold briefly in plain language, followed by what it shows, where it lives in the program, and one small thing to try. They follow the program's own path: from yourself, to the other person, to life together, to the ethics of keeping a ledger at all. Each story also lines up with one of the program’s Five Pillars , and each one works in two directions: inside you, and between you and other people. These are retellings in our own words, not quotations. Each story lists its source so you can read the original in full. They're ideas to think with, not proofs, and they apply to every kind of relationship: partners, family, friends, roommates, co-parents, coworkers and caregivers. Every relationship runs through your own mind and body first. A teaching of the Buddha · Sallatha Sutta, Saṃyutta Nikāya 36.6 · Pali Canon The Buddha asked his students what really separates a trained mind from an untrained one, since both feel pain. When pain comes to the untrained person, he said, they worry, grieve and lament over it, so they feel two pains: one in the body and one in the mind. It is as if a man were struck by an arrow, and then struck again, right away, by a second arrow in the same place. The trained person feels the first arrow, because pain is pain. They just don't fire the second one into themselves. What it shows The event is the first arrow. The second arrow is the story we add: what it means about us, about them, and about where this is all going. It often hurts more than the event itself. A lot of arguments are mostly second arrows. What this means for us When something small stings, check how much of the hurt is the event and how much is the story you added. Deal with the event. Look gently at the story before you act on it. This story belongs to Pillar III, Read your state first . In the program Chapter III is about how much of a reaction is leftover load from somewhere else. WP-02, the Battery & Stress Meter , separates what you were already carrying from what just happened, and the WP-11 Calm-Down Kit settles yo","f":1},{"u":"/learn/index.html","t":"Stories From Philosophy for Everyday Life","h":"Stories from Philosophy","d":"Twelve old stories from philosophy, retold in plain words, to help you know yourself, see the other person more clearly and live more kindly with others.","s":["Start with yourself.","You can both be right about different parts.","Closeness is a distance you can talk about.","The ledger serves the person, never the other way round."],"x":"Twelve old stories from philosophy, retold in plain words. They help you know yourself, see the other person more clearly, and keep a ledger without letting it replace the person. Each story lines up with one of the Five Pillars , and each works in two directions: inside you, and between you and other people. In the Buddha’s story of the second arrow, the event hurts once, and the story you add about it hurts again. Many disagreements are mostly second arrows. Epictetus adds another lesson: your side is yours to look after, and their answer is theirs. Dig deeper: part one, knowing yourself In the old story, each blind man touches one part of an elephant and describes it honestly. Two people in a relationship are always touching different parts. Don’t fill in their side for them. Ask, and listen. Dig deeper: part two, seeing the other person Porcupines huddle together for warmth, then pull apart when the quills prick, until they find a middle distance. Needing warmth isn’t clingy. Needing room isn’t cold. You can talk about the distance that suits you both. Dig deeper: part three, living together Ask yourself: would you use these tools the same way if the other person could see everything you wrote? And if the numbers ever start to stand in for the person, the ledger has stopped doing its job. Dig deeper: part four, the ethics of keeping a ledger Try this: Next time something stings, write two lines: “What happened” and “What I’m telling myself it means.” Only the first line belongs in the ledger. Read The Second Arrow →","f":0},{"u":"/legal/privacy-policy.html","t":"Privacy Policy","h":"Privacy policy","d":"How Spread Love & Acceptance handles your information: what you type in the tools stays on your device.","s":["What you type stays with you","Overview","What Data Is Collected","Third-Party Processors","How Data Is Used","Data Retention","Your Rights","Children's Privacy","Changes to This Policy","Contact"],"x":"Last updated: October 1, 2026 Everything you type or choose in the tools, worksheets, check-ins and calculators on this site stays in your browser, on your own device. It is never collected. It is not sent to us or to anyone else, and we never see it. Some tools, like Today’s Weather and the Carrier Wave Decoder, save your entries in your browser so you can come back to them. A few, like the chat with Professor Puddles, keep a draft only in the current tab until you close it. Clearing your browser data removes them, and What’s stored on this device lists every one with a button to erase it. The only things that ever reach us are things you choose to send: your email address if you sign up, a message if you write to us or report a broken link, and entries you save in the optional household dashboard, which exists to keep them in sync for you and your household. 01 This Privacy Policy explains what information is collected when you use spreadloveandacceptance.com and The Objective Ledger™, operated by Patrick Christian Andrews. The public site is intentionally simple: it is a static website with no server-side code of its own. The one exception is the optional household dashboard, which uses sign-in accounts and a database hosted by Supabase (see Section 02). Almost everything described below happens through third-party services, not through code we control directly. The site offers three levels of access, and each one involves a different amount of personal information: Open Access (the free tools, printable summary, and preview chapters): no personal information is requested. Free Member (the program-updates email list): your email address, held by Buttondown. Paid Member (the paid Spread Love & Acceptance membership, which has not launched yet): your name, email, and payment details, held by Gumroad. None of the three levels requires an account. Separately, anyone may choose to use the optional household dashboard , which does use an account. You sign in with a link sent to your email, never a password, and what you enter there is stored with Supabase so it syncs across your devices. 02 Information may be collected in these places: Free membership (email list): if you join as a free member, the email address you enter is sent to and stored by Buttondown, our newsletter service. Buttondown may also collect standard email engagement data (such as whether an e","f":0},{"u":"/legal/refund-policy.html","t":"Cancellation & Refund Policy","h":"Refund policy","d":"Cancellation and refund terms for Spread Love & Acceptance.","s":["Overview","How to Cancel","Refund Eligibility","Digital Content & IP Protection","Billing Disputes","Contact"],"x":"Last updated: September 27, 2026 01 This policy governs cancellations and refunds for the paid Spread Love & Acceptance monthly membership, billed and processed through Gumroad. Paid membership has not launched yet, and nothing is charged now. This policy will apply once it does. It applies alongside our Terms of Service . 02 You can cancel your membership at any time, for any reason, in either of these ways: Log in to your Gumroad Library (library.gumroad.com) and cancel the subscription directly. Email patrick.christian.andrews@gmail.com with your cancellation request and the email address used for your subscription. Canceling stops all future billing. You retain access to previously delivered content for the remainder of the billing period you already paid for. 03 Because this is digital content delivered at the start of each billing cycle, refunds are handled as follows: Partial months: we do not provide prorated refunds for canceling partway through a billing period. You keep access through the end of the period already paid for. First billing cycle: if you believe your first charge was made in error, or the content materially did not match what was described on the site at the time of purchase, contact us within 14 days of that charge to request a review. Discretionary review: requests outside the above are reviewed case by case at our discretion. We aim to respond within 5 business days. [This section is a placeholder framework — confirm the specific refund window and eligibility criteria with counsel and update this page to match the final policy before publishing.] 04 All manuscript chapters, workpapers, and calculators delivered as part of the membership are licensed for the personal use of the subscribing household only. Neither cancellation nor a refund transfers ownership or resale rights to previously downloaded materials. Redistribution, resale, or public republication of paid content without written permission is a violation of the Terms of Service and may result in termination of access without refund. 05 If you have a billing question or believe you were charged incorrectly, please contact us directly before filing a chargeback with your bank or card provider. Most billing issues can be resolved faster this way, and a direct resolution avoids unnecessary dispute fees on both sides. 06 For cancellations, refund requests, or billing questions","f":0},{"u":"/legal/terms-of-service.html","t":"Terms of Service","h":"Terms of service","d":"The terms for using Spread Love & Acceptance and The Objective Ledger.","s":["Acceptance of Terms","Description of Service","Subscription & Billing","Intellectual Property","Disclaimers","Limitation of Liability","Termination","Changes to These Terms","Governing Law","Contact"],"x":"Last updated: September 27, 2026 01 These Terms of Service (\"Terms\") govern access to and use of spreadloveandacceptance.com and The Objective Ledger™ framework, including all free tools, the printable infographic, the manuscript, the workpapers, the free membership email list, the optional household dashboard, and the paid Spread Love & Acceptance membership (collectively, the \"Service\"), operated by Patrick Christian Andrews (\"we,\" \"us,\" or \"the operator\"). By accessing or using the Service, you agree to be bound by these Terms. If you do not agree, do not use the Service. 02 The Service is a self-help educational framework combining conceptual models drawn from bookkeeping, contract theory, and nervous-system science to help individuals, and any two people who share a home, work or care, examine workload and communication patterns. The Service is offered at three levels of access: Open Access requires no sign-up and includes the Carrier Wave Decoder, Today’s Weather (the nervous-system self-check), the Lemonade Stand calculator, the printable Infographic Executive Summary, and the preview chapters of the manuscript. These tools run in your browser. Free Membership requires only an email address and adds program-update emails delivered through Buttondown. While the program is being built, it also opens the manuscript chapters, workpapers and tools in your browser at no charge. Paid Membership has not launched yet. When it does, it will be the recurring monthly Spread Love & Acceptance membership subscription, billed and delivered through Gumroad, and will provide access to the manuscript chapters, workpapers, and calculators described on the site. None of the three levels requires an account. Section 03 applies to Paid Membership only. All other sections apply to every level. Household dashboard (optional, free). The dashboard is a shared, synced version of the free tools for two people in one household. It requires signing in with an emailed link, and each household has at most two members. You are responsible for keeping access to your email account secure, and for what you enter. The Battery & Stress Meter is meant to be filled in by each person about themselves only; please don't enter it on someone else's behalf. The dashboard is provided as is, and we may change or discontinue it. If we discontinue it, we will give reasonable notice so you can export","f":0},{"u":"/lemonade-stand.html","t":"Who Did What This Week? A Free Chore Tracker","h":"The Lemonade Stand: a free chore tracker for who did what","d":"A free chore and mental load tracker for one person or a whole home: log who did what this week, see the split or your own load, and talk without blame.","s":["Who is this for?","The task library","This week’s list","How it reads, by Chapter II","By area","Work you can see, and work you can’t","My week, as a ledger","Sustainable or stretched?","Where my time and attention go","The invisible part, made visible","Hand off, drop, simplify or schedule","Explain your load to others","Week by week","Next steps","No project needed: one owner per job"],"x":"List the jobs, add the time, and see the split, or your own load. For just you, or for two to eight people who share a home. List what it took to keep life running this week, from the dishes to remembering the dentist, and about how long each job took. Just me shows your own week as a ledger: where your time and attention go, the thinking work nobody sees, and your rest. Me and others shows the split as a plain fact, not a verdict on anyone. Pick one to start. You can switch any time, and your entries stay put. Who's at the stand? Tap a name to change it. The grey jobs and numbers are just an example . They are never counted. Type over them, add from the library, or Tap a kind of job, then tap a task to add it. Typical minutes are filled in; change them to fit your week. Count your work, your rest and the thinking part too. Each person fills in only their own side. Tasks you add fill in the time for: One line per job: how often it happens, and about how long it takes each person you each time. Rough is fine. Open “More” on a job to count the noticing, planning and remembering that goes with a job, and who notices first. One line per shared cost, like rent, power, internet or groceries. Put what each person actually paid. Leave this empty if money isn't part of your picture. Add some hours above to see how the work is split. Compare with a split we agreed on, instead of an even share Fair isn’t always even. If one of you works longer hours outside the home, you might agree on something like 60/40. Three or more of you? Each share is compared with an even share, or with the split you agreed. This reads time, not how you feel. Only you know that, and the numbers are rough on purpose. Your biggest jobs, largest first. Tap what you’d like to do with each one. There’s no right answer, and “keep” is a fine choice. A plain summary you can send or read out. It says what your week holds and what would help, without blaming anyone. How the words are chosen With two people, the bigger share under 60% reads as fairly close , 60% or more as leaning one way , and 90% or more as nearly all . With three or more people, a share of 1.5 times an even share or more reads as leaning one way, and anything less as fairly even . Exactly equal hours read as an even split. These are plain descriptions of what’s written down, never a verdict. The balance score is Chapter II’s, with the","f":0},{"u":"/library.html","t":"Relationship Psychology Library, in Plain English","h":"The science behind the program: a relationship psychology library in plain English","d":"Hundreds of plain-language entries on relationships, conflict, kindness, gratitude, emotions, stress, calm and wiring, with honest notes on the evidence.","s":["The themes","The Five Pillars, and how every topic here ties to them","Pillar I: See the whole load (borrowed from ledger accounting)","Pillar II: Fix the setup, not the person (borrowed from systems thinking)","Pillar III: Read your state first (borrowed from nervous-system science)","Pillar IV: Tune how you send and receive (borrowed from signal theory, the Frequency Framework)","Pillar V: Notice the quiet incentives (borrowed from behavioral economics)","How to tie any topic to a pillar","Every entry, by theme","How relationships work","Conflict and how to resolve it","Talking and listening","Fairness and the load","Stress and the body","Feelings and emotions","Thinking traps","Differences in wiring","Motivation, habits and change","Kindness and connection","Systems and teams","Calm and attention","Family, money and life changes"],"x":"The Objective Ledger borrows from a lot of fields: relationship research, conflict resolution, psychology, behavioral science, even project management. This library is where those ideas are laid out properly, in plain words, with who found what, how strong the evidence is, and where it is still argued about. Each entry ends with questions to talk over together, and links back to the parts of the program that use the idea. A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Every entry has the same five parts: in a nutshell , going deeper , what the research says and its limits , how it connects to the program , and to talk about . Read one a day, or pick a theme that fits what is going on for you. Professor Puddles, the site’s chat helper on the Ask page , can look things up in here too. How relationships work What decades of research on couples, families and friends say about what keeps people close. (20 entries; Pillar IV) Conflict and how to resolve it Negotiation, de-escalation, repair, apology, mediation and fair process, from research and practice. (28 entries; Pillar II, Pillar IV) Talking and listening Listening, I-statements, validation, feedback, questions, texting and the other everyday skills of being understood. (18 entries; Pillar IV) Fairness and the load Equity, the second shift, the mental load, invisible work, and why we each think we do more. (14 entries; Pillar I, Pillar V) Stress and the body The stress response, allostatic load, the window of tolerance, polyvagal theory and its critics, sleep, hunger and burnout. (14 entries; Pillar III) Feelings and emotions What emotions are, how people regulate them, naming feelings, shame and guilt, anger, rumination, grief and resilience. (16 entries; Pillar III, Pillar IV) Thinking traps The biases and mental shortcuts that make us assume the worst, remember selectively and misread each other. (21 entries; Pillar V, Pillar IV) Differences in wiring Neurodiversity, the double empathy problem, autism, ADHD, sensitivity, personality and chronotype. (17 entries; Pillar IV) Motivation, habits and change Why people do things, how habits form, procrastination, perfectionism, mindset, self-esteem, values and making c","f":0},{"u":"/library/calm.html","t":"Calm and Attention, Explained","h":"Calm and attention","d":"What research says about calm and attention: breathing, mindfulness, nature, rest, mind-wandering and flow, with modest and honest claims about each one.","s":["Calm and attention and the Five Pillars","Contents","Slow breathing","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Box breathing","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","The long exhale and cyclic sighing","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Grounding: coming back to the present","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. The program has a calm-down kit, a night garden and a soundscape library. These entries set out what research says about the ideas behind them: some tools have reasonable evidence for short-term calm, some are popular but little studied, and none are treatments. The aim is to help you choose what might suit you, knowing what is established and what is simply pleasant. Calm and attention is Pillar III, Read your state first. In you, it offers ways to notice and shift your state: slow breathing, grounding, rest and attention. Between you, it helps you choose better moments to talk, protect each other’s recovery time and be present rather than half-listening. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (1); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (1); Pillar III, Read your state first (14); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (1); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (1). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: slow breathing, breathing exercises, deep breathing, resonance breathing, long exhale, calm breathing. Breathing slowly, often around six breaths a minute, with a longer out-breath, is one of the simplest ways to feel calmer in the moment. Evidence suggests modest short-term effects on feelings of stress and relaxation. Breathing is unusual: it runs automatically but can also be controlled. Slowing it down, particularly lengthening the out-breath, is associated with changes in heart-rate patterns linked with relaxation. Many techniques exist: counting breaths, breathing in for four and out for six, or following a visual guide. Research on “resonance” breathing, around six breaths per minute, has explored why this pace seems to have particular effects on heart-rate variability. Slow breathing is useful because it can be done anywhere, quickly, without anyone noticing. It won’t solve the problem that caused the stress, but it can make it easier to think clearly enough to deal with it. A 2018 systematic review by Andrea Zaccaro and colleagues found that slow breathing was associated with changes","f":0},{"u":"/library/communication.html","t":"Communication in Relationships: Talking and Listening","h":"Talking and listening: communication in relationships","d":"Plain-language research on communication in relationships: listening, asking, feedback, tone, apology and the everyday skills of being understood.","s":["Talking and listening and the Five Pillars","Contents","Active listening","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","I-statements","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Nonviolent Communication (NVC)","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Validation","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Most misunderstandings aren’t about bad intentions. They come from the gap between what one person means and what the other person hears. These entries collect what researchers and practitioners have learned about closing that gap: how to listen, how to say hard things kindly, how to ask good questions, and why tone is so easy to lose in a text message. Talking and listening is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive. In you, it starts with knowing how you send (direct or indirect, fast or slow) and how you tend to hear things. Between you, it is the work of translating across different styles so that what was meant is what lands. Saying no and boundaries also lean on Pillar III, Read your state first, because a clear no depends on knowing how much you have left. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (3); Pillar III, Read your state first (2); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (17). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: listening skills, how to listen, being a good listener, Carl Rogers. Active listening means giving full attention, trying to understand the other person’s meaning and feelings, and showing that understanding, rather than waiting for your turn to speak. Carl Rogers, one of the founders of humanistic psychology, believed people grow when they feel deeply understood. He described three conditions for helpful relationships: empathy (understanding from the other’s point of view), unconditional positive regard (valuing the person), and congruence (being genuine). Active listening puts these into practice. In everyday terms, active listening includes putting down the phone, following what the person is actually saying (not what you expect them to say), asking questions that help them say more, and reflecting back what you understand. It also includes noticing feelings, not just facts: “That sounds really frustrating.” It is harder than it sounds. Most of us listen partly to reply: to fix, advise, defend or relate it to our own story. Those impulses are well-meant, bu","f":0},{"u":"/library/conflict.html","t":"Conflict Resolution in Relationships, Explained","h":"Conflict and how to resolve it","d":"Plain-language research on conflict in relationships: why arguments escalate, how repair works, apology, forgiveness, mediation and fair process.","s":["Conflict and how to resolve it and the Five Pillars","Contents","Why conflict is normal, and what makes it harmful","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Interests versus positions","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","The four principles of principled negotiation","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","BATNA: knowing your alternatives","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Conflict isn’t a sign that something is broken. It is what happens when two people with real needs share a life, a house or a job. The fields of negotiation, mediation and couples research have learned a lot about how disagreements go well or badly. These entries gather the most useful ideas, from the first sentence of a hard conversation to the repair afterward, and end with some common flashpoints: chores, money, time, family and work. Conflict resolution sits across Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person, and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive. In you, it means knowing your own interests, your usual conflict style and your early signs of heat. Between you, it means turning a fight about who is right into a shared problem with a fair process, clear agreements and a way back. Timeouts and flooding belong to Pillar III, Read your state first, and fairness rules to Pillar I, See the whole load. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (4); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (17); Pillar III, Read your state first (6); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (18); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (4). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: disagreement, arguing, is fighting normal, healthy conflict. Disagreement is a normal part of any close relationship. What tends to cause harm is not conflict itself but how it is handled: contempt, escalation, and conflicts that never get repaired or resolved. It is easy to assume that happy couples, families or teams don’t argue. Research doesn’t support that. Many stable relationships have plenty of disagreement. What they tend to have alongside it is warmth, respect and a way back afterward. Conflict can even be useful. It brings hidden needs into the open, forces unfair arrangements to be renegotiated, and can leave people understanding each other better. Avoiding every disagreement can mean problems pile up unseen, which is one reason the program encourages writing things down early rather than waiting for an explosion. What tends to make c","f":0},{"u":"/library/connection.html","t":"Kindness, Gratitude and Connection, Explained","h":"Kindness and connection","d":"What research says about kindness, gratitude, appreciation and connection: small daily rituals, thanks that lands and the habits that keep people close.","s":["Kindness and connection and the Five Pillars","Contents","Gratitude","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Gratitude in relationships: find, remind, bind","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Responding to good news","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Self-compassion","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Much of what keeps people close is small and ordinary: a thank-you, real interest in someone’s good news, time together that isn’t about logistics. Positive psychology and relationship science have studied these small things carefully. Some findings are robust; some popular claims are larger than the evidence. These entries try to keep both in view. Kindness and connection belong mostly to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives, with Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive. In you, it means noticing what is good, treating yourself kindly and knowing what connection you need. Between you, it is the small, steady thanks, interest and rituals that keep things from quietly sliding back, and the way you respond to each other’s good news and hard days. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (2); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (1); Pillar III, Read your state first (7); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (5); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (10). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: gratitude, being thankful, gratitude journal, counting blessings, thankfulness. Gratitude is noticing and appreciating the good things in life and the people behind them. Studies of gratitude practices, like writing down things you’re thankful for, show modest benefits for mood on average. Robert Emmons and Michael McCullough asked people to write weekly about things they were grateful for, hassles, or neutral events. The gratitude group reported somewhat more positive feelings and optimism. This helped launch a large field of research. Gratitude seems to work partly by shifting attention: it counters the negativity bias by deliberately noticing what is going well. It also connects people, because gratitude is often about someone else’s kindness. Gratitude practice isn’t a cure-all. Some people find forced gratitude feels hollow, especially during hard times, and gratitude shouldn’t be used to dismiss real problems (“you should be grateful you have a partner at all”). It works best as an invitation to ","f":0},{"u":"/library/emotions.html","t":"Understanding Feelings and Emotions","h":"Feelings and emotions","d":"Plain-language research on feelings and emotions: naming them, shame and guilt, anger, rumination, grief and resilience, with honest notes on the evidence.","s":["Feelings and emotions and the Five Pillars","Contents","What emotions are: two views","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Emotion regulation: Gross’s process model","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Reappraisal versus suppression","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Naming feelings: affect labeling","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Emotions aren’t the opposite of good sense. They are fast signals about what matters to us. Psychologists have argued for a century about exactly what they are, but they agree on a lot about how to live with them well. These entries cover the main ideas, and the program’s habit of naming feelings before trying to solve anything. Feelings and emotions span Pillar III, Read your state first, and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive. In you, it is about noticing and naming what you feel, and choosing how to respond. Between you, it is about expressing feelings so they can be heard, and receiving the other person’s feelings without taking every one as a verdict. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (1); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (1); Pillar III, Read your state first (15); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (7); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (2). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: emotions, basic emotions, constructed emotion, Ekman, Barrett, feelings. One long-standing view says there are a few basic emotions, each with its own facial expression. A newer view says the brain constructs emotions from body sensations, context and learned concepts. The debate matters for how we read each other. Paul Ekman’s research in the 1960s and 1970s suggested that some facial expressions of emotion, such as happiness, sadness, fear, anger, disgust and surprise, are recognized across cultures. This supported the idea of a small set of built-in basic emotions. Lisa Feldman Barrett’s theory of constructed emotion argues instead that emotions are not hard-wired circuits. The brain predicts and interprets body sensations using context and concepts learned from culture and experience. On this view, the same sensation (a racing heart) might become excitement, fear or anger depending on the situation. Whatever view you take, a practical lesson follows: faces and body language don’t reliably tell us what someone feels. People express the same emotion differently, and different emotions","f":0},{"u":"/library/fairness.html","t":"The Mental Load and Fair Division of Labor at Home","h":"Fairness and the mental load at home","d":"What research says about the mental load, invisible work and the division of labor at home, why each person often thinks they do more, and what helps.","s":["Fairness and the load and the Five Pillars","Contents","Equity theory","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Communal and exchange relationships","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","The second shift","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","The mental load","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. At the heart of the program is a simple observation: work that nobody sees still has to be done, and when it builds up unevenly, it quietly turns into resentment. Sociologists and psychologists have been studying this for decades. These entries set out what they found, including a few surprises about how our own memories tilt the picture. Fairness and the load is Pillar I, See the whole load, with Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives. In you, it asks you to notice everything you carry, including planning, remembering and smoothing things over. Between you, it puts that work on one shared page so nobody has to argue about whose work counts, and it watches for the quiet drift that sends unclaimed jobs to the same person. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (11); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (5); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (8). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: equity, fairness in relationships, getting back what you put in, Adams. Equity theory says people feel most comfortable when what they get out of a relationship is in proportion to what they put in, compared with the other person. Feeling under-benefited leads to resentment; feeling over-benefited can lead to guilt. J. Stacy Adams developed equity theory to explain feelings about pay at work: people compare their ratio of inputs (effort, skill, time) to outcomes (pay, recognition) with someone else’s. When the ratios don’t match, they feel tension and try to restore balance, by working less, asking for more, or changing how they see things. Elaine Hatfield (writing as Elaine Walster) and colleagues applied the idea to close relationships. They found that people who felt under-benefited reported anger and resentment, while those who felt over-benefited sometimes reported guilt. Both kinds of imbalance were associated with less satisfaction than feeling things were roughly fair. In a household, the “inputs” include paid work, chores, planning, emotional support and more, and different people weigh them differently","f":0},{"u":"/library/life.html","t":"Family, Money and Big Life Changes","h":"Family, money and life changes","d":"Plain-language research on family life, money talks, co-parents, holidays, in-laws and the big transitions of life, and how to share the load through them.","s":["Family, money and life changes and the Five Pillars","Contents","Family systems and triangles","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Family scripts and inherited patterns","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","In-laws and extended family","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Parenting as a team","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Households don’t exist in a vacuum. They sit inside wider families, money pressures and life stages that keep changing: a new baby, a move, a parent who needs more help, children leaving home. These transitions reshuffle who does what, often without anyone deciding. These entries gather what research says about the family and life-stage side of sharing a life, gently and without clinical claims. Family, money and life changes run across Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person, and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive. In you, they ask what scripts and expectations you brought from your own family and how a life change is affecting you. Between you, they ask how the setup should change when life does, so that a new baby, a move or a parent’s care doesn’t quietly reshuffle the load (Pillar I) without anyone deciding. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (6); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (13); Pillar III, Read your state first (3); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (10); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (3). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: family systems, Bowen, triangles, triangulation, differentiation, family patterns. Family systems thinking sees a family as a connected system, where each person’s behavior affects the others. Murray Bowen described how tension between two people is often routed through a third, forming a triangle. Bowen observed that when two people are in conflict, anxiety often gets relieved by involving a third: a child, a parent, a friend. A couple who argue may both confide in one of their mothers; a parent may lean on a child for support against the other parent. These triangles can bring short-term relief but keep the original issue unresolved. Bowen also described differentiation of self: the ability to stay emotionally connected to family while keeping your own thoughts and choices. Highly differentiated people can disagree without cutting off or being swept along. The systems view is useful because it moves away from blaming one pers","f":0},{"u":"/library/motivation.html","t":"Motivation, Habits and Change, Explained","h":"Motivation, habits and change","d":"Plain-language research on motivation, habits and change: mindset, self-esteem, values, rewards and the small steps that help a new habit stick for good.","s":["Motivation, habits and change and the Five Pillars","Contents","Self-determination theory","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Rewards and intrinsic motivation","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Implementation intentions: if–then plans","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","How long does it take to form a habit?","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Knowing what would help is the easy part. Doing it, day after day, is harder. Psychologists have studied motivation, habits and behavior change for a long time, and some of the most useful findings are surprisingly practical: plan the when and where, start small, make progress visible, and be kind to yourself when you slip. These entries also flag some popular ideas that turned out weaker than first thought. Motivation, habits and change sit in Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives, and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person. In you, they help you spot the defaults, rewards and feelings that steer your own choices, and redesign routines instead of blaming yourself. Between you, they explain how shared habits form, how pressure backfires, and how clear plans and visible progress keep things going. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (2); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (8); Pillar III, Read your state first (3); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (2); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (18). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: self determination theory, SDT, autonomy, competence, relatedness, intrinsic motivation, Deci and Ryan. Self-determination theory says people thrive when three basic needs are met: autonomy (feeling you have a choice), competence (feeling capable) and relatedness (feeling connected). Motivation that comes from within tends to last longer than motivation driven by pressure. Edward Deci and Richard Ryan distinguished between different kinds of motivation. Intrinsic motivation is doing something because it is interesting or satisfying. Extrinsic motivation ranges from doing something only for reward or to avoid punishment, to doing it because you genuinely value its purpose. The more internalized the reason, the more lasting and satisfying the behavior tends to be. For households, this has practical implications. Chores done because someone is nagging (pressure) tend to feel resentful and slip. Chores done because both people agreed they matter, chose h","f":0},{"u":"/library/relationships.html","t":"How Relationships Work: What Research Says","h":"How relationships work","d":"What research on couples, families and friends says about what keeps people close: attachment, trust, appreciation, repair and sharing the load at home.","s":["How relationships work and the Five Pillars","Contents","Bids for connection","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","The four horsemen and their antidotes","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Repair attempts","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","The 5:1 ratio","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Relationship science is younger than most people think. Much of it started in the 1970s and 1980s, when researchers began filming real couples talking, following them for years, and asking what separated the ones who stayed close from the ones who drifted. These entries cover the ideas that came out of that work, and the ones the program leans on most. This theme lives mostly in Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive. In you, it asks how you reach out, what you need to feel safe, and how you hear the other person’s bids. Between you, it is about noticing and answering each other’s signals. Several entries also touch Pillar III, Read your state first (flooding, attachment under stress) and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (the steady deposits of warmth and thanks that keep a relationship from sliding). Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (1); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (3); Pillar III, Read your state first (7); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (15); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (8). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: bids, turning toward, turning away, turning against. A bid is any small attempt to get another person’s attention, interest or warmth, like “look at that bird” or a sigh that invites a question. How people respond to these tiny moments, over and over, seems to matter a great deal. The idea comes from John Gottman and his colleagues, who watched couples spend ordinary time together in an apartment-style lab. They noticed that people constantly reach out in small ways: a comment about the news, a question, a touch, a joke. The other person can turn toward the bid (respond with interest, even briefly), turn away (miss it or ignore it), or turn against it (respond with irritation). What makes the idea useful is how ordinary it is. Most relationships are not made or broken in big dramatic talks. They are built in hundreds of forgettable exchanges at the kitchen counter. Someone who turns toward bids is, in effect, making lots of small deposits o","f":0},{"u":"/library/stress.html","t":"Stress and the Body, in Plain English","h":"Stress and the body","d":"How stress shows up in the body and in relationships, in plain words: the stress response, polyvagal theory and its critics, sleep, hunger and burnout.","s":["Stress and the body and the Five Pillars","Contents","The stress response","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Stress appraisal: why the same event stresses people differently","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Allostatic load: the cost of carrying stress","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","The window of tolerance","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. A lot of what goes wrong between people starts in the body: a short night, a skipped lunch, a week that has used up every reserve. The program’s battery and weather checks are built on this idea. These entries explain the science behind it, including where the popular explanations go further than the evidence. Stress and the body is Pillar III, Read your state first. In you, it means knowing how full your battery is, and whether you are calm and connected, revved up, or running on empty, before you judge a moment. Between you, it means choosing the timing, pausing and coming back, and remembering that your state shapes how the other person’s words land, and theirs shapes how yours land. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (1); Pillar III, Read your state first (14); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (2). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: fight or flight, stress, adrenaline, cortisol, HPA axis, freeze. When the brain detects a threat, the body gets ready to act: heart rate and breathing speed up, energy is released, and attention narrows. This response is protective in short bursts, but tiring when it runs for too long. Walter Cannon described the “fight or flight” response in the early twentieth century. There are two main systems involved. The fast one is the sympathetic nervous system, which releases adrenaline within seconds. The slower one is the hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal (HPA) axis, which releases cortisol over minutes and helps sustain energy. Threats don’t have to be physical. A sharp email, a looming deadline or a tense silence at home can trigger the same system. The body doesn’t distinguish very well between a tiger and a difficult conversation. In the short term, this response can sharpen focus. When it is triggered too often, or doesn’t switch off, people tend to feel tired, irritable and less patient. That is one reason why the same comment can seem minor on a good day and unbearable on a bad one. People also respond with freezing or going quiet, not just fighting ","f":0},{"u":"/library/teams.html","t":"Fair Systems for Teams and Households","h":"Systems and teams","d":"How fair systems work for teams and households: clear owners, blameless reviews, checklists, and why shared jobs slip when everyone owns them at once.","s":["Systems and teams and the Five Pillars","Contents","RACI and one owner per job","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Role ambiguity","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Diffusion of responsibility","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Social loafing","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. A household is a small team running a lot of recurring projects: meals, money, laundry, appointments, care. Workplaces have spent decades learning how teams share work without dropping things or blaming each other, and much of that learning transfers. The program borrows several of these tools on purpose. These entries explain where they came from, and what the research says about why they work. Systems and teams is Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person. In you, it means seeing your habits and routines as a setup you can redesign rather than a flaw. Between you, it means clear owners, handoffs, reviews and agreements instead of blame. Several entries also help with Pillar I, See the whole load, by getting work out of one person’s head and onto a shared page. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (6); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (14); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (1); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (3). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: RACI, responsibility assignment matrix, responsible accountable consulted informed, ownership, one owner. RACI is a project-management tool that lists every task and marks who is Responsible (does it), Accountable (owns the outcome), Consulted and Informed. The program uses a simplified version: every regular job gets one named owner. In workplaces, RACI charts help clarify roles on complex projects, where confusion about who does what can lead to duplicated effort or dropped tasks. The key rule is usually that each task has exactly one person accountable. At home, the same problem appears: “I thought you were doing it.” Jobs that belong to “both of us” or “whoever notices” are the ones most likely to slip, or to fall to the person with the lowest mess threshold. A single owner removes ambiguity. The program’s version keeps it simple: for each regular job, write down who owns it (noticing, planning and doing) and who helps. Ownership can be changed on purpose, but not by drift. Agreeing on it means the arrangement is clear, n","f":0},{"u":"/library/thinking.html","t":"Common Thinking Traps, Explained","h":"Thinking traps","d":"Plain-language guides to common thinking traps: why we jump to conclusions, expect the worst, remember selectively and misread each other, and what helps.","s":["Thinking traps and the Five Pillars","Contents","The fundamental attribution error","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Actor–observer asymmetry","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Negativity bias: why bad weighs more than good","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Confirmation bias","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. Human minds take shortcuts. Most of the time they work well enough, but in close relationships they can quietly tilt the picture: we judge others by their actions and ourselves by our intentions, remember our own efforts better than anyone else’s, and assume the worst when we’re tired. Knowing the common traps doesn’t make anyone immune, but it makes them easier to spot, in ourselves first. Thinking traps belong mostly to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives: the mental shortcuts and defaults that steer judgments without our noticing. In you, it means catching your own biases, like remembering your efforts more than anyone else’s. Between you, it means seeing how those shortcuts turn a situation into a story about someone’s character. Many entries also point to Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person, and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (4); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (6); Pillar III, Read your state first (4); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (6); Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives (14). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: fundamental attribution error, correspondence bias, blaming character, assuming the worst about people, why do we assume the worst. People tend to explain others’ behavior by their character (“they’re careless”) and underweight the situation (“they were rushing to pick up the kids”). It is one of the most studied biases in social psychology. When someone cuts you off in traffic, it is easy to think “what a rude person” rather than “maybe they’re rushing to hospital.” With our own behavior, we usually know the circumstances, so we explain it by the situation. In close relationships, this bias can do a lot of damage. A forgotten errand becomes “you don’t care” rather than “you had a hard day.” Over time, these character explanations add up to a story about who the other person is, which then colors every new event. A useful habit is to ask “what situation would make a reasonable person do this?” It doe","f":0},{"u":"/library/wiring.html","t":"Neurodiversity Explained: Autism, ADHD and Sensitivity","h":"Differences in wiring: neurodiversity explained","d":"Plain-language entries on differences in wiring: autism, ADHD, the double empathy problem, high sensitivity, sensory overload, personality and chronotype.","s":["Differences in wiring and the Five Pillars","Contents","The double empathy problem","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","The neurodiversity paradigm","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Autism: a plain-language overview","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits","How it connects to the program","To talk about","Monotropism","In a nutshell","Going deeper","What the research says, and its limits"],"x":"A gentle note: this library is general education about how people tend to think, feel and get along. It is not diagnosis, therapy or treatment, and it can’t know your situation. Where the evidence is thin, mixed or debated, each entry says so. People process the world differently: how much sensory input they can take, how they communicate, how they manage time and attention, when in the day they are at their best. The program treats these differences as information, not flaws. These entries explain the research and the community ideas behind that view, with honest notes on where terms are formal diagnoses and where they are community language. To sort your own reactions into lasting wiring, learned patterns and today’s conditions, and explain them to others, start with Know your own wiring . Differences in wiring are Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive. In you, it means knowing your own wiring, pace, senses and how you process the world. Between you, it means treating a mismatch as tuning rather than a moral failing, and translating across different neurotypes, energies and body clocks. Sensory and energy entries also draw on Pillar III, Read your state first. Entries on this page, by pillar: Pillar I, See the whole load (1); Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person (2); Pillar III, Read your state first (7); Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive (16). Each entry says which pillar it belongs to and how it works in you and between you and others. The Five Pillars, explained . Also known as: double empathy, autistic communication, mutual misunderstanding, Milton. The double empathy problem, described by autistic researcher Damian Milton, says that misunderstandings between autistic and non-autistic people run both ways. It isn’t that one side lacks empathy; each finds the other hard to read. For decades, autism was described as a deficit in social understanding, as if autistic people alone struggled to read others. Milton pointed out that non-autistic people also misread autistic people, often badly. The difficulty lies in the gap between different ways of communicating and perceiving. Supporting research found that autistic people often communicate effectively with each other, and that mixed pairs have more difficulty. Non-autistic people also tend to form quick negative first impressions of autistic people, based on differences in style rather than c","f":0},{"u":"/membership.html","t":"Membership","h":"Free while it’s being built","d":"Everything on spreadloveandacceptance.com is free with an email sign-up while the program is being built. Paid membership is coming later.","s":["How access works","What your email opens","Leaving the list","Paid membership"],"x":"The program is still being written, so for now everything on this site is free. Enter your email once and every chapter, workpaper and tool opens in that browser. Paid membership is coming later. Nothing is charged now, and everyone on the email list will hear about it before anything changes. Not sure it’s for you? See if this is right for you . For what each level opens and shares, see Ways in . Enter your email here or on any page marked Free with sign-up . Your address goes to Buttondown, our newsletter service. They may send a confirmation email. Confirming it keeps you on the update list. Every page opens right away in that browser. On another device, enter the same email address again. This site doesn’t store your email on its own server. Your browser remembers that you signed up, so pages stay open. Clearing your browser data signs you out. Every email has an unsubscribe link. To close pages in this browser, use the sign-out button above. Questions: patrick.christian.andrews@gmail.com . Coming later, once more of the program is built. When it launches, it will be billed through Gumroad, and the cancellation and refund policy will apply to it. There’s nothing to buy yet.","f":0},{"u":"/neurodivergent-relationships.html","t":"Neurodivergent Relationships: ADHD and Autistic Couples","h":"Neurodivergent relationships: ADHD, autistic and AuDHD couples and families","d":"Practical tips for ADHD, autistic and AuDHD couples and families: communication differences, sensory overload at home, plans, chores and kind feedback.","s":["What “neurodivergent” means here","Common communication differences","ADHD in relationships: practical tips","Autistic relationships: practical tips","AuDHD: when both pulls happen at once","Sensory overload at home","When partners are wired differently","For families and kids","Questions people often ask","Do I need a diagnosis to use these tips?","How do I explain my ADHD or autism to my partner?","Can ADHD and autistic people have happy relationships?","What if our needs clash?","Is this therapy or medical advice?"],"x":"In neurodivergent relationships, the same words can land very differently. An ADHD partner may lose track of time or plans that were never written down. An autistic partner may take words literally, need more time to process, or feel overloaded by noise and change. People who are both (often called AuDHD) can feel both pulls at once. Most of the friction comes from these differences, not from a lack of love. Clear words, visible plans, agreed pauses and a calm, quiet space help everyone. A note before you start: this guide is for anyone who sees themselves in it, with or without a formal diagnosis. It describes common experiences, not rules. Every person is different. It does not diagnose anything, and it is not therapy or treatment. “Neurodivergent” describes a brain that works differently from what most people expect: in attention, in how it takes in information, in senses or in social signals. ADHD, autism, dyslexia and high sensitivity are common examples. “Neurotypical” describes the more expected pattern. Neither way is wrong. But when two people are wired differently, they can be completely sincere and still misread each other. The aim is not to change who anyone is. It is to build a shared way of talking and planning that works for both of you. Hints versus plain words. “The trash is getting full” may be a request to one person and just a fact to another. Saying the request out loud helps: “Could you take out the trash tonight?” Pace. Some people think out loud and fast. Others need quiet time before they can answer. Neither is avoiding the talk. Tone and faces. A flat tone or little eye contact can be how someone listens best. It does not always mean they are upset or bored. Interrupting and info-dumping. Jumping in or sharing a lot of detail about something exciting is often a way of connecting, not of taking over. Change. A sudden change of plan can feel small to one person and very hard to another. A heads-up, even ten minutes ahead, can make a big difference. Want to see your own patterns? The free Wavelength quiz gives you a four-letter Wave Code for your communication style: your pace, how much detail you like, and how you send and receive words. Read more on the communication style quiz page. Make plans visible. A shared calendar, a whiteboard on the fridge or a printable chore chart works better than “we talked about it.” One owner per job, ","f":0},{"u":"/night-garden.html","t":"The Night Garden: A Calm Breathing Exercise Game","h":"The Night Garden: a calm breathing game","d":"A breathing exercise you can play: breathe slowly to make flowers bloom, guide fireflies into constellations and stack glowing stones. Free, calm, no timers.","s":["Welcome to the Night Garden","A new constellation","Something to carry with you","Why a garden?"],"x":"The Night Garden A quiet place to breathe, play and let your mind settle. There are no timers and no way to lose. Everything here stays on your device. What would you like to do? The garden is silent unless you turn sound on, and you can turn it off again anytime with the Sound button at the bottom. If you like sound, headphones are lovely here. You can switch between the three activities anytime with the buttons at the bottom. Tap How to play whenever you need a reminder. Would you like to dedicate it to someone? Their name will glow beneath it in your sky. It stays on this device only. Who is this one for? Come back whenever you like. The garden will be here, and it keeps growing. Pause & Play Quiet Words Turning Toward Back to the site The Night Garden is a gentle place to land at the end of a day: a few quiet things to do, with nothing to win and nothing to lose. It isn’t a treatment, and it doesn’t replace talking to someone. Slow breathing. The garden brings a different calm pattern on most visits: box breathing (in, hold, out, hold, four counts each), a triangle, a slow wave or a long breath out. Each one breathes out for at least as long as it breathes in, at a few unhurried breaths a minute. The light in the garden keeps the count for you, so all you have to do is follow it. Gentle play. Fireflies to guide and lilies to fit together, at your own pace. There’s no score to chase here and nothing to lose. Busy hands. Fitting shapes together gives your attention one small, absorbing thing to hold, if your thoughts feel busy. Soft, natural movement. Slowly moving light, water and leaves, to watch for as long as you like. The garden grows as you visit: flowers you bloom stay, the moon follows the real one, the seasons change with the calendar, and every night has its own sky. The pictures, ponds and breathing patterns you’ve seen are remembered, so they don’t come round again too soon. Missing a day costs nothing. Everything is kept only in this browser, on this device.","f":0},{"u":"/on-this-device.html","t":"What’s stored on this device","h":"","d":"Everything this site keeps in your browser, in plain words, with a button to erase each one or everything at once. Nothing on this list is ever sent to us.","s":["Kept on this device right now","Kept only until you close this tab","What is never kept"],"x":"Some pages keep small notes in this browser, like your settings, a draft, or how far you got in a game. They stay on this device. They are never sent to us. Here is every one of them, in plain words, with a way to erase it. Only in this browser. Another browser or phone won’t see them. Erasing is for good. There is no copy anywhere else to bring it back from. Erasing a setting just puts that page back to its usual look. Looking… These disappear by themselves when the tab or browser is closed. What you type into the chat, the tools and the worksheets is not sent anywhere. Your email address is sent to the newsletter service only when you choose to sign up, and the site keeps a copy in this browser so every page stays open for you. How we handle your information","f":0},{"u":"/pause-and-play.html","t":"Calm Games for a Busy Mind: Free, No Timers","h":"Pause & Play: calm games with no timers","d":"Free calm games with no timers and no way to lose: the Night Garden breathing game, the Frequency Journey, Word Bloom, crosswords and a cozy word search.","s":["Only have a moment?","Breathe with the circle","The games","Drift: the calm visualizer","The Night Garden","The Frequency Journey","Word Bloom","Quiet Crossword","The Daily Ledger Crossword","Quiet Words","A breathing break","My garden"],"x":"Calm games for a busy mind The wind-down is silent: one minute of slow breathing, one minute with a quiet picture, and one kind sentence. You can stop at any time. 1 of 3 · Breathe What is Pause & Play? It’s the calm corner of the site: a handful of gentle games for when your mind needs a rest. Breathe in the Night Garden, take the Frequency Journey, spell words in Word Bloom, solve a crossword, or find words in a quiet word search. There are no timers and no way to lose. Every level you finish counts, and now and then something new appears in the living garden behind every page and joins the animals playing there. My garden keeps a list of everything that has arrived. A few minutes here is a lovely way to settle before a hard conversation, or to wind down at night. Nothing you do is sent anywhere. New Tell it how you feel, pop on headphones, and float through slow, deep 3D colors with soft, low tones and a light that breathes with you. 5 to 20 minutes, nothing to do Box breathing with a glowing light, fireflies that sing into constellations, and a slow lily-pond puzzle. It grows as you play. A moonlit place to settle New Guide two best friends through a stormy forest, melting glaciers, a golden meadow, a canyon, singing valleys and a starry summit, all the way up to an open sky where there’s nothing to do but rest. Riddles, life lessons and puzzles, a new kind every level New Swipe across the petals to spell words and fill a little crossword. Thousands of wheels in five sizes New A small, friendly crossword with a new themed one every day, and thousands more to play. Made for your phone New A newspaper-style crossword: a new Mini every day, a bigger grid that grows through the week, and a Big Sunday on Sundays. Five sizes, and the whole collection to play any time A gentle word search. Every word leaves a kind thought behind. A new theme each day, and hundreds more Everything that has arrived in the garden behind the pages, and the constellations you named. Kept on this device only","f":0},{"u":"/perspective-shifter.html","t":"See It From Their Side: A Free Perspective-Taking Tool","h":"The Perspective Shifter: see it from their side","d":"Step into the other person’s side of a moment: their day, their wiring, what they could see and what they could not. A free, private perspective-taking tool.","s":["Why what you can see changes what’s true for you","Where to go next"],"x":"Two people can share the same moment and still see two different things. The Perspective Shifter helps you step round to the other person’s side: who they are, how they’re wired, the state they were in, what was going on around them, and, most often missed, what they could see that you couldn’t. How it works: Tap what might be true for them. Their side of the moment builds itself as you go, with questions to check instead of guessing and a sentence to bridge the gap. It never decides who was right. It shows how each of you could be seeing something true. Everything happens on this device. Nothing is sent anywhere or kept. Most misunderstandings aren’t about different values. They’re about different information. One person saw the message from school; the other didn’t. One person knew the deadline; the other only heard the hurry. Each fills the gaps with what they can see, and the result can look like carelessness, nagging or coldness, when it’s really two people working from two different pictures. That’s why the Shifter asks three questions about visibility: what only they could see, what only you could see, and what neither of you could. The answers often explain the whole moment before anyone needs to apologize. The Signal Translator shows how a sentence may land, so you can test your bridge sentence before you say it. Chapter III explains the seven angles the Shifter is built on. Wired Differently goes deeper into how different wiring changes what’s heard. Wavelength helps you put your own side into words: how you think, talk and listen, with a Wave Code they can compare with theirs.","f":0},{"u":"/podcast-index.html","t":"Observational Podcast: The Kane and Christian Experience","h":"Observational Podcast","d":"The Objective Ledger podcast series: episodes exploring workpapers, chapters, and real-world applications of the framework with Kane and Christian.","s":["Unbilled Debt: Why Your Ledger Has Only One Column","The Radio Frequency Paradigm in Action","The Lemonade Stand: Why One Person's Books Aren't Enough","Your Nervous System is Data","The Extraction Cycle in Slow Motion","Opening the Ledger: How to Talk About the Numbers","Tone Matters: Separating Content from Delivery","Processing Speed: Why You Mobilize Faster Than They Do","The Phase-Locked Loop: Getting In Sync When You're Out of Sync","External Static: When the Stress Isn't Relational","Building a Ritual: How to Stay in Resonance for the Long Term"],"x":"Podcast The Kane and Christian Experience. Exploring The Objective Ledger framework through stories, real-life examples and conversation. About the podcast: Each episode is a deep dive into one workpaper, chapter or theme. Listen in any order, or follow the episode path matched to the PROG-01 curriculum weeks. Listening while you read or use the tools can add a human voice to the ideas. Hosted by Kane and Christian with rotating guests, real couple interviews, and listener questions. No episodes are published yet. The first three are recorded and coming soon; the rest are in production or planned. When they’re out, the links will appear right here. Filter by: Episode 1 28 minutes Kane and Christian open the series by exploring the core problem: invisible labor accumulates when only one person sees the books. What gets recorded? What stays hidden? And why does this matter? Foundation Preface + Chapter I PROG Week 1 Recorded Coming soon Episode 2 31 minutes Kane and Christian break down mismatches in pace, register (tone) and urgency, with real examples. Two reasonable people, slightly out of sync, create interference. How to notice when you're out of tune with each other, and how to get back in step. Foundation Chapter I PROG Week 2 Recorded Coming soon Episode 3 26 minutes A deep dive into the Lemonade Stand, the program's basic rule of thumb. One person makes the lemonade. One sells it. Both are necessary. Why does shared accounting change everything? Foundation The Lemonade Stand PROG Week 2 Recorded Coming soon Episode 4 33 minutes Kane, Christian and neuroscience guests discuss polyvagal theory (one model of how the nervous system shifts between calm, alert and shut down), why your body often notices an unsustainable arrangement before your mind does, and how to trust your body's signals. Diagnostics WP-02 (Battery) PROG Week 3 In Production Coming soon Episode 5 28 minutes How invisible work grows into resentment, with real household examples from listeners. What the extraction cycle looks like at each stage, and how stepping in early prevents the blowup. Diagnostics WP-01 PROG Week 3 In Production Coming soon Episode 6 35 minutes Kane and Christian interview couples who've successfully opened their shared ledger. What worked? What failed? What did they wish they'd known before they started? Communication WP-01, WP-09 PROG Week 4 In Production Coming so","f":0},{"u":"/polymath.html","t":"The Polymath Way: How Every Field Connects","h":"The polymath way: how every field connects","d":"How psychology, economics, nature, music and more grow from the same few roots, and how a polymath joined nine fields into one program you can learn.","s":["What is a polymath?","One root, many branches","⚖️ Balance","📡 Signal and noise","🔁 Feedback loops","🔋 Limited capacity","🗂️ Ownership and structure","🧲 Quiet incentives","🌤️ State and setting","The creator, a polymath","The nine fields, one chapter each","Where the fields meet: the Five Pillars","A new dimension","Try it: connect two fields","Every field connects to every other: all 36 pairs","From obvious to hidden: the climb","One field leads into the next","The intersection of all nine","1. How one moment travels through all nine fields","2. When something goes wrong: where to look first","3. Seven roots in the middle, nine fields around them","How to think like a polymath","Ask “what is this really about?”","Borrow from somewhere else"],"x":"A polymath is someone who learns deeply across many fields and lets them talk to each other. Underneath, the fields aren’t as separate as school makes them look: the same few patterns grow through all of them. That idea is where this whole program comes from. In short: Different subjects are branches of the same few roots, like balance, signals and limits. Christian, the creator, studied nine fields and found the places where they meet. Those meeting places became the Five Pillars and the tools, so you can learn one way of seeing that works at home, at work and inside yourself. The word comes from Greek and means “having learned much.” A polymath isn’t someone who knows a little about everything. It’s someone who goes deep in several fields and then notices that the fields are describing the same things in different words. History is full of them: Leonardo da Vinci painted, designed machines and studied anatomy, and his drawings of the body helped his painting, and the other way round. Hildegard of Bingen , a 12th-century abbess, wrote music, theology and books about plants and the natural world. Ibn Sina (Avicenna) was a philosopher and a physician whose medical writing was studied for centuries. Benjamin Franklin was a printer, writer, scientist and diplomat. Mary Somerville wrote a book in 1834 called On the Connexion of the Physical Sciences , about how astronomy, light, heat and magnetism are parts of one picture. Researchers who study creativity have noticed the same thing: people who make breakthroughs often have serious interests far outside their own field, because an idea from one area can unlock a problem in another. Learning experts call it transfer : taking what you understand in one place and using it somewhere new. Look closely and the same patterns keep turning up, whether you’re studying a bank account, a forest, a body or a conversation. These are the roots this program is built on: What flows in has to match what flows out, or something runs dry. A ledger, a household budget, a pond in summer, a tired body, a friendship where only one person gives. What’s sent isn’t always what arrives. Static gets in the way. A radio, a text message, a nerve, a hard conversation, a rumor. Small things repeated, with a check each time, shape the whole system. A thermostat, a habit, a garden, a weekly check-in, a song finding its rhythm. Everything has a li","f":0},{"u":"/prog-01-in-depth.html","t":"PROG-01 in depth: The 6-Week Guided Implementation Roadmap","h":"PROG-01: The 6-Week Guided Implementation Roadmap","d":"PROG-01: Structured, week-by-week curriculum for The Objective Ledger. Integrates chapters, workpapers, podcast episodes, and optional audio.","s":["Foundation & Self-Audit","📖 Read (1.5 hours)","🔧 Tools (2.5 hours)","📻 Listen (1 hour)","🎵 Audio Reset","Ownership Clarity: One Owner per Job","📖 Read (1.5 hours)","🔧 Tools (2.5 hours)","📻 Listen (1.5 hours)","🎵 Audio Reset","The Numbers & Your Batteries","📖 Read (1.5 hours)","🔧 Tools (2 hours)","📻 Listen (1.5 hours)","🎵 Audio Reset","Communication & Tone","📖 Read (1 hour)","🔧 Tools (3 hours)","📻 Listen (1.5 hours)","🎵 Audio Reset","Frequency Alignment & Synchronization","📖 Read (1.5 hours)","🔧 Tools (2.5 hours)","📻 Listen (1.5 hours)"],"x":"A structured, sequential path through The Objective Ledger framework. Each week brings together chapters, workpapers, podcast episodes and optional audio in one steady progression. How to use this curriculum: Follow the weeks in order. Each week takes about 5 to 7 hours in total (reading, using the tools, listening and practicing). You don't need to wait until week one is perfect to start week two. This is not a sprint. It's a gentle, structured way in. By the end of week six, you'll have worked through the core of the framework and be ready to keep using it. The other person's participation is optional but recommended. The program works for any two people who share time, space, work or care: partners, family members, roommates, co-parents, coworkers or caregivers (see how it fits your relationships ). Some weeks ask you both to read chapters or use tools together. If the other person isn't ready yet, you can still complete the curriculum individually. Week 1 Learn your starting point. Learn the key words. Start logging. Preface Unbilled Debt Read the opening. It's short, and it explains why you're building a shared ledger. Understand the problem before you look at solutions. Chapter I The Radio Frequency Paradigm Learn the metaphor. This is the central idea: two signals slightly out of sync create interference. Not a diagnosis. A shared vocabulary for getting back in rhythm. Check Your State Today’s Weather (Nervous-System Self-Check) Use the sky check: clear, gusty or fogged in. Know your own state before every significant conversation. Log it each day, and it becomes your baseline for the week. WP-01 Field Audit & Neutral Refusals Start logging daily tasks and emotional labor for 7 days. You're not sharing this yet—just building awareness of invisible work. Get to know the three Neutral Refusals scripts. Use them if a request comes in that you need to decline or defer. 📻 Podcast Episode 1: \"Unbilled Debt: Why Your Ledger Has Only One Column\" Kane and Christian explore the core problem: invisible labor accumulates when only one person sees the books. Listening alongside your reading adds a human voice to the ideas. (~28 min) 🎵 \"The Breath Beneath\" — 40 Hz ambient (3 min) Optional. A few quiet minutes at the end of the week, before week 2. It’s in the Soundscape Catalog ; no workpaper requires audio. Expected Output by End of Week 1 You've read the Preface ","f":1},{"u":"/prog-01.html","t":"A Six-Week Guided Program for Fairer Chores","h":"Six gentle weeks: a guided program for fairer chores","d":"Six gentle weeks, one worksheet a week: see the mental load, give every chore an owner, check your stress and how you talk, then look at what has changed.","s":["Week 1: See the work that’s already happening.","Week 2: Give every job one owner.","Week 3: Notice how full each of your batteries is.","Week 4: Talk about it kindly.","Week 5: Get back in step.","Week 6: Make it last."],"x":"A six-week path through the program, one step at a time. Follow the weeks in order. It works best with the other person, but you can do it on your own. Read the Preface and Chapter I . Before any hard talks, check how you are doing with Today’s Weather . Then jot down a week of who does what in “Who did what” (WP-01) , a simple daily log. You are not sharing it yet. Dig deeper: week 1, foundation Read Chapter II . Use “One owner per job” (WP-03) to name one owner for each regular job, so nothing is left vaguely shared. Then get a first read on whether the split can last from the Solvency Read (CALC-01) . Dig deeper: week 2, ownership Read Chapters III and IV . Use “How much are you carrying?” (WP-02) , a short stress check, each day for a week. Look for patterns: who is carrying more, and when stress runs high. Dig deeper: week 3, the numbers Learn to hear what someone means apart from how it came out. “Say it so it lands” (WP-09) helps you turn a raw reaction into fact, feeling and a clear ask. The Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) is where you agree on your pause line on a calm day. Dig deeper: week 4, communication Notice where your pace differs: one of you may move fast while the other needs time. Try the 90-second daily check-in (WP-13) together at least once. Dig deeper: week 5, getting in sync Run the Field Audit (WP-01) again and compare it with week 1. Separate outside stress, like poor sleep or money worries, from how you share the work. Then choose a simple weekly ritual that keeps you in step without becoming another chore. Turning Toward has ideas. Dig deeper: week 6, long-term habits Try this: Start week 1: each of you logs one week of who does what. Open WP-01 → Or do all six weeks in the Workpaper Suite : one fillable PDF, week by week, with a report at the end. For every page on your road in one go, use the Full path package . This part is still growing. As each part of the program is developed, new material is added right here: more examples, stories, try-this steps and mini dives, so it keeps getting easier to take in. Come back any time. Where it lives stays the same, and your place in the program does too.","f":0},{"u":"/program-overview-in-depth.html","t":"Program Overview in depth","h":"Program Overview, in depth","d":"How the manuscript chapters map onto the workpapers: what each chapter argues, which tool it pairs with, and what is built today.","s":["The Manuscript","The Radio Frequency Paradigm","The Epistemic Verdict Engine & P(Solvency)","Autonomic Saturation & the 7 Ocular Vectors","Deontological Parity & Sensory Gating","The Deficit Audit","The Signal Layer","90-second check-in (Phase-Locked Loop)","Carrier Frequency Audit","Static Dampening Log","Harmonic Resonance & Entrainment","How They Connect","Designed, Not Built"],"x":"A note on status: every item below is marked either built or designed-not-built. Sections marked as designed describe intended structure only. No text exists for them yet. Where this page and the Suite Index disagree, the Suite Index is correct. Layer One Five chapters and a preface. Each chapter makes an argument. The workpaper it pairs with turns that argument into something a household fills in. The framework borrows its vocabulary from signal processing and accounting because those fields already have precise words for things households experience vaguely: drift, saturation, an unbilled cost. The metaphors are descriptive tools, not claims about physiology or physics. Chapter I Live Separates the message from its carrier: what is being said, and the tone carrying it. Most household conflict isn't a disagreement about content. It's two people sending on frequencies the other can't receive, and hearing static where a message was meant. Pairs with: WP-09, the Tone Filter · Free to read on the site Chapter II Live The case for scoring an arrangement rather than a person, and the arithmetic that does it. Defines the three inputs the calculator reads and states plainly what the resulting number can and cannot support. Pairs with: CALC-01, the Solvency Read · Free to read on the site Chapter III Live How much of a reaction was already present before the conversation started, and which point of view each person is arguing from. Separates \"how much am I carrying\" from \"how upset am I about this specific thing.\" Those two questions feel identical in the moment but need different answers. Pairs with: WP-02, the Battery & Stress Meter · WP-11, the Calm-Down Kit Chapter IV Live Why most chore arguments are really arguments about what's fair, dressed up as arguments about dishes. Two people apply different ideas of what a fair share means, and neither has said which idea they hold. Sensory gating covers what gets filtered out before it reaches attention, and why one person genuinely doesn't see what the other cannot stop seeing. Pairs with: WP-03, the RACI Treaty Chapter V Live Why you need a monthly look-back on top of the weekly check-ins. Some patterns are invisible week to week and only come into focus when four weeks are laid side by side. A recurring gap usually turns out to be structural rather than a run of bad weeks. Pairs with: WP-04, the Deficit Audit Layer","f":1},{"u":"/program-overview.html","t":"Program Overview","h":"","d":"How the program fits together: the chapters explain the ideas and the worksheets put them to use. It also says plainly what is built today and what is not yet.","s":["Each chapter pairs with a worksheet.","A second layer looks at timing and tone.","Some worksheets feed one number.","No score ever comes from one person alone.","Some parts are designed, not built."],"x":"How the program fits together: the chapters explain the ideas and the worksheets put them to use. It also says plainly what is built and what isn't yet. Looking for where to begin? See which part of the program to use . There are five chapters and a preface. Each chapter shares one idea, and its matching workpaper (a worksheet) is where you put it to use. Dig deeper: the chapters These tools look at how things are said, not who does how much. One is built: the 90-second daily check-in (WP-13), a gentle check-in with no debate. Three more are designed but not written yet. Dig deeper: the signal layer WP-01, WP-02 and WP-03 feed the Solvency Read (CALC-01), a calculator that gives one read on whether the way you share things can last. Others give no score, on purpose. Dig deeper: how they connect Nothing reads tone of voice, recordings or messages. None of it is a validated clinical tool. A number should start a conversation, never end one. Dig deeper: what it will never use Chapters VI to XII and several workpapers and calculators are still only planned. If this page and the Suite Index ever differ, go with the Suite Index. Dig deeper: what isn't built yet Try this: See exactly what is live today, and what is still coming. Open the Content Roadmap →","f":0},{"u":"/program.html","t":"Which part of the program to use","h":"","d":"The program in one place: six gentle weeks, the Workpaper Suite, the package and report, the fill-in worksheets and the indexes, and which one to use when.","s":["Six gentle weeks","The Workpaper Suite","The package and report","Your progress, week by week","The maps: overview and indexes","Side by side"],"x":"There are a few ways into the same program. They all use the same chapters and worksheets; they differ in how much is laid out for you. If you only pick one, pick six gentle weeks . Use it when: you’d like to be walked through, one small step a week. One worksheet a week, in order, ending with a before-and-after look in week six. It works best with the other person, and works on your own too. A tracker can remember which weeks you’ve done, on this device only, if you turn it on. Open six gentle weeks The full week-by-week guide Use it when: you want the worksheets for your situation in one place, ready to fill in. Choose your road (just you, two of you, or a group) and the Suite lays out the right worksheets in order, as fillable PDFs you save on your device, with a plan for each week. Open the Workpaper Suite Just the fill-in worksheets Use it when: you’ve filled things in and want it all pulled together. One fillable PDF with every worksheet for your relationship (or just for you), then a report made from your answers: what stands out, things worth a second look, and kind next steps. How the package works The just-for-you version Use it when: you’re partway in and want the picture to build up instead of starting over. A simple week-by-week record of what each step turned up (REPORT-01). Open your progress record Use them when: you want to see how it all fits, or exactly what’s built. The Program Overview explains how chapters, worksheets and calculators connect. The Suite Index is the official list of what’s live today. Neither is a place to start; they’re for looking things up. Program Overview Suite Index Every page If you want… Use Time To be walked through it Six gentle weeks About an hour a week The right worksheets, filled in and saved The Workpaper Suite As you go Everything pulled into one report The package and report Once you’ve filled things in Just a taste first Start in 10 minutes Ten minutes To look something up Suite Index or Contents A minute Try this: Not ready for six weeks? Take the ten-minute path first. It shows you the whole idea in small pieces. Start in 10 minutes →","f":0},{"u":"/quest.html","t":"A map of six islands: Home Harbor, and one island for each of the Five Pillars. Choose an island to jump to its list.","h":"The Quest Map","d":"A friendly map of the whole program, grouped by the Five Pillars. Each chapter, workpaper, guide, tool, game and library shelf lights up as you explore it.","s":["Start here: three easy first steps","⚓Home Harbor: start here","ISee the whole load","IIFix the setup, not the person","IIIRead your state first","IVTune how you send and receive","VNotice the quiet incentives"],"x":"The whole program is a little island world, one island for each of the Five Pillars. On many reading pages you’ll find small “Check yourself” moments: a quick question, a card to flip, a word to match. Find them all and that page’s lantern lights up here. Every moment you find earns a petal, and petals raise your explorer level. Your explorer level Level 1: Curious Visitor Find a moment on any reading page to earn your first petal. A good place to begin The Five Pillars The five ideas under everything, in ten minutes. Start here How the pieces fit, in order. Know your own wiring Why you react the way you do. Choose an island to jump to its pieces. A lit lantern means you’ve found every “Check yourself” moment on that page. Lantern lit: every moment found Moments started Visited Still to explore Where every explorer sets out: the big picture, the library and a friendly guide. The Five Pillars Guide Start here Guide How it works Guide Program overview Guide The Professor’s Library Library Further reading Articles Stories from Philosophy Articles Chat with Professor Puddles Chat Notice everything you carry, then put it on one shared, fair page. Preface: The work nobody sees Chapter Chapter II: Is the split working? Chapter WP-01: Who did what Workpaper How it fits your relationships Guide The Lemonade Stand Tool CALC-01: Is the setup working for everyone? Tool REPORT-01: Your progress Workpaper Fairness and the load Library Treat habits and routines as a setup you can redesign, with one clear owner per job. Chapter V: The monthly look-back Chapter WP-03: One owner per job Workpaper WP-04: What keeps coming back? Workpaper Six gentle weeks Program Systems and teams Library Motivation, habits and change Library Know how full your battery is before you judge a moment, and pick your timing. Chapter III: Full tanks and different angles Chapter Know your own wiring Know yourself WP-02: How much are you carrying? Workpaper WP-11: The Calm-Down Kit Workpaper Check-ins Guide Today’s Weather Tool The Carrier Wave Decoder Tool Mood Arbitrage Tool Soundscapes Tool Pause & Play Game The Night Garden Game Word Bloom Game Quiet Words Game Stress and the body Library Calm and attention Library Feelings and emotions Library Know your own wiring, and translate across different wiring and tone. Chapter I: Why we get out of tune Chapter The Frequency Framework Guide Wired Differen","f":0},{"u":"/quick-checks.html","t":"Daily Mood Check-In and Stress Check: Today’s Weather","h":"Today's Weather: a one-minute daily mood check-in","d":"A one-minute daily mood check-in and stress check: how you are doing today, a gentle forecast for hard talks, and a calm pause with something soothing to do.","s":["Check today's weather","The sky","Who you'll mostly be around today","The pressure","Ground conditions","Last night's sleep","People load","What's blowing in","What would actually help in the next hour?","Your forecast appears here","A gentle pause before you talk","Good hours for","Leave until the front passes","Between you and others today","Sixty seconds that might help right now","Then, in the program","If you need to say something now","Your 7-day log"],"x":"People have inner weather. Some mornings are clear, some are gusty, some are fogged in, and it changes without asking your permission. This is a one-minute reading of today's conditions: the sky, the pressure, the ground underneath, and what's blowing in. We use weather on purpose. Weather isn't a grade, a diagnosis or a verdict on you, any more than rain is a verdict on a town. Conditions pass. Knowing them just tells you what today is good for, and what to leave until the front moves through. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar III, Read your state first , part of the Five Pillars . In you: know how full your battery is (calm, revved up or running on empty) before you judge a moment. Between you and others: your state shapes how other people's words land, so pick your timing, give people a heads-up, and pause and come back when you need to. Step 1 of 8 · required Pick the one closest to true right now. This is a reading of you, never of anyone else. Hard to tell? That's common. Pick the nearest one; you can change it any time. Where do these three come from? (optional) They're loosely borrowed from polyvagal theory, a popular idea about the nervous system that researchers still debate, which has its own science names for them. Here they're only everyday words for how you feel, not a measurement of your body: Clear means calm and connected, Gusty means revved up, and Fogged in means running on empty. Step 2 of 8 optional Pick one, and the forecast words its advice for them. Any kind of relationship counts. Step 3 of 8 optional How much were you already carrying before whatever just happened? Step 4 of 8 optional The body underneath the mood. Tap anything true today. Step 5 of 8 optional Roughly. Sleep counts for a lot in this reading, because a short night makes almost everything feel heavier. Step 6 of 8 optional How much time with other people has today already asked of you? Step 7 of 8 optional The fronts on the horizon. Tap any that are sitting on you today. Step 8 of 8 optional Pick as many as fit. Naming it is most of the work. Pick the sky in step 1 to begin Waiting Room in the day Show the numbers behind this Answer step 1 and the forecast fills in. Every extra step sharpens it, and none of them are required. The forecast suggests waiting a little. If you like, start a pause here: a quiet countdown on this page, plus something calm to do wh","f":0},{"u":"/quiet-crossword.html","t":"Quiet Crossword: Easy, Gentle Mini Crosswords","h":"Quiet Crossword: easy, gentle mini crosswords","d":"Small, friendly mini crosswords with gentle clues in five levels from easy to tricky. Free and calm with no timer: a peaceful puzzle for a few quiet minutes.","s":["Solved","Across","Down"],"x":"Small crosswords with friendly clues, from gentle to expert. There’s a new themed crossword every day, the same for everyone, and thousands more to play in order or at random. Each word glows when it’s right. No timer, no way to lose, and now and then something new in the garden behind the page. Why it helps: a small puzzle gives your mind one gentle thing to hold, and each right answer is a tiny, satisfying win. Take it slowly.","f":0},{"u":"/quiet-words.html","t":"Calm Word Search, No Timer: Quiet Words (Free)","h":"Quiet Words: a calm word search with no timer","d":"A calm, free word search with soft themes, levels from easy to tricky and no timer. Every word you find leaves a kind thought behind. Play on phone or computer.","s":[],"x":"A gentle word search. There’s no timer, no score and no way to lose. Each word you find glows and leaves a kind thought behind. Music is off unless you turn it on. A new theme each day (the same for everyone), hundreds more to choose from, and the same grid every time you open a puzzle. If you step away, your place and the words you found are kept on this device. Now and then, something new arrives in the garden behind the page. Theme Why it helps: a gentle puzzle gives your mind one simple, absorbing thing to do, which leaves less room for worry. Take it slowly. Notice each word, and the thought it leaves behind. Visit the Night Garden →","f":0},{"u":"/reading.html","t":"Further Reading","h":"Articles worth your time","d":"Hand-picked articles from Psychology Today, Greater Good Magazine, The Gottman Institute and other trusted sources, sorted by topic and by the Five Pillars.","s":["Fresh picks, grouped by topic","Browse all the articles"],"x":"A hand-picked library of articles that go with this program: some help you understand yourself, and some help you get along with the people you share time, space, work or care with. They come from sources we trust: Psychology Today, Greater Good Magazine from UC Berkeley, The Gottman Institute, Harvard Health, ADDitude, CHADD and the National Autistic Society. Each one is sorted by topic and by the Five Pillars it belongs to. The one-line summaries are ours; the articles open on the publishers’ sites in a new tab. Nothing you do on this page is sent anywhere. The only thing kept is a short list of the articles you’ve opened, in this browser, so new picks can skip them. A few articles from each topic, picked at random each time you visit. Filter the list The Five Pillars All pillars The Five Pillars Topic All topics Topic Loading the list… Every article is hand-picked for this program and opens on the publisher’s site. They are for everyday understanding, not medical advice. Found a link that no longer works? They get checked and refreshed from time to time.","f":0},{"u":"/relationships-in-depth.html","t":"How It Fits Your Relationships in depth","h":"How it fits your relationships","d":"How every chapter, workpaper and tool in The Objective Ledger connects to your relationship with yourself, and to partners, family, co-parents, friends.","s":["The map","Yourself","Start with","How the rest connects","Partners","Start with","How the rest connects","Family","Start with","How the rest connects","Co-parents","Start with","Four steps for calmer handoffs","How the rest connects","Friends","Start with","How the rest connects","Roommates","Start with","How the rest connects","Three or more people, and money","Coworkers and teams","Start with","How the rest connects"],"x":"For every road below there’s a workpaper package and report : one fillable PDF with the right workpapers, and a detailed report with findings, recommendations and a plan. The Objective Ledger starts with you. It helps you see your own stress, rhythms and reactions clearly and without shame. Then it gives any two people who share time, space, work or care a fair way to see what each of you carries, so hard conversations can start from shared facts instead of competing impressions. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar I, See the whole load and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person , part of the Five Pillars . In you: notice everything you carry, including the invisible, mental and emotional load. Between you and others: put it on one shared, fair page and give each job a clear owner, instead of blaming anyone. The ideas stay the same across every relationship. What changes is what's being shared: a home, a child, a project, a parent's care, or a friendship's give and take. This page shows how each part of the program connects to each kind of relationship, with an example of what it can look like in real life. Jump to: The map Yourself Partners Family Co-parents Friends Roommates Coworkers Caregivers Core means it was built for this, or it's one of the first things to reach for. Helpful means it works with small adjustments. A dash means it usually isn't the right tool. This is a guide, not a rule: you know your relationships best. Material Yourself Partners Family Co-parents Friends Roommates Coworkers Caregivers The book Preface: Unbilled Debt Invisible work that only one person can see Helpful Core Core Core Helpful Core Core Core Ch. I: Radio Frequency How pace, tone and urgency knock people out of sync Helpful Core Core Core Core Core Core Core Ch. II: P(Solvency) Is the current split of work sustainable? — Core Helpful Core — Core Core Core Ch. III: Autonomic Saturation Leftover stress, and the angle each person argues from Core Core Core Helpful Helpful Helpful Helpful Core Ch. IV: Parity & Gating Agreeing on fairness; letting a comment land first Helpful Core Core Core Helpful Core Core Core Ch. V: The Deficit Audit A monthly look at problems that keep returning — Core Helpful Core — Core Core Core Quick checks and tools Today’s Weather The sky, the pressure and what’s blowing in Core Core Core Core Core Core Core Core The Lemonade Stand This","f":1},{"u":"/relationships.html","t":"Relationship Tools for Couples, Families and Roommates","h":"How it fits your relationships: tools for couples, families and roommates","d":"Free ways to share chores fairly, use kinder words and hold calmer check-ins, for couples, families, co-parents, friends, roommates, coworkers and caregivers.","s":["Start with yourself.","With a partner, the unseen work is often heaviest.","With family, write down who owns what.","Co-parents need calm handoffs and one owner for each task.","With friends, use a light touch.","Roommates need the same picture of the chores and the bills.","At work, the same unseen work shows up.","Caregiving can get heavy quietly.","One map shows it all at once."],"x":"The Objective Ledger starts with you, then gives any two people who share time, space, work or care a fair way to see what each of you carries. Want everything for your relationship in one place? The workpaper package and report gives you one fillable PDF for your road and a detailed report from your answers. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar I, See the whole load and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person , part of the Five Pillars . In you: notice everything you carry, including the invisible, mental and emotional load. Between you and others: put it on one shared, fair page and give each job a clear owner, instead of blaming anyone. Every relationship runs through your own nervous system first. A quick check can show that a snap at 4 p.m. was leftover stress, not about them. It also helps to know the rulebook you each brought from the home you grew up in: many fights are two people following two different sets of unspoken rules. See Where your lens came from , and find how you think, talk and listen with Wavelength . Dig deeper: knowing yourself One of you may track every bill and birthday while the other never sees that work. Start with “Who did what” (WP-01): a week’s record of who did what, written down by each of you. Our guides to invisible labor and the mental load and how to stop fighting with your partner walk through the next steps, and the free printable chore chart for couples gives every job one owner. Dig deeper: where partners start Families often clash on tone and urgency more than on the task itself. Agree who owns what, so nobody has to guess. Never ask a child to keep track of what a parent does. Helping with the grandkids? See the page for grandparents . Dig deeper: where families start A court order or parenting plan always comes first. Inside it, four small habits keep two homes steadier: Keep handoffs short. Use the same few lines on exchange day: times, bags, school things. Kids are never the messengers. One owner per task on a shared calendar: school forms, dentist visits, permission slips. Check a message before you send it with the Tone Filter (WP-09) or the Signal Translator. A short weekly check-in across both homes, about next week only. In you: check your own battery before a handoff. Between you: clear owners and short handoffs instead of blame (Pillars II and III). Dig deeper: the exchange-day script and more O","f":0},{"u":"/roadmap.html","t":"Content Roadmap: What’s Live and What’s Planned","h":"What's live, what's next, and what's planned","d":"What is live today, what is being written and what is planned: the Preface and Chapters I to V are live now, with Chapters VI to XII still to come.","s":["Live now","Manuscript","Workpapers","Tools, calculators & program","Guides, everyday tools & the calm corner","In progress","Next up","Planned","Manuscript","Workpapers","Calculators"],"x":"5 of 12 chapters, 7 of 15 workpapers and 1 of 7 calculators are built and available today. The Preface, Chapters I and II, the guides, Today’s Weather, the Lemonade Stand and the other free tools need no sign-up. Everything else opens free with an email sign-up while the program is being built. Paid membership comes later. Release order and timing below are estimates, not guarantees or contractual commitments. This page is updated as work is actually finished. If it ever disagrees with the Suite Index , the Suite Index is correct. Preface Unbilled Debt free Ch. I The Radio Frequency Paradigm free Ch. II The Epistemic Verdict Engine & P(Solvency) free Ch. III Autonomic Saturation & the 7 Ocular Vectors free · email unlocked Ch. IV Deontological Parity & Sensory Gating free · email unlocked Ch. V The Deficit Audit free · email unlocked WP-01 Field Audit & Neutral Refusals free · email unlocked WP-02 The Battery & Stress Meter free · email unlocked WP-03 One owner per job (RACI Treaty) free · email unlocked WP-04 Deficit Audit free · email unlocked WP-09 Say it so it lands (Tone Filter) free · email unlocked WP-11 The Calm-Down Kit free · email unlocked WP-13 The 90-second check-in (Phase-Locked Loop) free · email unlocked Tool Wiring Card New · a one-page card for how you receive words. Nothing is saved. free Tool The Signal Translator New · how a phrase may land for someone wired differently, with the Wired Differently guide free Guide Check-ins New · one topic, no blame, no criticism of the person, and the steps for a hard conversation free Tool The Carrier Wave Decoder New · a guided session for the moment a conversation goes wrong free Tool The Lemonade Stand Shared household register free Tool Mood Arbitrage A printable plan builder, five scenarios and a four-week practice plan; the full toolkit opens with your email free Tool Frequency Calibration Your natural rhythms for money, decisions, check-ins and recovery free · email unlocked Tool Frequency Sync Visualizer A moving picture of how a daily check-in keeps two people in step free · email unlocked CALC-01 The Solvency Read free CALC-01 Solvency Read (full) New · hours and jobs for 2–8 people, with the math step by step free REPORT-01 The Full Read Your week-by-week record free · email unlocked PROG-01 The 6-Week Program A guided path through the live workpapers free · email unlocked Fill-in Fill-in wo","f":0},{"u":"/self-path.html","t":"Your Self-Discovery Path, Step by Step","h":"Your self-discovery path","d":"The self-discovery path: understand your own load, rhythms and reactions first, with a few short worksheets and free tools you can use on your own.","s":["Why start with yourself","The path, step by step","Check your battery","Get to know your wiring","See where your lens came from","Plan what settles you","Find words for what you feel","Practice kind ways to say no","All of it in one place, with a report","A gentle first week"],"x":"The self path is for you, on your own. No partner, family or team needed. It helps you see what you’re carrying, notice your own rhythms and reactions, and find kind words for them, so that everything else, including how you get along with others, gets a little easier. Every conversation you have runs through your own state first. When your battery is low, the same words land harder. When you know how you’re wired and what settles you, you can ask for what helps instead of hoping people guess. That’s Pillar III, Read your state first , and Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , starting inside you. Self-discovery isn’t self-criticism. The goal is to understand yourself as kindly as you’d want to be understood. Five quick questions that separate what you’re already carrying from what just happened. Try it for a few mornings in a row; patterns show up within a week. Fill in WP-02, How much are you carrying? Read the guide Or take one minute with Today’s Weather Sort a reaction into its three layers: how you’re built, what life taught you, and what kind of day you’re having. Then decide whether to work on it, work with it, or work around it. Know your own wiring Find your Wavelength Make your Wiring Card Chapter III: full tanks and different angles Each stage of growing up left you with understandings about yourself, other people and what they owe you. Write down the rulebook from the home you grew up in, and choose which rules you still want to live by. Where your lens came from Roots & Wings: find where a trait started The one-minute lens check The rulebook from home Decide ahead of time what helps you calm down, so it’s ready when you need it, and how you’ll say “I need a pause, and I’ll come back by…”. Fill in WP-11, the Calm-Down Kit Read the guide Try Drift, the calm visualizer When something stings, turn the raw reaction into a fact, a feeling and a clear ask, before you say or send anything. Fill in WP-09, Say it so it lands Read the guide Try the Signal Translator Part B of WP-01 gives you ready-made, kind ways to say no: say why a request is fair, say what you have left, and offer something instead. Fill in WP-01 (Part B) Read the guide The Individual road of the full path package puts every workpaper for this path into one fillable PDF, along with your Wiring Card and a few weeks of weather and battery notes. Fill it in, bring it back, and get a ","f":0},{"u":"/sent-this.html","t":"Sent this by someone?","h":"","d":"Someone sent you Spread Love & Acceptance? Here is what they can and can’t see, what stays yours, and kind ways to say yes, not yet or no.","s":["What the other person can see","Nothing is entered about you without you","Doing your side privately","Saying yes, not yet, or no, kindly","Want to send it on, or reply warmly?"],"x":"Someone thought of you and shared this site. That’s usually a hopeful sign: they’d like things between you to feel fairer and lighter. Here’s what that does and doesn’t mean, so you can decide in your own time. They can’t see whether you opened this, what you read, or what you type. Nothing you type into the tools or worksheets is sent to them, or to us. There’s no shared account and no linked profiles. Each person’s entries stay in their own browser, on their own device. They only see what you choose to show them: a worksheet you print or save as a PDF and hand over, or a conversation you decide to have. The program is built so each person fills in their own side. The worksheets ask “what did I do” and “how am I doing,” never “rate the other person.” The shared pieces, like agreeing who owns which job, only work when both of you sit down together, and either of you can stop at any time. It isn’t a test, a score of you, or a way to prove who’s right. It looks at the setup: who does what, how full each battery is, and how words land. You’re welcome to look around on your own first, with no one else involved: Today’s Weather : a one-minute read on how you’re doing. Just for you. Start in 10 minutes : the whole idea in four small steps. Your self-discovery path : understanding your own load and wiring, with or without anyone else. Anything you save stays in this browser. You can erase it from the same page, and clearing your browser’s site data removes it too. All three are fine answers. A kind reply usually thanks them for the thought, says where you are, and leaves the door where you want it. Yes, a little “Thanks for sending this. I had a look. I’m up for trying one small part together, maybe the 90-second check-in. Can we start this weekend?” Not yet “I can tell this matters to you, and I appreciate you sharing it. I’m not up for it right now. Can we come back to it in a couple of weeks?” No, thank you “Thank you for thinking of us. This kind of tool isn’t for me, and that’s not about you. I’d still like to talk about how things feel, just in our own way.” Here’s a message you can copy and change however you like. It isn’t sent from here; copy it into your own text or email. A warm invitation Try this: Before you reply, take one minute for yourself. It’s easier to answer kindly when you know how full your own battery is. Check today’s weather → Back to Star","f":0},{"u":"/signal-translator.html","t":"Tone Checker for Texts: See How Your Message Lands","h":"The Signal Translator: a free tone checker for texts","d":"A free tone checker for texts: type a message, see how it may land and what they may hear, then get a kinder, clearer way to say it. Private, on your device.","s":["Type your sentence","The people","The moment","Two optional notes","Talking notes","Check-in","Conflict","Any conversation"],"x":"Tool · Pairs with Wired Differently and Chapter I Type what you plan to say or send. See how it may land, and get a clearer version that keeps your ask. Got a message that stung or confused you? Choose Someone sent me this to read it kindly. Everything else is optional. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , one of the Five Pillars . I’m about to send this Someone sent me this Type it the way it would actually come out. A text, a Slack message, or something you plan to say out loud. Not sure how they saw it? Try the Perspective Shifter first → Want to know how you both send and receive? Find your Wave Codes in Wavelength → Or try an example Fine-tune (optional): who it's for, the channel, the listener's wiring What is this for? Relationship Channel Leave any of these on \"Not sure / skip\". Each one only sharpens the reading. What the relationships mean · Situation and room . You THE SPEAKER · SENDS THE MESSAGE Your name (optional) Person B THE LISTENER · RECEIVES THE MESSAGE Their name (optional) Their wiring is set above, under \"Listener's wiring\". Listener's state right now This is Pillar III, Read your state first : the same words land harder on someone who is stressed or worn out. Not sure? The thirty-second state check helps. Situation Where you are What you need in a hard moment What Person B needs in a hard moment Needs can differ. One person may need the bond confirmed now. The other may need space before they can think. Both can be cared for. A hug is never owed. What happened The event, in plain facts, so the sentence is about one thing. \"The dishes were still in the sink\" is a fact. \"You never help\" is a criticism of the person. What they already said Only if they have already answered. One reply, not the thread. After you translate, Try the reverse shows how you may read them. TAKE THIS INTO THE ROOM Translate one sentence at a time. Add it here, then write the next. This page is what you print. The score stays off it. How to choose what belongs on the list is on Check-ins . 1. OPEN \"We will start with one sentence. I am not against you. I do not think you meant harm. If we pause, we come back at a time we name now.\" Return time, if you need it: _______________ No sentences yet. Translate one above, add it, then use Write the next sentence for the one after that. 3. HEAR BOTH SIDES A productive conversation is two people heard, not one","f":0},{"u":"/snapshot/index.html","t":"A 2-Minute Snapshot of How Your Household Shares the Load","h":"Your 2-minute snapshot","d":"A gentle two-minute look at how the shared load is set up at home and how it bends, for couples, families, roommates and teams. Not a test or a diagnosis.","s":["What you'll get","Then what?"],"x":"Quick check Seven quick questions about how a shared load is set up, and how it bends when things get heavy. The snapshot looks through all Five Pillars , starting with Pillar I, See the whole load and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person . In you: you take an honest look at what you carry. Between you and others: you see the pattern as a setup you can change together, not a fault in anyone. It works for any shared load: a partner, family at home, roommates, people sharing care for someone, or a team at work. It's a kind look at how things are, from your point of view, today. It isn't a test, and it doesn't diagnose anything or anyone. Take the 2-minute snapshot Which of four areas you're in right now: Steady , Ready to readjust , Could bend more or Carrying a lot right now How it was worked out: two simple scores, one for the setup and one for how it bends, shown openly One small step to try this week, and which pillar it belongs to A copy you can print or paste, so each person can compare their own snapshot Nothing you pick is saved or sent. Practice with worked examples in the Workpaper Playground Log a real week with WP-01, Who did what , then see if the load can last with CALC-01 Read the stories from philosophy behind the ideas","f":0},{"u":"/snapshot/snapshot-diagnostic-snapshot-interactive.html","t":"The 2-minute snapshot","h":"","d":"Seven quick questions about how a shared load is set up and how it bends under pressure. You get one of four areas, the scoring shown openly, and one small.","s":[],"x":"Quick check Seven questions about how a shared load is set up, and how it bends when things get heavy. The snapshot looks through all Five Pillars . Questions 1 to 3 are about the setup: Pillar I, See the whole load and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person . Questions 4 to 6 are about how it bends: Pillar III, Read your state first , Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: an honest look at what you carry. Between you and others: a pattern you can change together, not a fault in anyone. This is not a test and not a diagnosis. It's your view, today. It works best when each person takes it on their own and then you compare. Nothing you pick is saved or sent. Who shares this load with you? Your snapshot, today One thing to try this week: Want to go further? This is a snapshot of one moment from one point of view, not a verdict. Answers change as the setup changes, so try it again in a month.","f":0},{"u":"/soundscapes.html","t":"Calm Music and Relaxing Soundscapes","h":"Soundscape Catalog: calm music and relaxing soundscapes","d":"Calm music and relaxing soundscapes: breathing tracks, peaceful ambient pieces and gentle songs about love, acceptance and finding your way back to each other.","s":["1. The Breath Beneath","2. One Breath to Anchor You","3. Nothing Needs to Change","4. The Road We Made","Live soundscapes"],"x":"Audio Every track below has actually been produced and is ready to hear. This page grows one real track at a time, not from a planned track list. A note on what these are: these are ambient, atmospheric audio pieces, not a validated therapeutic or clinical tool. Some people find ambient and low-frequency audio calming as part of a personal listening habit. That's a matter of personal taste, not an established treatment. What this page is, and isn't. This catalog holds functional and ambient audio, plus one grounding song. No workpaper requires audio, and none ever will. Any pairing below is only a suggestion for people who like to listen. Soundscapes sound best with headphones. The deep, low tones and the gentle left-to-right movement are much of what makes them calming, and small speakers can’t carry them. 40 Hz · 80 BPM · Vibroacoustic ambient · 2:55 Your browser can't play this inline. Download the track directly . The design. A pure 40 Hz drone you can almost feel, under an 80 BPM rhythm that matches the pace of a steady, resting breath. How it feels. The low tone sits deep and heavy, a little like a weighted blanket for the ears. It comes through best on good over-ear headphones. Good for. A bridge out of stressful or intensely analytical work: a few minutes to reset before you walk in the door, talk with family or settle into the evening. 40 Hz · 75 BPM · Minimal ambient with guided voice · 2:55 Your browser can't play this inline. Download the track directly . The design. Very spare and spacious. The tempo drops to 75 BPM, with long quiet spaces between the guided words. How it feels. Bright, busy sounds are stripped away, and a wide, soft reverb makes it feel like sitting alone in a large, calm room. Good for. Shutting the world out for three minutes, when the room is too loud or the lights too bright and you need to find your baseline again. 40 Hz · 65 BPM · Deep ambient · 2:56 Your browser can't play this inline. Download the track directly . The design. The slowest and deepest piece so far, at 65 BPM, made with dark, woody tones that sound more like strings than anything digital. How it feels. The pace sits a little below a typical resting heartbeat, and the long, slow swells are easy to breathe along with. Good for. The moment frustration or stress is spiking and you want something steady to hold on to. It pairs well with the Calm-Down Kit (WP-11","f":0},{"u":"/start-here.html","t":"Start Here: Fair Chores, Calm Talks, Kinder Relationships","h":"Start here: understand yourself first, then share the load kindly","d":"New here? The one idea behind it all, the best free first tools, and a gentle six-week path to fairer chores, fewer fights and kinder talks at home.","s":["Your best first step","Four questions that get tangled together","How it works, in order","“Is the setup working for everyone?” is not a relationship score","What’s free now, and what your email opens","Free, no sign-up","Free with sign-up","What this is, and what it isn’t","It isn’t","It is"],"x":"The Objective Ledger is a free, friendly way to see what you carry, read how you’re doing today, and talk about the work of a shared life without anyone being made the problem. It starts with you, on your own, and then helps with anyone you share time, space, work or care with. Pick the line that sounds most like you. Each one is a single page, and nothing needs to be read in order. I have ten minutes Start in 10 minutes: check your weather, read the Preface, try one practice card and log one thing. That’s it. Someone sent me this What the other person sees, what stays yours, and how to say yes, not yet or no kindly. We’re too worked up to talk right now Today’s Weather: a one-minute read on your own state, and whether now is a good time to talk. We keep going round about chores Who did what: one week’s simple log of the work, written down by both of you instead of remembered by one. I’d like to be walked through it Six gentle weeks: one small step a week, in order, with a tracker that remembers where you are if you’d like it to. I want to understand the ideas first The book, starting with the Preface: the quiet, unseen work of running a shared life. I’m not sure which part of the program to use Six weeks, the Workpaper Suite, the package and report, and the indexes, side by side: which to use when. Most arguments about the work of a home are really four separate questions being argued at once. The program pulls them apart so each one can be answered on its own. What actually happened? The work itself, written down by both people rather than remembered by one. What state was each person in? How much each of you was already carrying before a word was said. Who was responsible? The named owner of a job, rather than whoever happened to notice first. What did each person mean? What someone meant to say, and how it landed. The two are often not the same. Each step answers one of those questions. Pick them up one at a time. Starting them all at once turns into paperwork, and that is the most common reason people stop in week two. See the work Who did what WP-01 · also called the Field Audit What is actually happening? Week 1, a few minutes a day for seven days. Check your state How much are you carrying? WP-02 · also called the Battery & Stress Meter What is each of us bringing to this? Daily from week 1, and before any hard conversation. Too wound up? Settle firs","f":0},{"u":"/start-in-10-minutes.html","t":"Start in 10 minutes","h":"","d":"One short, straight path through the whole idea: check your weather, read the Preface, try one practice card, and log one thing you did today.","s":["Check your own weather","Read the Preface, the simple version","Try one practice card","Log one thing you did today"],"x":"One short, straight path through the whole idea. Four small steps, in order. There’s nothing to sign up for, and nothing you type here leaves your device. About 1 minute · Pillar III, read your state first Before anything else, see how you’re doing today. One required tap, and up to seven optional ones, give you a forecast and whether now is a good time for a hard talk. Check today’s weather It opens in this tab, with a bar at the top that brings you straight back to step 2 when you’re done. About 4 minutes · Pillar I, see the whole load The work nobody sees: the quiet, unseen jobs that keep a shared life running, and why they deserve to be noticed. A few short cards, one idea each. or open the Preface page About 3 minutes · Pillar IV, tune how you send and receive Say it so it lands: turn a big feeling into a fact, a feeling and a kind ask. “You never take the trash out.” Fact: The trash has gone out late three weeks running. Feeling: I feel worn out when I notice it every time. Ask: Could the trash be yours on Tuesdays? Now try one of your own (it isn’t saved or sent) Want more? The full worksheet is Say it so it lands . About 1 minute · Pillar I, see the whole load Just one. Something you did that kept things running, however small: packed a lunch, paid a bill, remembered a birthday. That’s the whole idea in small: see the work, notice your state, and say things so they land. When you’re ready for more, the Lemonade Stand logs a whole week. What next? If that felt useful, six gentle weeks walks you through the rest, one small step a week. See six gentle weeks → Back to Start here","f":0},{"u":"/suite-index-in-depth.html","t":"Suite Index in depth — What's Actually Live","h":"What's Actually Live","d":"An honest index of everything that is live today in The Objective Ledger Workpaper Suite: each chapter, worksheet and calculator you can use right now.","s":["Free, Public","Manuscript","The Order to Use Them In","Workpapers","Calculator & Program","What Nothing Here Will Ever Use","Not Yet Built"],"x":"Suite Index A map of everything live today. Nothing more, nothing less. Everything listed below exists, has been reviewed, and is ready to use today. Each row shows how you get it. Page means it has its own page on the site. Bundle means it arrives in the member PDF. Most items are both. For the counts behind this list (how much of the planned whole is built) see the Rollout Status . What this index covers: the manuscript, the workpapers and the calculators, which are the tools the framework is built from. Audio is a separate project with its own catalog and is deliberately not listed here. No workpaper requires audio, and none ever will. Ch. I–II The Radio Frequency Paradigm & The Epistemic Verdict Engine Free chapters of the manuscript, in full, on their own pages. The Preface is free to read too. Page Tool The Lemonade Stand Heuristic & Today’s Weather Free interactive tools, each on its own page. Today’s Weather holds the one-minute nervous-system self-check (once called the Nervous System Ladder). The Lemonade Stand totals feed the workload balance input in CALC-01. Page Tool Wiring Card wiring-card.html — fill in how you receive words, what silence means, what to avoid, and how a request should be phrased. Print or copy. Nothing is saved. Not a diagnosis. Added September 24, 2026. Page Tool The Signal Translator signal-translator.html — choose each person's neurotype (or a mix), the relationship, the situation, the setting and a phrase. You can also add the plain facts and one reply. It shows how the phrase may land, plus a way to meet both people's needs. Runs in the browser; nothing is saved. Added September 24, 2026. Page Tool The Carrier Wave Decoder carrier-wave-decoder.html — a guided session for the moment a conversation starts going sideways: find what slipped on pace, register or urgency, settle, and try again. Notes stay in your own browser. Page Guide Turning Toward turning-toward.html — seven small, everyday ways to feel closer to the people who matter to you, with a 7-day start. A reading page, not a tool. Page Guide Wired Differently wired-differently.html — how neurotypical, autistic, ADHD, dyslexic, dyspraxic, auditory processing (APD), developmental language disorder (DLD), Tourette, alexithymic, highly sensitive, anxious, trauma-wired and OCD receivers take in the same words, with sources. Added September 24, 2026. Page Guide Check-in","f":1},{"u":"/suite-index.html","t":"Suite Index","h":"What's Actually Live","d":"The official list of what’s built today. If something is listed here, it’s live and ready to use. If it isn’t listed, it isn’t live yet.","s":["Some of it is free for anyone.","The book has a preface and five chapters so far.","Seven workpapers are live.","Use them in order, not all at once.","One calculator, one record, one program.","Some things aren’t built yet."],"x":"The official list of what’s built today. If something is listed here, it’s live and ready to use. If it isn’t listed, it isn’t live yet. Looking for where to begin? See which part of the program to use . The Preface and Chapters I and II, the Lemonade Stand, Today’s Weather, the Wiring Card, the Signal Translator, the Carrier Wave Decoder, the Wired Differently, Check-ins and Turning Toward guides, the Program Overview and a printable infographic. Also free: the Five Pillars, Know your own wiring, the Professor’s Library, the long form of “Is the setup working for everyone?” (CALC-01) for 2–8 people, the articles page, the quest map, Drift and the calm games. Each has its own page on the site. Dig deeper: the free, public list Each one has its own page on the site. The Preface and Chapters I and II are free to read, and Chapters III to V open free with your email. They are also collected in the member bundle, a single PDF. Dig deeper: each chapter and what it covers Workpapers are worksheets you fill in: WP-01, 02, 03, 04, 09, 11 and 13. They open free with your email. The numbers have gaps on purpose, because they are slots in a bigger plan. Fill any of them in on screen or as a fillable PDF, or do them all in the Workpaper Suite , in the order that fits your relationship. Dig deeper: each workpaper and when to use it WP-01, then WP-02, WP-03, WP-09 and WP-13, with WP-04 once a month and WP-11 whenever either of you is too wound up to talk. If you are new, start with WP-01 and keep one week of it before touching anything else. Dig deeper: the order and where to start The Solvency Read (CALC-01) gives a single read on whether the way you split the work can last. REPORT-01 keeps your weekly record. PROG-01 walks you through six weeks. Dig deeper: the calculator and the program Chapters VI through XII, the remaining workpapers, and CALC-02 through CALC-07 are not yet written. New things are added to the list the day they go live. Dig deeper: what’s not built yet Try this: If you’re new, begin with WP-01 and keep one week of it before touching anything else. Start WP-01 →","f":0},{"u":"/telemetry.html","t":"Rollout Status","h":"","d":"Component-by-component build status for The Objective Ledger, stated as real fractions of the promised whole.","s":["Public Diagnostic Tools","Six-Week Implementation Curriculum","Tactical Workpaper Suite","Core Manuscript","Interactive Calculators"],"x":"The Objective Ledger · The Objective Ledger Build status for each part of the program, shown as real fractions of the planned whole. The percentages are simple counts, not estimates of effort or confidence. 5 / 12 Chapters 7 / 15 Workpapers 1 / 7 Calculators Live 5 of 5 built · free, no account required 100% Built: The Lemonade Stand Heuristic · Today’s Weather (the nervous-system self-check) · The Printable Infographic · The Signal Translator · Wiring Card Scope change, September 24, 2026: this count went from 4 of 4 to 5 of 5 when the Wiring Card was added. The Wiring Card does not diagnose anything. Wired Differently and Check-ins remain reading pages and are not counted as tools. Also live and free, not counted here: the Carrier Wave Decoder , the Conversation Reader , Turning Toward , Chat with Professor Puddles and the calm games in Pause & Play . Like the audio, they sit outside the totals the framework was designed around. Live 6 of 6 weeks written · PROG-01 100% Built: All six weeks, with reading, checkpoints, and a before-and-after comparison in week six. It puts the live workpapers in order rather than adding new material. In Progress 7 of 15 built 47% Built: WP-01 Field Audit · WP-02 Battery & Stress Meter · WP-03 RACI Treaty · WP-04 Deficit Audit · WP-09 Tone Filter · WP-11 The Calm-Down Kit · WP-13 90-second check-in (Phase-Locked Loop) Fill-in: all seven fill in on screen, download as fillable PDFs, and come together in the Workpaper Suite , built around your road. Remaining: WP-05 through WP-08, WP-10, WP-12, WP-14, WP-15 — topics not yet finalized. In Progress 5 of 12 chapters built, plus the Preface 42% Built: Preface · I. The Radio Frequency Paradigm · II. The Epistemic Verdict Engine · III. Autonomic Saturation & the 7 Ocular Vectors · IV. Deontological Parity & Sensory Gating · V. The Deficit Audit Remaining: Chapters VI through XII — topics not yet finalized. Chapters I and II are free to read publicly. In Progress 1 of 7 built 14% Built: CALC-01 The Solvency Read — runs in the browser, stores nothing, sends nothing. Remaining: CALC-02 through CALC-07 — topics not yet finalized. How to read this. Each bar is a plain count divided by the total the framework was designed around — twelve chapters, fifteen workpapers, seven calculators. Nothing is weighted by difficulty, and no component is marked complete before it goes live. Totals never ","f":0},{"u":"/turning-toward-in-depth.html","t":"Turning Toward in depth","h":"Turning Toward","d":"The everyday practices that build connection: noticing bids, specific thanks, celebrating good news, the stress-reducing conversation, small rituals, knowing.","s":["1. Notice bids, and turn toward them","2. Say thank you, specifically","3. Celebrate good news","4. The stress-reducing conversation","5. Small rituals of connection","6. Know their world","7. Remember what you like about them","Across every kind of relationship","If you’re wired differently","Where this comes from"],"x":"A Tuesday, nothing wrong Maya is on the sofa. She laughs and holds up her phone: “Look at this.” You’re halfway through a message. You could say “mm” without looking up. Or you could lean over, look, and laugh with her. It feels like too small a moment to matter. But most of your days together are ordinary Tuesdays like this one, not birthdays or big talks. These tiny moments add up to most of your time together, so how you answer them quietly shapes how close you feel. Researchers who followed newlywed couples for six years saw this clearly. The couples who were still together had turned toward these small “look at this” moments most of the time. The couples who split up had done it only about a third of the time. Most of this program is about what to do when something has gone wrong: the log, the treaty, the audit, the check-in. This page is about the other side, the part that makes the hard conversations easier when they come. Research on couples, friends and families keeps finding the same thing: connection is built less in big conversations than in small, ordinary moments, answered warmly, many times over. These practices are for every kind of relationship on this site, not only partners. None of this replaces fairness. Warmth doesn’t make an unfair split of work acceptable, and a fair split doesn’t make up for coldness. The two support each other. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives , part of the Five Pillars . In you: notice the small moments when someone reaches for you, and what you tend to do by default. Between you and others: answer those moments warmly, and let specific thanks keep things steady. A bid is any small reach for attention, interest or support: “look at this,” a sigh, a joke, a question about your day, a hand on the shoulder, a link sent with no comment. The researcher John Gottman and colleagues observed that people answer bids in one of three ways: Response What it looks like How it tends to land Turning toward Looking up, answering, asking one question, a small “oh nice” “You’re here with me.” Turning away Not noticing, staying on the phone, changing the subject “I don’t matter much right now.” Turning against Irritation: “Can’t you see I’m busy?” “Reaching for you costs me.” In their studies, couples who more often turned toward each other’s ever","f":1},{"u":"/turning-toward.html","t":"Turning Toward: 7 Small Habits for Closer Relationships","h":"Turning Toward: small habits for closer relationships","d":"Connection grows in small moments. Seven simple habits for love and appreciation: notice when someone reaches for you, give specific thanks, and repair gently.","s":["Say one specific thank-you","Notice when they reach for you.","Say thank you, specifically.","Celebrate their good news.","Be on their side about outside stress.","Keep a few small rituals.","Keep learning their world.","Remember what you like about them.","Your 7-day start"],"x":"Feeling close to someone isn’t built mostly on big talks or special dates. It’s built in tiny, everyday moments: a joke, a sigh, a “look at this.” Turning toward simply means noticing those little moments and reaching back. Here are seven easy ways to do it, with a partner, a friend, family, a roommate or a coworker. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive and Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives , part of the Five Pillars . In you: notice the small moments when someone reaches for you, and what you tend to do by default. Between you and others: answer those moments warmly, and let specific thanks keep things steady. TODAY’S SMALL MOMENT Who’s it for? Dig deeper: why specific thanks works Change anything in [square brackets] to fit. A new idea appears each day. Nothing you pick is sent anywhere. A Tuesday, nothing wrong Maya is on the sofa. She laughs and holds up her phone: “Look at this.” You’re halfway through a message. You could say “mm” without looking up. Or you could lean over, look, and laugh with her. It feels like too small a moment to matter. But most of your days together are ordinary Tuesdays like this one, not birthdays or big talks. These tiny moments add up to most of your time together, so how you answer them quietly shapes how close you feel. A “look at this,” a sigh, a joke, a “guess what happened today”: each one is a small reach for your attention. Look up and answer, even for ten seconds. It says “you matter to me.” If you really can’t right now, say when you can: “Give me two minutes, then I want to hear.” Dig deeper: bids, and three ways to answer them “Thanks for everything” is nice. “Thanks for handling the school forms. I know that ate your whole lunch break” is better, because it shows you noticed. A specific thank-you is how invisible effort finally gets seen. Dig deeper: why specific thanks works When they share something good, match their excitement and ask one question: “No way! What did your boss say?” Save any worries or “buts” for another day. Being happy with someone is one of the fastest ways to feel close. Dig deeper: four ways people respond, and the one that helps Spend a few minutes on what’s weighing on them from outside: work, family, the news. This isn’t about the two of you. Your job is to listen and be on their side, not to fix it. Not sure what they need? Ask: “Do you wan","f":0},{"u":"/wavelength.html","t":"Wavelength: Free Communication Style Quiz and…","h":"Wavelength: how you think, talk and listen","d":"A free quiz on how you think, talk and listen. Pick your wiring (ADHD, autistic, AuDHD and more) and get your four-letter Wave Code and archetype.","s":["What’s in Wavelength","The four Wave Code letters","The 16 Wavelength archetypes","Sharing your Wavelength"],"x":"Wavelength helps you understand how you think and how you communicate, both giving and receiving. Pick your wiring if you like, tap through four short parts, and get your four-letter Wave Code and archetype. Then read sixteen self-discovery chapters written for your answers, explore how the nine inputs of learning connect, and build a warm personal statement to share. How it works: Mostly tapping, about ten minutes. Your wiring step is optional and you can select more than one. Every part shows options that are common for the wiring you picked, and you can open the rest anytime. Wavelength replaces Your Heartprint: the personal statement and its “Things you might not have noticed” are now Part 7. Nothing you type or choose is sent anywhere. Your answers and journal stay on this device only if you tick the box, and “Erase everything” removes them. Your wiring (optional). Neurotypical, ADHD, Autistic, Dyslexic, Highly sensitive, Not sure yet, or Something else, in any combination. ADHD and Autistic together is often called AuDHD. It only decides which options you see first. It’s self-identified, and it isn’t a diagnosis: a doctor or psychologist can do a formal assessment if you want one. Your Wave Code. Four parts, each with three either/or questions and tap-to-select statements: how your brain gets going (pace), how you think things through (lens), how you send what you mean, and how you take things in. The 9 inputs of learning. Words, logic and numbers, pictures and space, hands and movement, sound and rhythm, people, inner reflection, nature and patterns, and big questions and meaning. Pick the ones that help things click, then your strongest. What helps me feel understood, and your statement (optional): a short, warm note about how you work best, with connections between your choices you may not have noticed. Sixteen self-discovery chapters: seven for your Wave Code (your speed, your lens, your voice, how you listen, your energy and your senses, masks and scripts, asking for what helps) and nine, one for each input. Each has a reading written for you, journal prompts and one thing to try this week. How the 9 inputs connect: a map from the obvious links to the most hidden, three flowcharts, and a nine-point check before a hard conversation. Your Wave Code is four letters, a bit like the four-letter personality types you may have seen. It’s a fun way to des","f":0},{"u":"/ways-in-in-depth.html","t":"Ways In in depth — Free While It’s Being Built","h":"Ways in","d":"How to get into The Objective Ledger: open access with no sign-up, everything free with an email address, and paid membership coming later.","s":["Open Access","Free with Email","Paid Membership","What each level shares, and with whom","Open Everything Free"],"x":"The whole site is free while the program is being built. Open access needs nothing from you. An email address opens everything else, and paid membership is coming later. Access The whole site is free while the program is being built. Open access needs nothing from you. An email address opens everything else. Paid membership is coming later. Neither free option asks you to create an account or a password. Free · no sign-up The Carrier Wave Decoder , a guided practice tool The Signal Translator and the Wiring Card , plus the Wired Differently and Check-ins guides The Lemonade Stand and Today’s Weather Turning Toward , the Conversation Reader and Chat with Professor Puddles The calm games in Pause & Play The Infographic Executive Summary — a printable one-pager The Preface and Chapters I & II of the manuscript These stay free because a quick, honest read on yourself and your household shouldn't require payment. They give you a quick snapshot, not the full system. Start with the free tools Free · email only Everything in Open Access Chapters III–V of the manuscript All seven live workpapers: WP-01, 02, 03, 04, 09, 11 and 13 REPORT-01, your week-by-week record, and PROG-01, the 6-week program The full Mood Arbitrage toolkit, Frequency Calibration and the Sync Visualizer A note by email when something new goes live Buttondown, our newsletter service, holds your email address and uses it only for program updates. You can unsubscribe from any email. Open everything free Coming later A paid membership will come once more of the program is built: Chapters VI–XII, the remaining workpapers, and CALC-02 to CALC-07 are still being written. Nothing is charged now. Everyone on the email list will hear about paid membership before anything changes. Exactly what's live is always on the Suite Index . Open Access: nothing you type into the tools leaves your browser. Free with email: your email address, held by Buttondown. This browser also remembers that you signed up, so pages stay open. Paid membership (when it launches): your name, email, and payment details, held by Gumroad. We never see your card details. Nothing is sold or used for advertising. Full details are in the Privacy Policy . Join This program is still being built, and while it is, all of it is free. Enter your email and every chapter, workpaper and tool on the site opens in this browser. There's no password and ","f":1},{"u":"/ways-in.html","t":"Ways In: Free While It’s Being Built","h":"Ways in","d":"Everything here is free while it is being built. Most pages need nothing at all, a free email sign-up opens the rest, and paid membership comes later.","s":["Most of the site is open to anyone.","Your email opens the rest, free.","Paid membership comes later.","You share very little."],"x":"The whole site is free while the program is being built. Most of it needs nothing from you. An email address opens everything else, and paid membership is coming later. No sign-up is needed. That includes the Carrier Wave Decoder, the Signal Translator, the Wiring Card, the guides, the quick checks, and the Preface and Chapters I and II. Dig deeper: what’s open to everyone Chapters III–V, all seven live workpapers, REPORT-01, PROG-01 and the full tools. There is no account and no password. Dig deeper: what an email opens You won’t be charged anything now. Everyone on the email list will hear about paid membership before anything changes. Dig deeper: the plan for paid membership What you type into the tools stays in your browser. If you sign up, Buttondown (our newsletter service) holds your email address. Nothing is sold or used for advertising. Dig deeper: what each level shares, and with whom Try this: If you want everything open, enter your email once. It takes a few seconds. Open everything free →","f":0},{"u":"/whats-new.html","t":"What’s new","h":"","d":"Everything newly added to the site, and everything that’s grown: new pages, new games, new sounds, and parts of the program that go deeper than before.","s":["🌊 Newest: Wavelength","🌞 Easier to find your way","🌱 Newly added","🌊 Expanded and deeper"],"x":"The site keeps growing. Here’s everything newly added, and everything that’s been expanded, with the newest first. Tap any card to go straight there. Added on October 2, 2026 . A fuller way to understand how you think, talk and listen. Wavelength replaces Your Heartprint NEW October 2, 2026 Pick your wiring, find your Wave Code, read your self-discovery chapters. Choose any mix of wiring (or none), tap through four short parts for your four-letter Wave Code and archetype, explore how the nine inputs of learning connect, and compare codes with someone. Your Heartprint statement is still there as Part 7, and your saved answers came with you. Meet Kip the Paperclip NEW October 3, 2026 A small, friendly paperclip now sits by the Share buttons. Tap him for a trick (he dances, spins, bends into a heart and more), and he does a happy dance when you pass a page along. Don’t want him? Switch him off in Settings. Added on September 29, 2026 . Clearer ways in, kinder reading, and a few things that were getting in the way, fixed. Where your lens came from NEW September 29, 2026 How each stage of growing up shapes what you expect of yourself and others, why timing matters, and the rulebook from home. Woven into Know your own wiring and the self path. Start in 10 minutes NEW September 29, 2026 One short, straight path: check your weather, read the Preface, try one practice card and log one thing. Nothing to sign up for. Sent this by someone? NEW September 29, 2026 What the other person sees, what stays yours, doing your side privately, and a warm way to say yes, not yet or no. There’s a ready-to-copy invitation too. A tracker for six gentle weeks NEW September 29, 2026 Turn it on to keep your start date and tick off each week, with a “pick up where you left off” card. It stays on your device, and Erase removes it. Which part of the program to use NEW September 29, 2026 Six weeks, the Workpaper Suite, the package and report, and the indexes, side by side, with which to use when. Plain names and their technical names NEW September 29, 2026 Every tool’s everyday name beside the technical name you may meet on the Full pages, so the two never get confused. Search, and a real contents list EASIER September 29, 2026 Search every page’s title and words from the menu, right on your device. The Contents page now lists every page, and each chapter shows where you are, with previous ","f":0},{"u":"/wired-differently-in-depth.html","t":"Wired Differently in depth","h":"Wired Differently","d":"How autistic, ADHD, dyslexic, anxious, highly sensitive and neurotypical people can hear the same sentence in very different ways, with examples and research.","s":["An honest note before you read on","A few words, used carefully","The problem runs in both directions","Three channels, tuned differently","Neurotypical","How information arrives","How things can sound","Speaking to this wiring","When they're the one talking","Autistic","How information arrives","How things can sound","Speaking to this wiring","When they're the one talking","ADHD","How information arrives","How things can sound","Speaking to this wiring","When they're the one talking","Autistic and ADHD together","Speaking to this wiring","When they're the one talking","Dyslexic","How information arrives"],"x":"How different neurotypes receive the same words, and how to speak so the message you meant is the message that arrives. A text, read twice “Fine.” One of you meant it literally: that is fine, go ahead. The other saw the period and heard a door closing. Same four letters, two entirely different messages. Chapter I describes a household argument as two radios tuned close to, but not exactly on, the same frequency. This page adds a second layer to that picture. Even when two people are tuned to the same station, the receivers themselves are built differently. One receiver picks up every word literally. One loses the second half of a long sentence. One hears a flat tone as anger, and another hears a period at the end of a text as a slammed door. None of these receivers is broken. They were built to different specifications, and the static comes from not knowing how the other person's receiver is built. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , part of the Five Pillars . In you: know your own wiring, pace and how you hear things. Between you and others: translate across different wiring and tone. A mismatch is a tuning problem, not a moral failing. Below you'll find how each common wiring tends to take in information, a set of examples showing how the same words can sound different to different people, and practical ways to talk to someone wired differently than you. When you want to test a real sentence, the Signal Translator lets you pick both people's wiring, type a phrase, and see how it might land. Open the Signal Translator Free. Nothing you type is saved or sent. Everything here describes tendencies that research and lived experience report across groups. It does not describe any one person. Two autistic people can be as different from each other as any two neurotypical people, and many people carry more than one of these wirings at once. This page is not a diagnostic tool and can't tell you what anyone has. The most reliable source of information about how someone receives words is that person, so use this page to find better questions to ask, not to answer those questions for them. Neurotype means the general way a brain is built to take in, sort and respond to information. Neurotypical describes the wiring most social customs were designed around. Neurodivergent describes wiring that differs from that design in a m","f":1},{"u":"/wired-differently.html","t":"Wired Differently: Neurodiversity and Communication","h":"Wired Differently: neurodiversity and communication","d":"How ADHD, autistic and other differently wired minds can hear the same words differently, and simple ways to share plans, chores and feedback that land.","s":["A mix-up usually runs both ways.","For some people, too much gets through.","Some people feel plenty but can’t find the word.","The relationship changes how words land.","The setting matters, and so do both people’s needs.","Tell each other how you’re built, before a hard talk."],"x":"The same words can land very differently, depending on how each person is wired. This guide helps you speak so the message you meant is the message that arrives. Where this fits: This page belongs to Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive , part of the Five Pillars . In you: know your own wiring, pace and how you hear things. Between you and others: translate across different wiring and tone. A mismatch is a tuning problem, not a moral failing. A text, read twice “Fine.” One of you meant it literally: that is fine, go ahead. The other saw the period and heard a door closing. Same four letters, two entirely different messages. When two differently wired people misread each other, it is not one person’s fault. Each of you is decoding with a receiver built for your own kind of signal. Compare notes instead of looking for who is to blame. Dig deeper: the double empathy problem The full guide walks through 14 wirings, one by one. Highly sensitive people, for example, pick up tone and mood others miss, so a slightly clipped “Sure.” can sting for hours. A soft start, a quiet room and one topic help. Dig deeper: the highly sensitive profile For them, “How does that make you feel?” can feel like a quiz with no right answer. Ask about the body or actions instead: “More like tired, or more like annoyed? Space or company?” Dig deeper: the alexithymia profile The same sentence can be a loose plan between friends and a broken agreement between partners. A hint between friends risks a little awkwardness. Between co-parents, it can feel like someone is keeping notes. Dig deeper: seven kinds of relationship A calm kitchen can hold a hard talk. A group chat or a 1 a.m. text cannot. If one of you needs closeness and the other needs space, say you are not leaving, name a clock time, and come back. Dig deeper: situation, room and caring for both needs A Wiring Card says how you take words in, what your silence means, and how a request should be phrased. Swap cards on an ordinary day, not in the middle of an argument. Dig deeper: make a wiring card Try this: Each of you fills in a Wiring Card, then you read them to each other on a calm day. Nothing you type is saved. Make your Wiring Card → For everyday tips at home, read neurodivergent relationships: ADHD, autistic and AuDHD couples and families . See also: Know your own wiring , for sorting a reaction into lasting wiring, lea","f":0},{"u":"/wiring-card.html","t":"Wiring Card: A One-Page “How I Communicate” Card","h":"Wiring Card","d":"Make a free one-page card about how you take in words, what your silence means, what to avoid and how a request lands best, to share with people you love.","s":["The two cards, side by side"],"x":"The Objective Ledger Tools ← Read: Wired Differently Tool · Pairs with the wiring card in Wired Differently Fill in how you actually receive words. Your card updates as you go: beside the form on a wide screen, and below it on a phone. Print it, or copy it, and give it to the people you live and work with before a hard conversation, not during one. Runs in your browser. Nothing you type is sent anywhere. A draft is kept in this tab only until you close it (Clear removes it sooner), and nothing is kept longer unless you tick “Keep my card on this device” (Erase removes it). This is not a diagnosis and not a label: it describes what helps words reach you. When you are running low, every line applies more strictly. The Wiring Card puts Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive to work. In you: you name your own wiring, pace and how you hear things. Between you and others: people learn how to send a request so it lands, and a mismatch becomes tuning, not a moral failing. See also: Know your own wiring , which helps you tell lasting wiring from learned patterns and today’s conditions, and Wavelength , which finds your four-letter Wave Code for how you think, talk and listen. Rows where your picks don’t overlap are shaded gold: not a problem, just the best places for a short talk. Rows you share are shaded green. What this is for. A card is a simple note about how you take in words. It is not a label, not permission to criticize, and not proof of bad intent. It tells someone how to send a request so it gets through. It doesn't make them responsible for managing you, and it doesn't mean you have to be available no matter how you feel. Stick to one topic at a time. The ground rules explain how to use what you wrote here in a conversation. Pair the card with the Signal Translator when you want to test one sentence, and with Check-ins when the topic needs a calm, safe space. Wired Differently explains the ideas behind it.","f":0},{"u":"/word-bloom.html","t":"Word Bloom: A Relaxing Letter-Wheel Word Game","h":"Word Bloom: a relaxing word game","d":"A free, relaxing word game: swipe across a wheel of letters to find the hidden words. Hundreds of levels from easy to tricky, with no timers and no ads.","s":["Level bloomed"],"x":"Swipe across the petals to spell words and fill the little crossword. Any everyday word that fits a slot fills it, even if it isn’t the one we had in mind, and other real words go in your bonus jar. No timer, no way to lose, and now and then something new in the garden behind the page. Thousands of wheels, so you’ll rarely see the same one twice. Level 1 0/10 Bonus jar How to play: drag your finger from petal to petal to spell a word, then let go. Slide back to undo a letter. You can also tap petals one at a time and tap the middle, or type on a keyboard. 🔀 mixes up the letters, and 💡 shows one letter, as often as you like. If spelling is tiring, turn on “First letters” to see where each word starts, or tap “Hear a word” to hear one of the words out loud.","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/calculators/calc01-solvency.html","t":"Is the Chore Split Fair? A Free Calculator (CALC-01)","h":"Is the setup working for everyone?","d":"Type in the totals from your chore worksheets, for 2 to 8 people, and see in plain words whether the way you share the household load is working for everyone.","s":["Who's sharing the load?","1. Balance: how the hours split workload balance · weight 0.40 (40%)","2. Ownership: does each job have a name? ownership clarity · weight 0.35 (35%)","3. Stress: how much each person is carrying average load · weight 0.25 (25%)","The math, step by step","What would help most"],"x":"CALC-01 · The quick read Also called the solvency read. Works for 2 to 8 people. Type in the totals from your worksheets and see, in plain words, whether the way you share the load right now is working. It scores the setup , never a person, and never the relationship as a whole. It works for partners, families, roommates, co-carers and small teams: 2 to 8 people. (\"Solvency\" just means the setup can carry its own weight.) CALC-01 puts Pillar I, See the whole load and Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work, with a battery check from Pillar III, Read your state first . In you: you see the whole load you carry as one number. Between you and others: you get one fair read of the setup, never a score on anyone. Want each input worked out from your raw entries, the apex score and a trend log? Use the full CALC-01 . The math is the same on both pages. Three steps, each counted by how much it matters to the read: balance counts for 40%, ownership for 35% and stress for 25% (the “weights” 0.40, 0.35 and 0.25 you see beside each step). From WP-01, Who did what, and kind ways to say no or the Lemonade Stand : each person's total hours (or minutes) for the week. I have… Each person's hours A balance score already worked out (0 to 1) Compare with a split we agreed on, instead of an even share Balance score From WP-03, One owner per job : count every regular job you listed yourself, including the ones nobody owns yet, and how many of them have an owner. Starter examples you never filled in don’t count. Jobs on the list Jobs with an owner From WP-02, How much are you carrying? : each person's own score, their five 0–4 answers added up and divided by 20. It runs from 0 to 1, and higher means heavier. Everyone fills in their own; nobody guesses for someone else. How the setup is working — Show the numbers Fill in the three steps above to see a read. Part Could earn Earned Shortfall The words and their cut-offs: 0.70 or more, working well. 0.40 up to 0.70, needs a look. Below 0.40, needs a rethink, together. Here higher means the setup is working better. Read from the score rounded to two decimals, so the number and the words always agree. This is a rule-of-thumb worksheet, not a tested model or a compatibility test. It only reflects what you enter, and it can't decide whether a relationship is worth continuing. Nothing you type is saved or sent. Read the reasoning i","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/report-01-in-depth.html","t":"REPORT-01 in depth — The Full Read","h":"The Full Read WHERE ALL YOUR WORKSHEETS COME TOGETHER","d":"Where all your worksheets come together: one place to record each week's numbers, and a report written for you on how they fit together.","s":["Names","Add a Week","From the weekly closing","The weeks so far","Add a Month","From WP-04, the Deficit Audit","The months so far","The Full Read","What the figures say to each other","The shape of it so far","What to do next","How This Report Is Written"],"x":"REPORT-01 One place to record what your worksheets produced, and a report that reads them side by side instead of one at a time. This report brings all Five Pillars together: the load (I), how the setup is holding (II), your load scores (III), how repair is going (IV), and the jobs that quietly slip back (V). In you: you see your own patterns over time. Between you and others: you get one shared picture of the setup, never a verdict on either person. Where the numbers come from: the fill-in workpapers · CALC-01 · Build your Workpaper Suite Each worksheet answers one question well, but it can't see the others. WP-01 can't see whether each job has an owner. WP-03 can't see whether the work is shared fairly. CALC-01 reads three of them and still can't see whether last month's fixes held. This page is where they're read together. Type in what your worksheets produced. It takes about a minute a week. The page then writes what no single worksheet can: where the numbers agree, where they disagree, and which disagreement matters. Your ledger is a file, not a login. Nothing here is stored in this browser, and nothing you type or choose is sent anywhere. Press Save ledger to download a small file. Next week, press Load ledger to pick up where you left off. That's on purpose. A household's records shouldn't sit in the browser of a computer two people share. A file you keep is one you can put somewhere private, copy, or delete completely. Setup Person A Person B Capture Everything here comes from a worksheet you've already filled in. Leave blank anything you didn't do this week. The report works around gaps instead of guessing. Week of WP-01 minutes · A Field Audit total WP-01 minutes · B Field Audit total Noticed & handled without being asked Count, either person WP-02 battery · A Five scores added, ÷ 20 WP-02 battery · B Five scores added, ÷ 20 WP-03 tasks owned Both R and A named WP-03 tasks listed Total rows WP-09 friction events WP-09 retunes used WP-13 loops run WP-13 items moved on To WP-03 or WP-09 WP-11 kit used Times this week WP-03 treaty changes Treaty changes logged Week of WB OC AS RF Solv. Δ Apex Loops No weeks recorded yet. Key: WB = workload balance (WP-01). OC = ownership clarity (WP-03). AS = autonomic saturation, how stretched you both are (WP-02). RF = retuning frequency (WP-09). Solv. = solvency, how well the setup holds up (CALC-01). Δ = change in","f":1},{"u":"/workpapers/report-01.html","t":"Your progress, week by week (REPORT-01)","h":"Your progress, week by week","d":"One place to type in what your worksheets produced each week, and a report that reads them side by side to show where they agree and where they don’t.","s":["Your ledger is a file you keep.","Once a week, copy in your numbers.","Once a month, add the monthly look-back (WP-04).","Read what it writes.","It reads the setup, never a person."],"x":"One place to type in what your worksheets produced each week, and a report that reads them side by side to show where they agree and where they don’t. This report brings all Five Pillars together: the load (I), how the setup is holding (II), your load scores (III), how repair is going (IV), and the jobs that quietly slip back (V). In you: you see your own patterns over time. Between you and others: you get one shared picture of the setup, never a verdict on either person. Nothing is stored in the browser or sent anywhere. Press Save ledger to download a small file, and Load ledger next week to carry on. Dig deeper: how saving works After the weekly closing (your short end-of-week review), type the figures from the worksheets you ran: WP-01 minutes, WP-02 scores, WP-03 counts and so on. Leave blank what you didn’t run. It takes about a minute. Dig deeper: adding a week Enter the gaps you found, and how many were back after being fixed last month. That repeat count matters most. Dig deeper: adding a month The report says where things stand, how the numbers fit together, and what to do next. Every note follows a written rule you can check. Dig deeper: the report itself It never judges either of you or compares you. It is not a clinical tool. If a note is used to end a conversation, it’s being used the wrong way. It’s meant to start one. Dig deeper: how the report is written Try this: After your next weekly closing, enter that week and save your ledger file somewhere private. Open the ledger and add a week → Filled in a whole road at once? The Full path package turns one filled PDF into a full report, on your device. Goes with CALC-01 · the monthly look-back (WP-04) · Build your Workpaper Suite","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html","t":"WP-01 in depth — Who did what, and kind ways to say no","h":"Who did what, and kind ways to say no Also called Field Audit & Neutral Refusals Part A: the 7-day log · Part B: kind ways to say no","d":"WP-01 in full: the 7-day Field Audit and the expanded guide to kind ways to say no, in one document.","s":["The Field Audit","The 7-Day Log","The Neutral Refusals","When plans slip","The Capacity Check","The Delegation Pivot","The Time Commitment"],"x":"WP-01 The complete WP-01 module, in one document. This worksheet puts Pillar I, See the whole load to work, and its kind ways to say no use Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive . In you: notice everything you carry, including the unseen work. Between you and others: it all goes on one shared page, so nobody has to argue about whose work counts. Part A — Field Audit Part B — Neutral Refusals Fill it in on screen and save a PDF · Read the Preface · Build your Workpaper Suite Purpose: write down what actually happened this week, before either of you starts remembering it as a story. Fill it in together, or each on your own and then compare. Each of you writes only your own side, never the other person's. This worksheet has two parts. Part A is a plain log of what happened, written down before anyone decides what it means. Part B is a set of scripts for saying \"no\" or \"not yet\" to a task without starting a fight. The totals from Part A go into the Lemonade Stand calculator and into the Verdict Engine's workload_balance input (the Verdict Engine is the program's overall check, explained in Chapter II). Part A A 7-Day Log of What Actually Happened Day Task noticed / done Who did it Minutes (rough) Was it asked for, or just noticed and handled? Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri Sat Sun Audit rule: log what happened, not what should have happened. \"I noticed the trash three times before I took it out\" is one honest row, not three rows of resentment. Part B An Expanded Scenario Guide No list of scripts can cover every situation two people might face, and it would be a false promise to claim otherwise. Instead, here is a wide range of common, everyday situations in four groups. Find one close to yours and adapt the wording. The last section shows the pattern underneath, so you can build your own script for anything not covered here. All three refusal types follow the same three-part shape: Acknowledge the request is real and reasonable, State Capacity honestly, and Offer an Alternative so the other person isn't left with nothing. None of these is meant to end a conversation on your say-so alone. Each one is an opening line. The other person can still answer, push back, or suggest something different. For the everyday slips that happen in every home: a job you said you’d do, running late, a half-finished task, or a reminder that won’t stick. Kind and plain, with no labels on any","f":1},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-01.html","t":"Who Did What: A One-Week Chore Log and Kind Ways to Say No","h":"Who did what, and kind ways to say no","d":"A one-week log of who did what at home, plus three calm ways to say no or “not right now” without starting a fight. A free worksheet for couples and roommates.","s":["Write down the week before it turns into a story.","Each day, log the small jobs too.","Log what happened, not what should have.","Say no in three calm steps.","Pick the kind of “not now” you mean.","When plans slip, say so kindly."],"x":"A one-week log of who did what at home, plus simple scripts for saying “not right now” without starting an argument. This worksheet puts Pillar I, See the whole load to work, and its kind ways to say no use Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive . In you: notice everything you carry, including the unseen work. Between you and others: it all goes on one shared page, so nobody has to argue about whose work counts. Memories of a busy week blur fast. This sheet gently catches what actually happened. Fill it in together, or each on your own and then compare. Each of you writes only your own side. Dig deeper: what the log is for For every task, note the day, what it was, who did it, roughly how many minutes, and whether someone asked for it or it was just noticed and handled. Dig deeper: the 7-day log If you noticed the bin three times before taking it out, that is one honest row, not three rows of resentment. Dig deeper: the honest-row rule Say why the request is fair. Say honestly what you have left right now. Then offer something instead: another time, another person, or part of the job. That way the other person always has something to go on. Dig deeper: building your own script You are already full (a capacity check). Someone else is better placed (a delegation pivot). Or you will do it, just later (a time commitment). The sheet has ready-made lines for each. Dig deeper: the example scripts Said you’d do it and can’t tonight? Running behind, or with a job still half-finished? Acknowledge it, say plainly what you can do, and offer a when. Dig deeper: words for when plans slip Try this: Log one ordinary week, each of you on your own sheet. At the end, add up the minutes. Those totals go into the Lemonade Stand quick check and the CALC-01 calculator. Fill it in on screen and save a PDF → Then try the Lemonade Stand check → Goes with the Preface: The work nobody sees · CALC-01 · Build your Workpaper Suite","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-02-battery-stress-meter-in-depth.html","t":"WP-02 in depth — How much are you carrying?","h":"How much are you carrying? Also called The Battery & Stress Meter A Five-Question Check on Yourself","d":"WP-02 in full: a five-question check on yourself that separates the stress you're already carrying from how upset you are about one thing.","s":["The Checklist","Today’s Scores"],"x":"WP-02 Separating how much you're already carrying from how upset you are about this specific thing. This worksheet puts Pillar III, Read your state first to work. In you: you check how full your battery is before you judge a moment. Between you and others: you can pick your timing, and say \"I'm carrying a lot today\" before a hard talk. Fill it in on screen and save a PDF · Read Chapter III · Settle first with the Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) · Build your Workpaper Suite Purpose: a short checklist that each of you fills in about yourself. It separates \"How much stress am I already carrying?\" from \"How upset am I about this one thing?\" It is not a clinical test, just a structured gut-check. Fill it in on your own. Score each row from 0 (not at all) to 4 (very true), based on how the last 24–48 hours have actually gone. Factor 0 1 2 3 4 Sleep debt (less rested than usual) Workload elsewhere (job, school, caregiving) Recent unresolved conflict (any source) Physical state (hungry, sick, in pain) Time pressure today specifically 0 = not at all · 4 = very true Load score = the five scores added up, then divided by 20 (higher means a heavier load) Higher means heavier. Under 0.30 is a low load, 0.30 up to 0.60 medium, 0.60 and up high. 0.0–0.3 — Low load. Whatever is coming up is probably about the thing itself. 0.3–0.6 — Medium. Before a hard conversation, it's worth saying out loud: \"heads up, I'm carrying more than usual today.\" 0.6–1.0 — High. Put off anything that doesn't need deciding in the next hour. What this workpaper is not for: Don't fill this in about the other person. And don't use your own high score to end a conversation you don't want to have. It's a way to press pause, not a way out. A high score means \"let's come back to this tomorrow,\" not \"this doesn't need to happen.\" Score Date Person A Person B The Objective Ledger™ · Workpaper Suite · WP-02 · Free with email while in development · please don't redistribute","f":1},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-02-battery-stress-meter.html","t":"Stress Check: How Much Are You Carrying? (WP-02)","h":"How much are you carrying? A quick stress check","d":"A five-question stress check that separates how much you are already carrying from how upset you are about one thing. A reflection tool, not a test.","s":["Use it before a hard conversation.","Score five things, 0 to 4.","Add them up and divide by 20.","A high score means “later,” not “never.”"],"x":"A kind, quick check on yourself that separates how much you are already carrying from how upset you are about this one thing. The score runs from 0 to 1, and higher means heavier : under 0.30 is a low load, 0.30 up to 0.60 medium, 0.60 and up high. This worksheet puts Pillar III, Read your state first to work. In you: you check how full your battery is before you judge a moment. Between you and others: you can pick your timing, and say \"I'm carrying a lot today\" before a hard talk. Sometimes a small thing feels huge because the rest of the day was heavy. This helps you tell the two apart. It is a simple gut-check, not a medical test. Dig deeper: what it measures Each of you scores only yourself, on your own, thinking about the last day or two: sleep, workload elsewhere, any unresolved conflict, how your body feels, and time pressure today. 0 means not at all, 4 means very true. Dig deeper: the checklist Under 0.3: whatever comes up is probably about the thing itself. 0.3 to 0.6: say out loud “heads up, I’m carrying more than usual today.” Over 0.6: be gentle with yourself and put off anything that doesn’t need deciding in the next hour. Dig deeper: what each score means Fill it in only about yourself. Use your score to pace a talk, not to dodge one you’d rather avoid. A high score means “let’s come back to this tomorrow.” Dig deeper: what it is not for Try this: Next time something small stings more than it should, score yourself before you answer. Fill it in on screen and save a PDF → Goes with Chapter III: Full tanks and different angles · the Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) · Build your Workpaper Suite","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html","t":"WP-03 in depth: The RACI Treaty","h":"The RACI Treaty","d":"The full household chore agreement: give every regular job one owner, with an optional helper, so nothing slips and nobody has to keep asking who does what.","s":["The Treaty","Changing the treaty","Agreeing on it"],"x":"Fill it in on screen and save a PDF · Read Chapter IV · Build your Workpaper Suite This worksheet puts Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: a job that keeps slipping is a setup problem, not a character flaw. Between you and others: every job gets one clear owner, so unclaimed work stops drifting to one person. Purpose: a living agreement that gives every regular household job one owner (the Responsible name), with an optional helper (standing in for Accountable), so you stop re-deciding who owns what every week. (RACI is a planning term from the workplace. It stands for Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed. This treaty uses just the first two.) Owner (Responsible, R): does the job and sees it through. Every job needs one. Helper (optional, standing in for Accountable, A): pitches in, covers, or notices if it didn't get done and follows up. Task Frequency Owner Helper (optional) Notes Groceries Weekly Cooking Daily Dishes Daily Laundry Weekly Bills & scheduling Monthly Cleaning (bathroom/kitchen) Weekly Pet care Daily Car maintenance As needed Social/family calendar Ongoing Emotional check-ins Weekly (add rows as needed) This treaty isn't set in stone. It's meant to be reworked when life changes (a new job, a health issue, a new baby), not quietly shifted when one person just starts doing more. To change it: Either of you can ask to revisit it at the weekly closing, your short end-of-week review ( Chapter V ). Write each change into the table above. Don't just agree out loud. Note the date and who agreed to it. Agreeing means you’ve both read this version and know who owns what. It doesn’t mean every job feels perfectly fair, only that ownership is clear. ☐ We’re agreed on this. Look at this again on: ____________ Ownership Clarity Score (for the Verdict Engine, Chapter II ): count the jobs that have an owner (R) filled in, then divide by the total number of jobs listed. This is the ownership number in CALC-01.","f":1},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty.html","t":"One Owner per Chore: A Free Household Chore Agreement","h":"One owner per job: a household chore agreement","d":"Give every recurring household chore one clear owner from start to finish, so nothing slips, nobody nags and you stop re-deciding who does what each week.","s":["Every job gets one owner.","Go down the list together.","Change it on purpose, not by drift.","Agreeing means clear, not perfect.","Count how many jobs have an owner."],"x":"A shared list that gives every regular household job one clear owner, so you can stop re-deciding who does what every week. This worksheet puts Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: a job that keeps slipping is a setup problem, not a character flaw. Between you and others: every job gets one clear owner, so unclaimed work stops drifting to one person. The owner does the job and sees it through. If you like, add a helper who pitches in or notices if it slips. The helper is optional. Dig deeper: the owner and the helper Start with the common jobs on the sheet, like groceries, dishes, bills and the family calendar, and add your own rows. For each, write how often it happens and who owns it. Dig deeper: the agreement table When life changes, either of you can ask to revisit it at the weekly closing (your short end-of-week review). Write the change into the table, with the date, rather than one person quietly doing more. Dig deeper: how to update it Agreeing says you both know who owns what, as written. It doesn’t say every job feels perfectly fair. Dig deeper: agreeing together Count the jobs with an owner written in, then divide by the total number of jobs on the list. Starter examples you never filled in don’t count. That is your ownership clarity score, one of the numbers in Chapter II’s check. Dig deeper: the ownership clarity score Try this: Sit down on a calm day and fill in names for the first five jobs. Leave the harder ones for next time. Fill it in on screen and save a PDF → Goes with Chapter IV: Two kinds of fair · CALC-01 · Build your Workpaper Suite","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html","t":"WP-04 in depth: The Deficit Audit","h":"The Deficit Audit","d":"The full mental load audit: a monthly look-back over four weeks of chore logs and owners, to find the tasks that keep slipping and fix the setup together.","s":["Part A — Gather the month's notes","Part B — Sort each gap","Part C — Wrap up the audit","What this audit is not for"],"x":"Fill it in on screen and save a PDF · Read Chapter V · Track it in REPORT-01 · Build your Workpaper Suite This worksheet puts Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: you look for what in the setup lets things slip, instead of blaming yourself. Between you and others: repeat problems get fixed in the setup, and you catch jobs quietly drifting back to one person. Purpose: a monthly look-back, done together. You look across four weeks of WP-01 logs (who did what) ( WP-01 ) and your RACI Treaty ( WP-03 ) to find the tasks that keep slipping. (Here, a \"deficit\" simply means a job that needed doing and didn't get done.) The goal is not to tally what either of you \"owes.\" It's to find where the household's setup needs a real fix, instead of another one-off patch. How often: monthly. Not weekly, because this audit looks for patterns you can't see from one week to the next. From your four weekly WP-01 logs (who did what), list every task that was logged as noticed but not done, or done only after being raised more than once, in any week this month. Task Week 1 Week 2 Week 3 Week 4 Times flagged this month Put a check in a week's box if the task was noticed as a gap that week (not done, or raised more than once before it got done). A task flagged 3–4 times this month is a real pattern, not a fluke. For every task flagged twice or more, decide which of these it actually is: Task Has a named RACI owner? (WP-03) Classification Action Y / N Structural gap | Capacity issue | One-off, no action Y / N Structural gap | Capacity issue | One-off, no action Structural gap: there's no clear owner, or the named owner isn't the person who actually handles it. Fix: update the RACI Treaty (WP-03) in this session. Capacity issue: it has a named owner, but that person hasn't been able to keep up with it. Fix: an honest conversation, using a neutral refusal or a handover (WP-01, WP-09 ). Not a private promise to just try harder. One-off, no action: flagged more than once, but tied to a clear, temporary cause (illness, travel, a busy month at work). Note it and move on. Don't let a temporary cause push you into a permanent change. Update the RACI Treaty (WP-03) for every task sorted as a structural gap. For anything sorted as a capacity issue, set a time for that conversation. This audit brings it to light, but it do","f":1},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit.html","t":"Mental Load Audit: Find the Chores That Keep Slipping","h":"What keeps coming back? A mental load audit","d":"Once a month, look back at four weeks of chore logs together, find the jobs and arguments that keep coming back, and fix the setup instead of blaming a person.","s":["Do it monthly, not weekly.","List the jobs that slipped.","Sort each repeat into one of three kinds.","Wrap it up and keep count.","Nobody owes anything."],"x":"Once a month, together, you take a gentle look back at four weeks of logs to find the jobs that keep slipping, and fix the setup instead of patching it again. This worksheet puts Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: you look for what in the setup lets things slip, instead of blaming yourself. Between you and others: repeat problems get fixed in the setup, and you catch jobs quietly drifting back to one person. Some patterns only show up across a month. You need your four weekly WP-01 logs and your WP-03 job list on hand. Dig deeper: what the look-back is for Write down every job that was noticed but not done, or only done after being raised more than once, in any week. Check off each week it happened. Three or four checks is a real pattern, not a fluke. Dig deeper: gathering the month’s notes No clear owner, or the named owner isn’t the one doing it: update WP-03. The owner can’t keep up: have a kind, honest conversation. A temporary cause like illness or travel: note it and move on. Dig deeper: sorting each gap Make the changes, set a time for any conversation you need, and write down how many structural gaps (jobs with no clear owner) you found. Fewer each month means it is working. More just means something bigger changed, and it’s worth a talk. Dig deeper: wrapping up the look-back Past hours don’t get charged to anyone. A job with no owner is a gap in the list, not a judgment on whoever kept covering it. Dig deeper: what the look-back is not for Try this: Put a date in the calendar for the end of this month, and keep your weekly logs until then. Fill it in on screen and save a PDF → Goes with Chapter V: The monthly look-back · REPORT-01 · Build your Workpaper Suite","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-04.html","t":"WP-04 — The Deficit Audit","h":"The Deficit Audit A Monthly Look-Back at What Keeps Slipping","d":"WP-04 in full: the monthly look-back over four weeks of WP-01 logs (who did what), sorting the jobs that keep slipping.","s":["Gather the Month's Notes","Sort Each Gap","Wrap Up the Audit","First: did last month's fixes hold?","Then: wrap up the month","What This Audit Is Not For","Ownership Clarity"],"x":"This is the original, full-length page for WP-04. The newer, simpler page is The monthly look-back (WP-04) , and you can fill it in on screen or as part of your Workpaper Suite . WP-04 A monthly look back over four weeks of WP-01 logs, to find the tasks that keep slipping. This worksheet puts Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: you look for what in the setup lets things slip, instead of blaming yourself. Between you and others: repeat problems get fixed in the setup, and you catch jobs quietly drifting back to one person. Part A — Gather Part B — Sort Part C — Wrap Up Not For Purpose: a monthly look-back, done together. You look across four weeks of WP-01 logs (who did what) (WP-01) and your RACI Treaty (WP-03) to find the tasks that keep slipping. (Here, a \"deficit\" simply means a job that needed doing and didn't get done.) The goal is not to tally what either of you \"owes.\" It's to find where the household's setup needs a real fix, instead of another one-off patch. How often: monthly. Not weekly, because this audit looks for patterns you can't see from one week to the next. Part A Before you start, run WP-02. This is a look back, together, at four weeks of dropped balls. Of all the sessions in the suite, it is the one most likely to go badly. If either of you has a high load score, move the audit to another day. A month-old pattern will keep. List every task that was logged as noticed but not done, or done only after being raised more than once, in any week this month. Use whichever record you actually keep: Four weekly WP-01 logs (who did what) — if you log a full audit every week, this is your richest source. The WP-13 daily loop log — if you only run a full WP-01 log once a quarter, use the \"one small thing that didn't feel great\" line from your daily check-ins as the month's record. It runs every day, which is exactly what this audit needs. Either one works. You don't need to keep a WP-01 log going forever to run this. Logging forever turns a quick check into one more chore, and extra chores are where all of this started. Task Week 1 Week 2 Week 3 Week 4 Times flagged this month Put a check in a week's box if the task was noticed as a gap that week (not done, or raised more than once before it got done). Twice or more is the cutoff. Those are the tasks you sort in Part B. Three","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter-in-depth.html","t":"WP-09 in depth: Say it so it lands","h":"Say it so it lands (also called The Tone Filter)","d":"The full checklist for getting back in tune before you reply: put the radio-tuning idea from Chapter I into everyday words that are calm, clear and kind.","s":["The transducer step — before you speak or send","The filter step — before you react to what they said","Worked example","Everyday slips: forgetting, lateness, half-done jobs"],"x":"Fill it in on screen and save a PDF · Read Chapter I · Build your Workpaper Suite This worksheet puts Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive to work. In you: you notice your own reaction and how you tend to hear things. Between you and others: you shape the message so it lands, and treat a mismatch as tuning, not a moral failing. Purpose: a practical checklist for \"retuning\" before you respond. It puts the radio-frequency idea from Chapter I into everyday practice. Nothing is recorded, and nobody's actual voice is analyzed. Each of you uses it as a self-check on your own side of the conversation. A transducer is a device that turns one kind of signal into another. Here, it means turning your raw reaction into something the other person can actually take in. Before you respond to something that landed hard, run it through three questions: What's the fact underneath this? (One sentence, no adjectives.) What's the feeling underneath this? (Name it — frustrated, tired, unseen, rushed.) What's the actual ask? (What do you want to happen next, specifically?) If your sentence is a verdict on the person, stop. \"You always\" and \"you're careless\" are criticism, not an ask. They fill the room with tension and suggest a bad motive the other person then has to defend. Swap them for one fact and one ask, and say that you're not against them. The ground rules cover the rest of this. Write all three down before you respond, even if only in your head. A message built from fact, feeling and ask comes across far more clearly than one built from the raw reaction. You can paste the ask into the Signal Translator . Put the fact in its facts box, so a verdict doesn't get scored as the whole sentence. If you're about to say it out loud in a planned talk, Check-ins explains the order to follow: each of you acknowledges what the other said before adding your own side. The filter works in the other direction. It helps you decide how much weight to give a message you just received, before you respond to it. Check Yes / No Is this about a specific, nameable task or event? Would I read this the same way if my battery weren't already running high ( WP-02 )? Is there a neutral interpretation that also fits what was said? Am I responding to their words, or to a pattern from a past conversation? If your load is running high (WP-02), or a neutral reading still fits, use a neutral refusal or","f":1},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter.html","t":"Say It So It Lands: Kinder Words Before You Reply","h":"Say it so it lands","d":"A quick self-check before you answer something that landed hard, so what you say next is calm, clear and something the other person can actually hear.","s":["It is a check on your own next message.","Before you speak, find three things.","Before you react, weigh what you heard.","See it in action.","Everyday slips get kind words too."],"x":"A quick, kind self-check to run before you answer something that stung, so what you say is something the other person can actually hear. This worksheet puts Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive to work. In you: you notice your own reaction and how you tend to hear things. Between you and others: you shape the message so it lands, and treat a mismatch as tuning, not a moral failing. Nothing is recorded and nobody else’s voice is analyzed. Each of you uses it on yourself. Dig deeper: what it is for The fact, in one sentence with no adjectives. The feeling underneath, like tired or unseen. And the ask: what you want to happen next. If your sentence is a judgment on the person, like “you always,” pause and swap it for one fact and one ask. Dig deeper: sorting out what you want to say Is it about a specific task? Would it read the same if you weren’t already stretched? Could a neutral meaning fit? Are you answering their words, or an old argument? If you are stretched or unsure, pause first. Dig deeper: weighing what you heard “You never think about anyone but yourself” becomes: the bin wasn’t out before pickup, I feel a bit unseen, can we pick a fixed day for it? Dig deeper: the worked example A forgotten task, running late, a half-finished chore, or “I said I’d do it.” Keep to the fact, name the feeling, and ask for something small, like a reminder or a by-when. Dig deeper: words for everyday slips Try this: Next time you feel the urge to snap back, write the fact, the feeling and the ask first, even just in your head. Fill it in on screen and save a PDF → Then test your ask in the Signal Translator → Goes with Chapter I: Why we get out of tune · Build your Workpaper Suite","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-11.html","t":"WP-11 — The Calm-Down Kit","h":"The Calm-Down Kit A Toolkit for Calming Your Senses","d":"WP-11: an earlier version of the Calm-Down Kit, built around calming your senses.","s":["I. What This Kit Is For","II. A Note on the Science — Read This First","III. Why Try Steady Sensory Input at All?","IV. Worksheet — Build Your Own Calming Toolkit","V. Using WP-11 with the Rest of the Program"],"x":"This is the original, full-length page for WP-11. The newer, simpler page is The Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) , and you can fill it in on screen or as part of your Workpaper Suite . WP-11 Part of The Objective Ledger™ Workpaper Suite. This is an earlier version of the kit, built around the senses. The current Calm-Down Kit, with ready-made defaults, a stepping-away line and a plan for coming back, is here , and the sounds are in the Sound Toolkit . This kit puts Pillar III, Read your state first to work. In you: you learn what settles you, and decide it on a calm day. Between you and others: you pause with a clear line and a time to come back, so a break is never mistaken for walking out. To help you interrupt a sudden spike of stress with something calming for your senses, before you go back to a hard conversation. WP-11 offers a menu of options, such as sound, touch and lighting, that different people find calming for different reasons. That way you can build your own toolkit instead of following one fixed routine. This is a rule-of-thumb toolkit, not a tested clinical treatment. It works the same way the rest of this program does: it offers structure and options, not a guaranteed result. Some of the tools below (steady background sound, physical bass, dimmer lighting) are common in everyday relaxation practices, and plenty of people find them calming. It's worth being honest about what is and isn't known: Predictable, steady sound masking irregular noise (a flickering light's hum, distant traffic) is a widely used, low-risk calming technique. Many people find a steady sound easier to tune out than an unpredictable one. Physical bass or vibration is commonly used in sensory tools (weighted blankets, drum circles, bass-heavy music), and many people describe it as physically grounding. That's a real, common experience. But it isn't proof of how it works, and we're not claiming it is. 40 Hz / gamma-frequency audio is an area of early, narrow scientific research, mostly studied for other purposes. It is not an established tool for calming an argument or settling the nervous system. We include it only as one optional sound some people enjoy, not because anyone has shown it works for this. None of this is a substitute for professional mental health care, and none of it is a diagnostic or clinical claim. Treat everything below as \"worth trying to see if it works for you","f":0},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html","t":"WP-13 in depth: The Phase-Locked Loop","h":"The Phase-Locked Loop","d":"The full 90-second daily check-in for couples and housemates: a short, easy daily talk that keeps two people in step with small, steady corrections.","s":["Why small and frequent beats big and rare","The daily loop (about 90 seconds)","The weekly catch-up","Failure mode to watch for"],"x":"Fill it in on screen and save a PDF · Read Chapter I · Build your Workpaper Suite This check-in puts Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: you take a quick reading of your own day. Between you and others: small daily corrections keep you in tune, and a daily thank-you keeps things steady. Purpose: a short, easy check-in you do every day. It is named after a phase-locked loop, an electronic circuit that keeps two signals in step by making constant small corrections instead of occasional big ones. Here it becomes a daily habit that keeps the \"frequency drift\" described in Chapter I from building up into a squeal. A phase-locked loop doesn't wait for two signals to drift far apart and then yank them back into step. It makes tiny corrections all the time, so the drift never builds up. At home, it works the same way: a 90-second daily check-in heads off most of what would otherwise become a 45-minute weekend argument. Pick the same moment each day: after dinner, before bed, whatever is realistic. Each of you answers these out loud, in one sentence each: Load: \"Today I was at about [low / medium / high] capacity.\" Appreciation: \"One thing I appreciated about you (or us) today was ___.\" Specific beats general: name the thing and the moment. Research on everyday connection suggests these small moments of noticing matter as much as fixing what went wrong. See Turning Toward . Friction: \"One small thing that didn't feel great today was ___.\" (Optional — skip if there's nothing.) Ask: \"One thing that would help tomorrow is ___.\" (Optional.) No debating, no solving, no arguing back. One of you talks, and the other just listens. Anything that needs a real discussion gets saved for the weekly closing, your short end-of-week review ( Chapter V ). Don't argue it out in the 90 seconds. Once a week, when you do your weekly closing: Skim the week's daily loop notes for anything that repeated more than twice. A sore spot that keeps coming up moves to the RACI Treaty ( WP-03 ) or the Tone Filter ( WP-09 ), instead of staying a low-level irritation. If the daily check-in starts being used to deliver criticism (\"well, since we're doing check-ins...\") instead of a plain update, you'll both start avoiding it. And avoidance causes exactly the drift this check-in is meant to prevent. Keep the daily version to pl","f":1},{"u":"/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol.html","t":"90-Second Daily Check-In for Couples and Housemates","h":"The 90-second daily check-in","d":"A 90-second daily check-in: one sentence each about how you are doing and what is coming up, so small things do not pile up into a weekend argument. Free.","s":["Small and often beats big and rare.","Once a day, one sentence each.","Just listen. No need to debate.","Once a week, look for repeats.","Keep it a quick update, not a complaint."],"x":"A 90-second daily check-in that makes small corrections every day, so little things don’t pile up into a big weekend argument. This check-in puts Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: you take a quick reading of your own day. Between you and others: small daily corrections keep you in tune, and a daily thank-you keeps things steady. A phase-locked loop is a circuit that keeps two signals in step. This check-in does the same for two people, with tiny daily nudges instead of one big jolt. Dig deeper: why it works Pick a regular moment, like after dinner. Each of you says how full you were today (low, medium or high), one thing you appreciated about the other, one small thing that didn’t feel great, and one thing that would help tomorrow. The last two are optional. Dig deeper: the daily loop One speaks, the other takes it in. Nothing gets solved here. If it needs a real talk, save it for the weekly closing (your short end-of-week review). Dig deeper: just listening, no debate Skim the week’s notes. Anything that came up more than twice moves to your job list (WP-03) or your tone check (WP-09) instead of just hanging around. Dig deeper: the weekly catch-up If the check-in turns into criticism, it stops feeling safe and you’ll both start to skip it. Keep the daily one to plain, kind facts. Dig deeper: what to watch for Try this: Choose your moment tonight and try it. Start with the appreciation, because it sets the tone for the rest. The whole thing takes about 90 seconds. Fill it in on screen and save a PDF → Goes with Chapter I: Why we get out of tune · Check-ins · Build your Workpaper Suite","f":0},{"u":"/wp-01.html","t":"Fair Chore Chart: Who Did What (WP-01)","h":"Who did what, and kind ways to say no Also called Field Audit & Neutral Refusals Part A: the 7-day log · Part B: kind ways to say no","d":"A free chore chart worksheet: note who did what this week, see the split clearly, and find kind ways to say no. For couples, families and roommates.","s":["The Field Audit","The 7-Day Log","The Neutral Refusals","When plans slip","The Capacity Check","The Delegation Pivot","The Time Commitment"],"x":"This is the original, full-length page for WP-01. The newer, simpler page is Who did what, and kind ways to say no (WP-01) , and you can fill it in on screen or as part of your Workpaper Suite . WP-01 The complete WP-01 module, in one document. This worksheet puts Pillar I, See the whole load to work, and its kind ways to say no use Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive . In you: notice everything you carry, including the unseen work. Between you and others: it all goes on one shared page, so nobody has to argue about whose work counts. Part A — Field Audit Part B — Neutral Refusals Purpose: write down what actually happened this week, before either of you starts remembering it as a story. Fill it in together, or each on your own and then compare. Each of you writes only your own side, never the other person's. This worksheet has two parts. Part A is a plain log of what happened, written down before anyone decides what it means. Part B is a set of scripts for saying \"no\" or \"not yet\" to a task without starting a fight. The totals from Part A go into the Lemonade Stand calculator and into the Verdict Engine's workload_balance input (the Verdict Engine is the program's overall check, explained in Chapter II). Part A A 7-Day Log of What Actually Happened Day Task noticed / done Who did it Minutes (rough) Was it asked for, or just noticed and handled? Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri Sat Sun Audit rule: log what happened, not what should have happened. \"I noticed the trash three times before I took it out\" is one honest row, not three rows of resentment. Part B An Expanded Scenario Guide No list of scripts can cover every situation two people might face, and it would be a false promise to claim otherwise. Instead, here is a wide range of common, everyday situations in four groups. Find one close to yours and adapt the wording. The last section shows the pattern underneath, so you can build your own script for anything not covered here. All three refusal types follow the same three-part shape: Acknowledge the request is real and reasonable, State Capacity honestly, and Offer an Alternative so the other person isn't left with nothing. None of these is meant to end a conversation on your say-so alone. Each one is an opening line. The other person can still answer, push back, or suggest something different. For the everyday slips that happen in every home: a job you said you’","f":0},{"u":"/wp-02.html","t":"How Much Are You Carrying? A Stress Self-Check (WP-02)","h":"How much are you carrying? Also called The Battery & Stress Meter A Five-Question Check on Yourself","d":"A quick self-check of how full your plate feels today, to help you pace a hard talk. A reflection tool, not a test or diagnosis.","s":["The Checklist","Today’s Scores"],"x":"This is the original, full-length page for WP-02. The newer, simpler page is How much are you carrying? (WP-02) , and you can fill it in on screen or as part of your Workpaper Suite . WP-02 Separating how much you're already carrying from how upset you are about this specific thing. This worksheet puts Pillar III, Read your state first to work. In you: you check how full your battery is before you judge a moment. Between you and others: you can pick your timing, and say \"I'm carrying a lot today\" before a hard talk. Purpose: a short checklist that each of you fills in about yourself. It separates \"How much stress am I already carrying?\" from \"How upset am I about this one thing?\" It is not a clinical test, just a structured gut-check. Fill it in on your own. Score each row from 0 (not at all) to 4 (very true), based on how the last 24–48 hours have actually gone. Factor 0 1 2 3 4 Sleep debt (less rested than usual) Workload elsewhere (job, school, caregiving) Recent unresolved conflict (any source) Physical state (hungry, sick, in pain) Time pressure today specifically 0 = not at all · 4 = very true Load score = the five scores added up, then divided by 20 (higher means a heavier load) Higher means heavier. Under 0.30 is a low load, 0.30 up to 0.60 medium, 0.60 and up high. 0.0–0.3 — Low load. Whatever is coming up is probably about the thing itself. 0.3–0.6 — Medium. Before a hard conversation, it's worth saying out loud: \"heads up, I'm carrying more than usual today.\" 0.6–1.0 — High. Put off anything that doesn't need deciding in the next hour. What this workpaper is not for: Don't fill this in about the other person. And don't use your own high score to end a conversation you don't want to have. It's a way to press pause, not a way out. A high score means \"let's come back to this tomorrow,\" not \"this doesn't need to happen.\" Score Date Partner A Partner B The Objective Ledger™ · Workpaper Suite · WP-02 · Free with email while in development · please don't redistribute","f":0},{"u":"/wp-03.html","t":"Who Owns Which Chore? One Owner per Job (WP-03)","h":"One owner per job Also called the RACI Treaty Ownership · Changes · Agreeing on It","d":"Give every recurring job one owner from start to finish, so nothing slips and nobody nags. A free household planning worksheet.","s":["The Treaty","Changing the Treaty","Agreeing on It","Does every job have an owner?"],"x":"This is the original, full-length page for WP-03. The newer, simpler page is One owner per job (WP-03) , and you can fill it in on screen or as part of your Workpaper Suite . WP-03 A living agreement that gives every regular household job one owner, and, if you like, a helper. This worksheet puts Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: a job that keeps slipping is a setup problem, not a character flaw. Between you and others: every job gets one clear owner, so unclaimed work stops drifting to one person. The Treaty Changing the Treaty Agreeing on It Does every job have an owner? Purpose: a living agreement that gives every regular household job one owner, so you stop re-deciding who owns what every week. (The technical name comes from RACI, a planning term from the workplace: Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed. Here the owner is the Responsible name, and the optional helper stands in for Accountable.) Owner: the one person who does the job and sees it through. Helper (optional): someone who pitches in, covers, or notices if it slips. Part A Before you start, run WP-02. This is the longest session in the suite: 45 to 60 minutes of working out who owns what. Don't try it at the end of a hard day. If either of you has a high load score, pick another time. The list below is a starting point, not the whole list. Add every regular task your household actually has before you assign anything. The score at the bottom is only as honest as the list behind it, and the tasks most worth writing down are the ones nobody has ever said out loud. Job How often Owner Helper (optional) Notes Visible work Groceries Weekly Cooking Daily Dishes Daily Laundry Weekly Bills & scheduling Monthly Cleaning (bathroom/kitchen) Weekly Pet care Daily Car maintenance As needed The invisible column Noticing what's running low Ongoing Remembering appointments weeks out Ongoing Social/family calendar Ongoing Gifts, cards, birthdays As needed Tracking what the kids/pets need next Ongoing Sensing tension before it builds Ongoing Emotional check-ins Weekly add rows as needed Why the second block is there. The Preface opens on someone noticing the milk is low, and someone remembering a dentist appointment three weeks out. That kind of work is the whole reason this program exists. It is also the easiest to leave of","f":0},{"u":"/wp-04.html","t":"Mental Load Audit: What Keeps Coming Back? (WP-04)","h":"The Deficit Audit A Monthly Look-Back at What Keeps Slipping","d":"Find the jobs and arguments that keep coming back, see the invisible work behind them, and fix the setup instead of blaming a person.","s":["Gather the Month's Notes","Sort Each Gap","Wrap Up the Audit","First: did last month's fixes hold?","Then: wrap up the month","What This Audit Is Not For","Ownership Clarity"],"x":"This is the original, full-length page for WP-04. The newer, simpler page is The monthly look-back (WP-04) , and you can fill it in on screen or as part of your Workpaper Suite . WP-04 A monthly look back over four weeks of WP-01 logs, to find the tasks that keep slipping. This worksheet puts Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: you look for what in the setup lets things slip, instead of blaming yourself. Between you and others: repeat problems get fixed in the setup, and you catch jobs quietly drifting back to one person. Part A — Gather Part B — Sort Part C — Wrap Up Not For Purpose: a monthly look-back, done together. You look across four weeks of WP-01 logs (who did what) (WP-01) and your RACI Treaty (WP-03) to find the tasks that keep slipping. (Here, a \"deficit\" simply means a job that needed doing and didn't get done.) The goal is not to tally what either of you \"owes.\" It's to find where the household's setup needs a real fix, instead of another one-off patch. How often: monthly. Not weekly, because this audit looks for patterns you can't see from one week to the next. The job is to tell a gap in the setup apart from a bad month, so that repeat problems get fixed where they really start, without blaming anyone. Part A Before you start, run WP-02. This is a look back, together, at four weeks of dropped balls. Of all the sessions in the suite, it is the one most likely to go badly. If either of you has a high load score, move the audit to another day. A month-old pattern will keep. List every task that was logged as noticed but not done, or done only after being raised more than once, in any week this month. Use whichever record you actually keep: Four weekly WP-01 logs (who did what) — if you log a full audit every week, this is your richest source. The WP-13 daily loop log — if you only run a full WP-01 log once a quarter, use the \"one small thing that didn't feel great\" line from your daily check-ins as the month's record. It runs every day, which is exactly what this audit needs. Either one works. You don't need to keep a WP-01 log going forever to run this. Logging forever turns a quick check into one more chore, and extra chores are where all of this started. Task Week 1 Week 2 Week 3 Week 4 Times flagged this month Put a check in a week's box if the task was noticed as a ","f":0},{"u":"/wp-09.html","t":"Say It So It Lands: Kinder Wording (WP-09)","h":"Say it so it lands Also called The Tone Filter What You Send: Transducer · What You Hear: Filter","d":"Turn a hard sentence into one that lands: what you mean, what they may hear, and a calmer way to say it. A free communication worksheet.","s":["The Transducer Step","Working space","The Filter Step","Worked Example","Everyday slips: forgetting, lateness, half-done jobs","The Week's Tally","Failure Modes to Watch For"],"x":"This is the original, full-length page for WP-09. The newer, simpler page is Say it so it lands (WP-09) , and you can fill it in on screen or as part of your Workpaper Suite . WP-09 A practical checklist for \"retuning\" before you respond. It puts the radio-frequency idea from Chapter I into everyday practice. This worksheet puts Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive to work. In you: you notice your own reaction and how you tend to hear things. Between you and others: you shape the message so it lands, and treat a mismatch as tuning, not a moral failing. The Transducer The Filter Worked Examples Weekly Tally Purpose: a practical checklist for \"retuning\" before you respond. Nothing is recorded, and nobody's actual voice is analyzed. Each of you uses it as a self-check on your own side of the conversation. It works in two directions. The transducer is for what you send: it reshapes what you're about to say. The filter is for what you hear: it helps you decide how much weight to give what just came in. Neither one is for grading how the other person said something. What You Send Before you speak or send A transducer is a device that turns one kind of signal into another. Here, it means turning your raw reaction into something the other person can actually take in. Before you respond to something that landed hard, run it through three questions: What's the fact underneath this? (One sentence, no adjectives.) What's the feeling underneath this? (Name it — frustrated, tired, unseen, rushed.) What's the actual ask? (What do you want to happen next, specifically?) Write all three down before you respond, even if only in your head. A message built from fact → feeling → ask comes across far more clearly than one built from the raw reaction. Raw reaction Fact Feeling Ask What You Hear Before you react to what they said The filter works in the other direction. It helps you decide how much weight to give a message you just received, before you respond to it. Check Yes / No Is this about a specific, nameable task or event? Would I read this the same way if I weren't already carrying a lot (WP-02)? Is there a neutral interpretation that also fits what was said? Am I responding to their words, or to a pattern from a past conversation? Reading the four answers. Each answer points you to a next step: Not about a nameable task or event → there's nothing to solve yet. Ask wha","f":0},{"u":"/wp-11-in-depth.html","t":"WP-11 in depth — The Calm-Down Kit","h":"The Calm-Down Kit Plan Ahead · In the Moment · Coming Back","d":"WP-11 in full: a plan made ahead of time for calming down enough to pick a conversation back up, or to put it off honestly.","s":["Before You Need It","What tends to start it","Your two defaults","Your signal line","In the Moment","1 · Read where you are","2 · Say the line","3 · Run your default","Coming Back","Coming back","What This Kit Is Not For","Where It Connects"],"x":"WP-11 A short plan, made ahead of time, for calming your body down enough to have the conversation, or to put it off honestly. This kit puts Pillar III, Read your state first to work. In you: you learn what settles you, and decide it on a calm day. Between you and others: you pause with a clear line and a time to come back, so a break is never mistaken for walking out. Part A — Defaults Part B — In the Moment Part C — Coming Back Not For Fill it in on screen and save a PDF · Read Check-ins · Sound Toolkit · Build your Workpaper Suite Purpose: to calm a heated body and mind enough that WP-03, WP-09 or an ordinary conversation is possible again. One person uses this worksheet, about themselves. It is never aimed at the other person. Once you've settled, Check-ins explains the order for the conversation. Don't use your new calm to deliver the criticism you paused. Stick to one topic, no blame, and use the return line . Test what you'll say in the Signal Translator before you pick things back up. And don't go back just because the time you named has come, if your WP-02 score hasn't come down. Part A is filled in while calm. That's the whole idea. A kit that asks you to weigh your options at the very moment you're least able to think clearly isn't a kit. It's just one more task. Decide now, so that later you only have to do what you already chose. Part A Fill this in on an ordinary day, not a hard one. Name the two or three that actually keep coming up for you. Be specific rather than broad: \"the second time something gets asked\" is more useful than \"stress.\" Trigger How it shows up in the body first The body's first signal is the useful one: a tight jaw or shoulders, shallow breathing, sharper hearing, cold hands. It shows up before the thought does, which is what makes it worth knowing. Pick two from Part B and write them here. In the moment, you don't choose. You just do these. First default Second, if the first isn't available Leaving the room in the middle of a disagreement without saying why looks like walking out, however you mean it. Agree on one sentence now, so using the kit isn't mistaken for shutting the other person out. The shape of it Say how you are, how long you need, and when you'll be back. \"I'm at capacity. I need ten minutes. I'll be back at quarter past and we can pick this up.\" The return time is the part that matters. Without it, the sente","f":1},{"u":"/wp-11-sound-toolkit.html","t":"Calm-Down Sounds: Brown Noise, a Low Hum and More","h":"The Sound Toolkit","d":"Three simple background sounds to try as part of your calm-down plan: a low hum, a soft flutter and brown noise. Free in your browser, and a matter of taste.","s":[],"x":"A companion to the Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) Three simple background sounds: a low hum, a soft flutter and brown noise. Some people like one of them in the background while they settle; others don't, and that's useful to know too. This is a matter of taste, not a treatment. Nothing here is shown to relieve stress or change how your body works. If a sound does nothing for you, the other settling steps in the Calm-Down Kit will serve you better. This kit puts Pillar III, Read your state first to work. In you: you learn which surroundings help you settle. Between you and others: you can say \"I'm taking ten minutes with some quiet sound, then I'll come back,\" and keep the promise. See the Five Pillars . Try one at a time, at a low volume, and notice how it actually feels to you. Low hum A steady, very low tone (40 vibrations a second, written 40 Hz). Phone and laptop speakers often can't play a tone this low, so you may need headphones to hear it. Soft flutter A mid tone that switches on and off 40 times a second, so it sounds like a fast, even flutter. Some people enjoy it; no particular effect has been shown. Brown noise A smooth, low hiss, a bit like distant surf. It can cover uneven background sounds. Volume Start low, especially with headphones: low tones can feel stronger than you expect. Sounds stop when you leave or hide this page. ← Back to the Calm-Down Kit (WP-11) · Fill in your kit · Soundscapes","f":0},{"u":"/wp-11.html","t":"Calm-Down Kit: A Plan for Heated Moments (WP-11)","h":"The Calm-Down Kit: a plan for heated moments","d":"Make a simple calm-down plan before you need it: your early signs, a pause signal, what helps you settle, and how to come back to the talk kindly and on time.","s":["It is for you, never for them.","Decide now, while you’re calm.","In the moment, just follow the plan.","Let your load score guide when you go back.","It is not a treatment, and not a way out of the talk."],"x":"A plan you make while calm, for bringing your body back down in a heated moment, so you can finish the conversation or honestly put it off. This kit puts Pillar III, Read your state first to work. In you: you learn what settles you, and decide it on a calm day. Between you and others: you pause with a clear line and a time to come back, so a break is never mistaken for walking out. One person uses it about themselves. It is never something to hand to the other person. Dig deeper: what the kit is for On an ordinary day, write down what tends to set you off and where you feel it first. Pick two calming steps. Agree on one sentence to say when you step away: how you feel, how long, when you’ll be back. Dig deeper: making your plan ahead Check your load score (WP-02), say your line, then do your first calming step, like breathing in for four and out for six, or a slow walk. Dig deeper: the calming steps Check your load score again. Under 0.50, go back at the time you said. From 0.50 to 0.59, do another round. Still 0.60 or more after two rounds? That’s okay: pick a new time and say when. When you go back, do one small, simple task first, like putting the dishes away. Dig deeper: coming back These are everyday settling steps for the ordinary hard moments of a shared life. And your line always includes a time you’ll return. Dig deeper: what the kit is not for Try this: This week, on a calm evening, choose your two calming steps and write your stepping-away sentence. Fill it in on screen and save a PDF → Learn the WP-02 score the kit uses → Goes with Check-ins · the Sound Toolkit · Build your Workpaper Suite","f":0},{"u":"/wp-13.html","t":"90-Second Daily Check-In for Couples (WP-13)","h":"The 90-second daily check-in Also called the Phase-Locked Loop Daily Check-In · 90 Seconds · Weekly Catch-Up","d":"A 90-second daily check-in for couples and housemates: how you’re doing, what’s coming up, and one small way to help.","s":["Why Small and Frequent Beats Big and Rare","The Daily Loop","The Week's Log","The Weekly Catch-Up","Failure Mode to Watch For"],"x":"This is the original, full-length page for WP-13. The newer, simpler page is The 90-second check-in (WP-13) , and you can fill it in on screen or as part of your Workpaper Suite . WP-13 A short, easy daily check-in that keeps two people in step with small, steady corrections instead of occasional big ones. This check-in puts Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive to work, with a nod to Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives . In you: you take a quick reading of your own day. Between you and others: small daily corrections keep you in tune, and a daily thank-you keeps things steady. Why Small & Frequent The Daily Loop The Week's Log Weekly Catch-Up Purpose: a short, easy check-in you do every day. It is named after a phase-locked loop, an electronic circuit that keeps two signals in step by making constant small corrections instead of occasional big ones. Here it becomes a daily habit that keeps the \"frequency drift\" described in Chapter I from building up into a squeal. Premise A phase-locked loop doesn't wait for two signals to drift far apart and then yank them back into step. It makes tiny corrections all the time, so the drift never builds up. At home, it works the same way: a 90-second daily check-in heads off most of what would otherwise become a 45-minute weekend argument. Part A About 90 seconds Pick the same moment each day: after dinner, before bed, whatever is realistic. Each of you answers these out loud, in one sentence each: Load: \"Today I was at about [low / medium / high] capacity.\" Appreciation: \"One thing I appreciated about you (or us) today was ___.\" Specific beats general: name the thing and the moment. Friction: \"One small thing that didn't feel great today was ___.\" (Optional — skip if there's nothing.) Ask: \"One thing that would help tomorrow is ___.\" (Optional.) No debating, no solving, no arguing back. One of you talks, and the other just listens. Anything that needs a real discussion gets saved for the weekly closing, your short end-of-week review (Chapter V). Don't argue it out in the 90 seconds. Part B This is optional, and only as much as fits on one line. The weekly catch-up below works from whatever you write here, so the goal is a short, readable note, not a word-for-word record. Day Load (A / B) Friction Ask Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri Sat Sun Part C Once a week, when you do your weekly closing: Skim the week's daily loop notes for","f":0}]};
